scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:25:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1959-21-1,commonlii-LKCA-1959-21,79 NLR 1,LKCA,1959,A defendant can be held liable for losses caused to a plaintiff.,"(1) That the first defendant was liable for the loss caused to the plaintiff. (2) (Vythialingam, J. dissenting) That though the first defendant's servants issued the delivery orders without production of the Bills of Lading to persons who, to their knowledge, were other than those entitled under the Bills of Lading to receive them, the 2nd defendant is not liable for the non-delivery of the goods as he was protected by the exemption clause in the Bills of Lading. The plaintiff has failed to show any fundamental breach of his contract of affreightment committed-by the 2nd defendant to disentitle him of the benefit of the exemption clause. (3) That a party to a contract may be precluded from relying upon an exemption clause where such parties have been guilty of a fundamental breach of contract or of a breach of a funda'mental term. A fundamental breach disentitling the party from relying on an exemption clause occurs if an act amount'ing to a complete departure from the contract is done which can be imputed to the contracting party himself as opposed to an act for which the contracting party is vicariously res'ponsible as having been committed by one of his servants",SRC030-s772,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:26:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1959-21-2,commonlii-LKCA-1959-21,79 NLR 1,LKCA,1959,A party cannot claim damages for non-delivery of goods if the delivery orders were issued to unauthorized persons.,"(1) That the first defendant was liable for the loss caused to the plaintiff. (2) (Vythialingam, J. dissenting) That though the first defendant's servants issued the delivery orders without production of the Bills of Lading to persons who, to their knowledge, were other than those entitled under the Bills of Lading to receive them, the 2nd defendant is not liable for the non-delivery of the goods as he was protected by the exemption clause in the Bills of Lading. The plaintiff has failed to show any fundamental breach of his contract of affreightment committed-by the 2nd defendant to disentitle him of the benefit of the exemption clause. (3) That a party to a contract may be precluded from relying upon an exemption clause where such parties have been guilty of a fundamental breach of contract or of a breach of a funda'mental term. A fundamental breach disentitling the party from relying on an exemption clause occurs if an act amount'ing to a complete departure from the contract is done which can be imputed to the contracting party himself as opposed to an act for which the contracting party is vicariously res'ponsible as having been committed by one of his servants",SRC030-s772,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:27:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1959-21-3,commonlii-LKCA-1959-21,79 NLR 1,LKCA,1959,A party to a contract may be precluded from relying on an exemption clause if they have committed a fundamental breach of contract.,"(1) That the first defendant was liable for the loss caused to the plaintiff. (2) (Vythialingam, J. dissenting) That though the first defendant's servants issued the delivery orders without production of the Bills of Lading to persons who, to their knowledge, were other than those entitled under the Bills of Lading to receive them, the 2nd defendant is not liable for the non-delivery of the goods as he was protected by the exemption clause in the Bills of Lading. The plaintiff has failed to show any fundamental breach of his contract of affreightment committed-by the 2nd defendant to disentitle him of the benefit of the exemption clause. (3) That a party to a contract may be precluded from relying upon an exemption clause where such parties have been guilty of a fundamental breach of contract or of a breach of a funda'mental term. A fundamental breach disentitling the party from relying on an exemption clause occurs if an act amount'ing to a complete departure from the contract is done which can be imputed to the contracting party himself as opposed to an act for which the contracting party is vicariously res'ponsible as having been committed by one of his servants",SRC030-s772,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:72:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1977-4-1,commonlii-LKCA-1977-4,80 NLR 256,LKCA,1977,A misdescription of land boundaries in a deed does not invalidate the deed.,"That as the evidence and the findings of the Trial Judge show that the assessment numbers given in the respective deeds are the correct assessment numbers of the allotment of land each party intended to buy and in fact purchased and was in possession of, the description of the land by the boundaries was a misdescription which although it would not invalidate the deeds, could be rejected or ignored. The maxim falsa demonstrate non nocet which is embodied in section 95 of the Evidence Ordinance applied. Per Pathirana, J. ""The Assessment numbers provide a definite and sufficient description of the land in the respective deeds of the plaintiff and the defendant of what was intendend to pass and the descriptions in those deeds by boundaries are erroneous"". A PPEAL from the judgment of the District Court of Kandy. H. W. Jayewardene with B, J. Fernando and Miss S. Senaratne for Defendant-Appellant. T. B. Dissanayake for Plaintiff-Respondent",SRC029-s95,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:100:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1984-24-1,commonlii-LKCA-1984-24,(1985) 2 Sri LR 35,LKCA,1984,A party to a suit cannot set up the title of a third party who is not their predecessor in title and who has not been joined in the action.,A party to a suit cannot under s. 3 of the Prescription Ordinance set up the title of a third party who is not his predecessor in title and who has not been joined in the action. The judgment in a case must be declaratory of the right of a party to the suit not of a stranger,SRC071-s3,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:114:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1985-22-1,commonlii-LKCA-1985-22,(1985) 2 Sri LR 1,LKCA,1985,A tenant is entitled to obtain a tenancy in their own name if the original tenant has abandoned the premises.,"(1) Mrs. Sinniahpillai had abandoned the premises and in the changed circumstances Nagaratnam was entitled to obtain the tenancy for himself. (2) A co-owner has the right to lease out his share and this gives the lessee the right to possess and his possession is therefore a bona fide possession. (3) The question of estoppel by acquiescence was, not raised in the District Court proceedings. and cannot be raised now. (4) Under s. 328 of the Civil Procedure Code all that had to be established was that the possession of the disputant was bona fide on his own account or on account of some person other than the judgment-debtor and that he was not a party to the action in which the decree was passed. (N.B. Section 328 referred to in this decision was as it stood before the passage of Law No. 20 of 1977.) 2",SRC030-s328,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:115:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1985-22-2,commonlii-LKCA-1985-22,(1985) 2 Sri LR 1,LKCA,1985,A co-owner has the right to lease out his share.,"(1) Mrs. Sinniahpillai had abandoned the premises and in the changed circumstances Nagaratnam was entitled to obtain the tenancy for himself. (2) A co-owner has the right to lease out his share and this gives the lessee the right to possess and his possession is therefore a bona fide possession. (3) The question of estoppel by acquiescence was, not raised in the District Court proceedings. and cannot be raised now. (4) Under s. 328 of the Civil Procedure Code all that had to be established was that the possession of the disputant was bona fide on his own account or on account of some person other than the judgment-debtor and that he was not a party to the action in which the decree was passed. (N.B. Section 328 referred to in this decision was as it stood before the passage of Law No. 20 of 1977.) 2",SRC030-s328,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:116:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1985-22-3,commonlii-LKCA-1985-22,(1985) 2 Sri LR 1,LKCA,1985,Estoppel by acquiescence cannot be raised in a case if it was not raised in the initial proceedings.,"(1) Mrs. Sinniahpillai had abandoned the premises and in the changed circumstances Nagaratnam was entitled to obtain the tenancy for himself. (2) A co-owner has the right to lease out his share and this gives the lessee the right to possess and his possession is therefore a bona fide possession. (3) The question of estoppel by acquiescence was, not raised in the District Court proceedings. and cannot be raised now. (4) Under s. 328 of the Civil Procedure Code all that had to be established was that the possession of the disputant was bona fide on his own account or on account of some person other than the judgment-debtor and that he was not a party to the action in which the decree was passed. (N.B. Section 328 referred to in this decision was as it stood before the passage of Law No. 20 of 1977.) 2",SRC030-s328,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:135:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1986-22-1,commonlii-LKCA-1986-22,(1996) 1 Sri LR 346,LKCA,1986,The Court or the O.I.C. Ragama could have found out who the registered owner of the vehicle was.,"(1) The number plates of the lorry were available and the Court or the O.I.C. Ragama could have found out who the registered owner of the vehicle was. (2) The conduct of the O.I.C. Ragama was fraudulent in that he deliberately misled the Magistrate by filing the 'A' report which led the Magistrate to include the lorry in the public auction. (3) The indecent haste in including the vehicle for sale and the sale for a paltry sum of Rs. 31,000/‐ disclose a covert manoeuvre by a judicial officer with deliberate assistance by a corrupt Police Officer‐ in‐ Charge of a Police Station. (4) The Magistrate acted in contravention of the provisions of section 431(2) of the Code of Criminal Procedure and his action is tainted and does not appear to be a bona fide mistake when one considers his subsequent conduct in sending directions to O.I.C. Peliyagoda and O.I.C Sapugaskanda (in regard to handing over possession of the vehicle). (5) The vehicle was not a property taken in terms of section 29 (b) (of the Code of Criminal Procedure Act) as there was no reason to believe that the vehicle (and its contents) were the instruments or the fruits or evidence of crime. (6) If the legal owner was not known the Magistrate should have acted in terms of section 431(2) of the Code of Criminal Procedure Act, and, as the articles were not perishables, he could have detained them and published a notification in the court notice board and two public places to be decided on by him specifying the articles o",SRC030-s218(k),none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:136:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1986-22-2,commonlii-LKCA-1986-22,(1996) 1 Sri LR 346,LKCA,1986,A public official's conduct is considered fraudulent if they deliberately mislead a Magistrate by filing false reports.,"(1) The number plates of the lorry were available and the Court or the O.I.C. Ragama could have found out who the registered owner of the vehicle was. (2) The conduct of the O.I.C. Ragama was fraudulent in that he deliberately misled the Magistrate by filing the 'A' report which led the Magistrate to include the lorry in the public auction. (3) The indecent haste in including the vehicle for sale and the sale for a paltry sum of Rs. 31,000/‐ disclose a covert manoeuvre by a judicial officer with deliberate assistance by a corrupt Police Officer‐ in‐ Charge of a Police Station. (4) The Magistrate acted in contravention of the provisions of section 431(2) of the Code of Criminal Procedure and his action is tainted and does not appear to be a bona fide mistake when one considers his subsequent conduct in sending directions to O.I.C. Peliyagoda and O.I.C Sapugaskanda (in regard to handing over possession of the vehicle). (5) The vehicle was not a property taken in terms of section 29 (b) (of the Code of Criminal Procedure Act) as there was no reason to believe that the vehicle (and its contents) were the instruments or the fruits or evidence of crime. (6) If the legal owner was not known the Magistrate should have acted in terms of section 431(2) of the Code of Criminal Procedure Act, and, as the articles were not perishables, he could have detained them and published a notification in the court notice board and two public places to be decided on by him specifying the articles o",SRC030-s218(k),none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
