scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:921:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-1994-18-1,commonlii-LKSC-1994-18,(2002) 3 Sri LR 299,LKSC,1994,The discretion of the court to order security for costs of the defendant under section 416 of the Civil Procedure Code should be exercised judicially and with reason; and the amount ordered should itself be reasonable,"(1) The discretion of the court to order security for costs of the defendant under section 416 of the Civil Procedure Code should be exercised judicially and with reason; and the amount ordered should itself be reasonable. (2) Section 416 provides for ""security"" which is required to be furnished, in order to create a fund from which an order for costs made by the court could be satisfied, if such costs are not directly paid by the plaintiff. ""Incurred"" costs under the section means that amount of costs which the court may financially award, regardless of what the party may actually spend. (3) In making the order the court should not be unduly influenced by the view that the plaintiff did not have a prirna facie case or the absence of a valuation report annexed to the plaint. (4) In the instant case order for costs was unnecessary and premature in that firstly, the trial could well be concluded within a short time; secondly, at 300 the stage of the",SRC030-s416,none,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:922:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-1994-18-2,commonlii-LKSC-1994-18,(2002) 3 Sri LR 299,LKSC,1994,"Section 416 provides for ""security"" which is required to be furnished, in order to create a fund from which an order for costs made by the court could be satisfied, if such costs are not directly paid by the plaintiff. ""Incurred"" costs under the section means that amount of costs which the court may financially award, regardless of what the party may actually spend","(1) The discretion of the court to order security for costs of the defendant under section 416 of the Civil Procedure Code should be exercised judicially and with reason; and the amount ordered should itself be reasonable. (2) Section 416 provides for ""security"" which is required to be furnished, in order to create a fund from which an order for costs made by the court could be satisfied, if such costs are not directly paid by the plaintiff. ""Incurred"" costs under the section means that amount of costs which the court may financially award, regardless of what the party may actually spend. (3) In making the order the court should not be unduly influenced by the view that the plaintiff did not have a prirna facie case or the absence of a valuation report annexed to the plaint. (4) In the instant case order for costs was unnecessary and premature in that firstly, the trial could well be concluded within a short time; secondly, at 300 the stage of the",SRC030-s416,none,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:923:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-1994-18-3,commonlii-LKSC-1994-18,(2002) 3 Sri LR 299,LKSC,1994,In making the order the court should not be unduly influenced by the view that the plaintiff did not have a prirna facie case or the absence of a valuation report annexed to the plaint,"(1) The discretion of the court to order security for costs of the defendant under section 416 of the Civil Procedure Code should be exercised judicially and with reason; and the amount ordered should itself be reasonable. (2) Section 416 provides for ""security"" which is required to be furnished, in order to create a fund from which an order for costs made by the court could be satisfied, if such costs are not directly paid by the plaintiff. ""Incurred"" costs under the section means that amount of costs which the court may financially award, regardless of what the party may actually spend. (3) In making the order the court should not be unduly influenced by the view that the plaintiff did not have a prirna facie case or the absence of a valuation report annexed to the plaint. (4) In the instant case order for costs was unnecessary and premature in that firstly, the trial could well be concluded within a short time; secondly, at 300 the stage of the",SRC030-s416,none,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:941:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-1996-16-1,commonlii-LKSC-1996-16,(1997) 1 Sri LR 103,LKSC,1996,"The pleadings in the case disclosed a ""conflict of claims"" between the widow and the executor within the meaning of Section 523 of the Civil Procedure Code, in view of which the claim of the executor has to be preferred to that of the widow. The expression ""conflict of claims"" cannot be confined to a situation where there are opposing","(1) The pleadings in the case disclosed a ""conflict of claims"" between the widow and the executor within the meaning of Section 523 of the Civil Procedure Code, in view of which the claim of the executor has to be preferred to that of the widow. The expression ""conflict of claims"" cannot be confined to a situation where there are opposing",SRC030-s523,none,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:962:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-1998-3-1,commonlii-LKSC-1998-3,(1998) 2 Sri LR 105,LKSC,1998,"1. Accordingly, in terms of section 2","1. Accordingly, in terms of section 2 (1) (a) a defendant is entitled to counterclaim for damages for wrongful arrest of a vessel where there was either mala fides or gross negligence which implies malice. Per Fernando, J. "". . . . the power of the court, consistently recognised and exercised over a long period of time to award damages for malicious arrest, is ancillary or incidental to its power to arrest a vessel, and can be exercised in the same proceedings. Such a claim for damages can be made without awaiting the termination of the proceedings."" 2. The High Court has the power to order the plaintiff to give security for the defendant's counter-claim for damages for malicious arrest. 106",SRC030-s654,none,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:963:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-1998-3-2,commonlii-LKSC-1998-3,(1998) 2 Sri LR 105,LKSC,1998,"(a) a defendant is entitled to counterclaim for damages for wrongful arrest of a vessel where there was either mala fides or gross negligence which implies malice. Per Fernando, J. "". . . . the power of the court, consistently recognised and exercised over a long period of time to award damages for malicious arrest, is ancillary or incidental to its power to arrest a vessel, and can be exercised in the same proceedings. Such a claim for damages can be made without awaiting the termination of the proceedings."" 2. The High Court has the power to order the plaintiff to give security for the defendant's counter-claim for damages for malicious arrest. 106","1. Accordingly, in terms of section 2 (1) (a) a defendant is entitled to counterclaim for damages for wrongful arrest of a vessel where there was either mala fides or gross negligence which implies malice. Per Fernando, J. "". . . . the power of the court, consistently recognised and exercised over a long period of time to award damages for malicious arrest, is ancillary or incidental to its power to arrest a vessel, and can be exercised in the same proceedings. Such a claim for damages can be made without awaiting the termination of the proceedings."" 2. The High Court has the power to order the plaintiff to give security for the defendant's counter-claim for damages for malicious arrest. 106",SRC030-s654,none,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1032:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-2004-21-1,commonlii-LKSC-2004-21,(2005) 1 Sri LR 31,LKSC,2004,1. The defendant failed to establish prescriptive title to the land as required by section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance. 2. The Court of,1. The defendant failed to establish prescriptive title to the land as required by section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance. 2. The Court of,SRC071-s3,none,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1067:draftly-rule-courts-coa-1095-99-f.pdf-1,courts-coa-1095-99-f.pdf,,COA,,The burden of proving prescriptive title falls on the defendant when the legal title is in the plaintiff.,"It is settled law that where the legal title is in the plaintiff the burden of proving prescriptive title falls on the defendant (Siyanaris Vs Jayasinghe Udeni De Silva 52 NLR 289, Juliana Hamine Vs. Don Thomas 59 NLR 546, Sirajudeen and two others Vs. Abbas 1994 (2) SLR 365).",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1072:draftly-rule-courts-coa-15.-ca-dcf-1058_2000.pdf-1,courts-coa-15.-ca-dcf-1058_2000.pdf,,COA,2000,"A conditional transfer of immovable property is not considered a mortgage for the purposes of the Debt Conciliation Ordinance, and therefore, Section 43 of the Ordinance does not apply to such transfers.",The amendment to the definition of ‘mortgage’ made by Act No. 5 of 1959 has not altered the law relating to the creation of a mortgage over immovable property and has not recognized as a mode of creating a mortgage the execution of a conditional transfer.,SRC029,ok,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1075:draftly-rule-courts-coa-25.-dcf-065-98-2.pdf-1,courts-coa-25.-dcf-065-98-2.pdf,,COA,,A court in a partition action cannot investigate the title to a portion of land excluded from the corpus or make any determination about the improvements standing thereon.,"If the trial Judge decides to exclude any portion of the land shown in the preliminary plan from the corpus he may do so, but he is not entitled to investigate the title to that portion or make any determination about the improvements standing thereon.",SRC027-s25,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1080:draftly-rule-courts-coa-4.-ca-1366-2000-f-11.3.2019.pdf-1,courts-coa-4.-ca-1366-2000-f-11.3.2019.pdf,,COA,2019,"In a rei vindicatio action, the plaintiff must strictly prove their title as pleaded and relied upon by them, and the defendant need not prove anything.","It is well settled law that in a rei vindicatio action, the plaintiff must strictly prove his title as pleaded and relied upon by him no matter how weak the defendant’s case is. The defendant need not prove anything, still less his own title. If the plaintiff fails to prove his title, his action must fail.",SRC030-s323,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1081:draftly-rule-courts-coa-43.-corrected-caption.pdf-1,courts-coa-43.-corrected-caption.pdf,,COA,,"A party can claim their rights when partitioning land according to the Partition Act, even if the plaintiff's pleadings have discrepancies and errors.","all parties can claim their rights when partitioning lot 1 and 2 of the preliminary plan, according to the partition act.",SRC027,ok,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1085:draftly-rule-courts-coa-628-99-dc-negombo-27.2.19-autosaved.pdf-1,courts-coa-628-99-dc-negombo-27.2.19-autosaved.pdf,,COA,,A person claiming prescriptive title must show a change in the nature of the possession and an ouster.,"It is to be noted that the above admission of the 2nd Defendant creates an absurdity to the adverse possession claimed by him and also he himself admitted that the Ladis Laus Perera is the owner of the house. In Tillekaratne Vs. Bastian [(1918) 21 NLR 12] it is an established principle of law that a person who has entered into possession of land as a licensee is presumed to continue to possess it in the same capacity. Where a licensee claims that his original possession has later become adverse, he must prove of the manifestation of his intention to possess adversely to the true owner by what is sometimes referred to as an ‘overt unequivocal act’.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1087:draftly-rule-courts-coa-BOR-0001-2027.03.2026-1.pdf-1,courts-coa-BOR-0001-2027.03.2026-1.pdf,,COA,2026,"The valuation submitted by the Appellant's expert witness must be supported by evidence, both oral and documentary.","Hence, it clearly, appears that the valuation submitted by Mr. Kaleel- Private Valuer for the original Appellant, of the land intended to be acquired by the Respondent on the date of the publication of the notice under section 7 of the Act in the Gazette (A2/R7) on 03.01.1994 had not been supported by any evidence oral or documentary by the Appellant and therefore, it is without any basis and as such it cannot, sustain both in fact and law and therefore, it should be rejected as rightly, held by LABR.",SRC029-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1088:draftly-rule-courts-coa-BOR-0001-2320.02.2026.pdf-1,courts-coa-BOR-0001-2320.02.2026.pdf,,COA,2026,"The burden of proof lies on the appellants to prove what they had asserted before it in terms of section 3 of the Evidence Ordinance to be read with sections 101,102 and 103 thereof.","rden of proof will lie on the Appellants to prove what they had asserted before it in terms of section 3 of the Evidence Ordinance to be read with sections 101,102 and 103 thereof.",SRC029-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1093:draftly-rule-courts-coa-c.a-dc-1289_2000.pdf-1,courts-coa-c.a-dc-1289_2000.pdf,,COA,2000,"A co-owner who has made improvements should be allotted the portion which contains the improvements, provided it does not cause substantial injustice to the other co-owners.","a co,owner who has made improvements should be allotted the portion which contains the improvements, provided it does not cause substantial injustice to the other co,owners,see J.D. liyanage v. L.H. Thegiris 56 N.LR 546.",SRC027-s33,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1095:draftly-rule-courts-coa-c.a-dc-135-1999f-1.pdf-1,courts-coa-c.a-dc-135-1999f-1.pdf,,COA,1999,A party invoking the provisions of section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance to defeat the ownership of an adverse claimant to immovable property must establish a starting point for their acquisition of prescriptive rights.,"Where a party invokes the provisions of section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance in order to defeat the ownership of an adverse claimant to immovable property, the burden of proof rests squarely and fairly on him to establish a starting point for his acquisition of prescriptive rights.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1096:draftly-rule-courts-coa-c.a-dc-135-1999f.pdf-1,courts-coa-c.a-dc-135-1999f.pdf,,COA,1999,A party invoking the provisions of section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance to defeat the ownership of an adverse claimant to immovable property must establish a starting point for their acquisition of prescriptive rights.,"Where a party invokes the provisions of section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance in order to defeat the ownership of an adverse claimant to immovable property, the burden of proof rests squarely and fairly on him to establish a starting point for his acquisition of prescriptive rights.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
