scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1102:draftly-rule-courts-coa-c.a-dc-505_2000.pdf-1,courts-coa-c.a-dc-505_2000.pdf,,COA,2000,A court cannot rewrite a contract entered into between parties.,Court cannot rewrite a contract entered into between parties.,SRC027-s25(1),none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1103:draftly-rule-courts-coa-c.a-dc-555-1994-1.pdf-1,courts-coa-c.a-dc-555-1994-1.pdf,,COA,1994,A right of way by necessity must be granted by the court to enable a person to access a public road by the shortest way and with the least damage.,"The underlying principle seems to be that laid down in the opinion of Grotius which is followed in De Vaas vs. Mendis (1948) 49 N.L.R. 525, namely that where a party proves that he has",SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1106:draftly-rule-courts-coa-c.a-dc-785-1998.pdf-1,courts-coa-c.a-dc-785-1998.pdf,,COA,1998,"In a Rei-Vindicatio action, the Plaintiff must prove his title and cannot rely on the weakness of the Defendant's title.","It is settled law that in a Rei-Vindicatio action, the Plaintiff must prove his title in establishing his rights. The Plaintiff cannot rely on the weakness of the Defendant's title.",SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1111:draftly-rule-courts-coa-c.a-dcf-808-1999.pdf-1,courts-coa-c.a-dcf-808-1999.pdf,,COA,1999,A District Judge trying a partition action is under a sacred duty to investigate into title on all material that is forthcoming at the commencement of the trial.,"In the exercise of this sacred duty to investigate title, a Trial Judge cannot be found fault with for being too careful in his investigation. He has every right even to call for evidence after the parties have closed their cases.",SRC027-s25,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1112:draftly-rule-courts-coa-c.a-dcf-858-1996.pdf-1,courts-coa-c.a-dcf-858-1996.pdf,,COA,1996,"A person who is let into occupation of property as a licensee must be deemed to continue to occupy on the same footing, and to prove possession, there must be some overt act which manifests their intention to occupy.","A person who is let into occupation of property as a licensee must be deemed to continue to occupy on the same footing. Therefore, in order to prove possession there must be some overt act which manifest his intention to occupying.",SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1120:draftly-rule-courts-coa-c.a-rev_15_2016.pdf-1,courts-coa-c.a-rev_15_2016.pdf,,COA,2016,A party cannot invoke the jurisdiction of the court by way of revision and/or restitutio in integrum if they have been guilty of a blatant lack of due diligence.,The remedy of restitutio in integrum is not available to a party that has been guilty of a blatant lack of due diligence [Sri Lanka Insurance Corporation Ltd v. Shanmugam and Another (1995) 1 Sri.L.R. 55].,SRC030-s211,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1125:draftly-rule-courts-coa-c.a.-dc_1043_1997.pdf-1,courts-coa-c.a.-dc_1043_1997.pdf,,COA,1997,"The burden of seeking and getting evidence before court, in the course of investigation of title to the land sought to be partitioned by parties before Court, prior to deciding what share should go to which party is more the duty of the judge than the contesting parties.","The burden of seeking and getting evidence before court, in the course of investigation of title to the land sought to be partitioned by parties before Court, prior to deciding what share should go to which party is more the duty of the judge than the contesting parties.",SRC029-s110,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1128:draftly-rule-courts-coa-c.a.-dc_1344_2000.pdf-1,courts-coa-c.a.-dc_1344_2000.pdf,,COA,2000,"A person who has a right and sees another person infringing it, but does not object, may be estopped from asserting their right later.","Where A has a right or title which B is in fact infringing under a mistaken belief that his acts are not acts of infringement at all, and A is aware of his own title or right, and also of B's invasion of that title or right, and of his erroneous belief that he is not encroaching thereon, but is lawfully exercising rights of his own, and yet, with that knowledge, A so conducts himself or so abstains from objection, protest, warning or action, as to foster and maintain the delusion under which he knows that B is labouring, and induces B to act to his prejudice on the faith of the acknowledgment to be implied from such conduct or inaction or, A is not permitted afterwards to assert his own rights against B, or contest B's rights against himself,,1",SRC059-s84,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1131:draftly-rule-courts-coa-c.a.-dc_657_2000.pdf-1,courts-coa-c.a.-dc_657_2000.pdf,,COA,2000,"A court can only investigate title in a partition action within the limits of pleadings, admissions, points of contest, evidence both documentary and oral.","Although there is a duty cast on the court to investigate title in a partition action, the court can do so only within the limits of pleadings, admissions, points of contest, evidence both documentary and oral. The court cannot go on a voyage of discovery tracing the title and finding the shares in the corpus for them [Thilagaratnam v. Athpunathan and Others (1996) 2 Sri.L.R. 66].",SRC029-s90,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1133:draftly-rule-courts-coa-c.a.-dc_769-a-b_1996.pdf-1,courts-coa-c.a.-dc_769-a-b_1996.pdf,,COA,1996,Non-execution of a writ of possession does not prevent the successors of the judgment-creditor from claiming ownership of the land.,"Therefore, I hold that non-execution of the writ of possession does not prevent the successors of G. D. Clarice from claiming the ownership of the said lands by virtue of the judgment in D. C. Panadura Case No. 12417.",SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1137:draftly-rule-courts-coa-c.a.-phc_51_2013.pdf-1,courts-coa-c.a.-phc_51_2013.pdf,,COA,2013,"The right of prosecution for any crime or offence, other than treason or murder, shall be barred by the lapse of twenty years from the time when the crime or offence was committed, and the right to commence a prosecution must be exercised within this time limit.",The right of prosecution for any other crime or offence (save and except those as to which special provision is or shall be made by law) shall be barred by the lapse of twenty years from the time when the crime or offence shall have been committed.,SRC030-s456,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1142:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca-1130-99-10.01.19.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca-1130-99-10.01.19.pdf,,COA,,An interlocutory order of a District Court in a Partition Action is not a final judgment and can only be appealed with the leave of the Court of Appeal.,It is to be noted that the question arises whether the Interlocutory Order of the learned Judge of the District Court of Ratnapura falls under Section 754(1) or 754(2) of the Civil Procedure Code.,SRC030-s754,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1143:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca-1171-1998-f-18.1.2019.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca-1171-1998-f-18.1.2019.pdf,,COA,2019,A person who claims prescriptive title against the rightful owner who has the paper title has a very heavy burden to prove all the requirements prescribed in the Prescription Ordinance.,A person who claims prescriptive title against the rightful owner who has the paper title has a very heavy burden to prove all the requirements prescribed in section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1144:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca-118-2001.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca-118-2001.pdf,,COA,2001,A court cannot allocate a portion of land less than the minimum extent required by law regarding the sub-division of lands for development purposes.,"When reading Section 31 of the Partition Act No.21 of 1977 as amended by Act No.17 of 1997, it is clear that the court cannot allocate a portion of land less than the minimum extent required by law regarding the sub-division of lands for development purposes.",SRC027-s31,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1147:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca-417-1998-f-18.1.2019.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca-417-1998-f-18.1.2019.pdf,,COA,2019,"A party cannot be inconsistent in their approach in legal proceedings, and they cannot blow hot and cold, affirm and disaffirm the same transaction simultaneously to suit the occasion.","He cannot blow hot and cold, affirm and disaffirm the same transaction simultaneously to suit the occasion. The doctrine of approbate and reprobate forbids him from doing so.",SRC030-s772,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1148:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca-423-2000-f-22.1.2019.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca-423-2000-f-22.1.2019.pdf,,COA,2019,A person who claims prescriptive title against the rightful owner who has the paper title has a very heavy burden to prove all the requirements prescribed in section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.,A person who claims prescriptive title against the rightful owner who has the paper title has a very heavy burden to prove all the requirements prescribed in section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1152:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca-648-1999-f-16.1.2019.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca-648-1999-f-16.1.2019.pdf,,COA,2019,"A partition action cannot be maintained if a previous declaration of title action was dismissed due to non-appearance of the plaintiff, and the same cause of action is involved.",The abatement of an action for declaration of title to land is a bar against the institution of an action for partition in respect of the land where the same question of title is involved.,SRC030-s403,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1153:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca-669-670-2000-f-28.10.2019.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca-669-670-2000-f-28.10.2019.pdf,,COA,2019,The person claiming prescriptive title must prove all the ingredients stated in section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance to succeed on a claim of prescription.,The person claiming prescriptive title shall prove all the ingredients stated in section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance in order to succeed on a claim of prescription.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1158:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca-891-2000-f-21.1.2019.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca-891-2000-f-21.1.2019.pdf,,COA,2019,"A Settlement Officer's order under the Waste Lands Ordinance gives a title good against all others, including claimants who failed to appear.","Proceedings under the Ordinance relating to Claims to Forest, Chena, Waste and Unoccupied Lands, No. 1 of 1897, are proceedings in rem, and an order embodying an agreement or admission and falling under section 4(2) of that Ordinance gives to the claimants mentioned in the order a title good against all others, including the claimants who failed to appear before the Special Officer.",SRC027-s26(2),bad-source,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1159:draftly-rule-courts-coa-CA-DC-1198-96.pdf-1,courts-coa-CA-DC-1198-96.pdf,,COA,,"A surveyor in a partition action must verify their report and plan by an affidavit, and the affidavit must be attested by a Justice of the Peace.","Even though the commissioner of this case has submitted his report in the said form, in the affidavit, there is no name and rubber stamp of the Justice of the peace deemed to have attested the affidavit.",SRC027-s18(1),none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1160:draftly-rule-courts-coa-CA-DC-1308-98.pdf-1,courts-coa-CA-DC-1308-98.pdf,,COA,,"A party who executes a deed with a condition to re-transfer the property to the original owner upon payment of a specified sum, and fails to comply with that condition, is entitled to have a decree in their favor.","Hence, it is clear that the defendant has failed to establish that she did not receive the purchase price for the land she has alienated.",SRC059-s83,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1162:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca-dc-galle-993-97f-1.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca-dc-galle-993-97f-1.pdf,,COA,,"A judge's site inspection to decide on a partition scheme is not inconsistent with the Partition Law, and the Civil Procedure Code can be applied to fill the omission in a partition case.","An inspection of the subject matter by the judge in order to decide the question as to the feasibility of the division of the subject matter among the co-owners in terms of the interlocutory decree, on the invitation of the parties and with their express consent to abide by the decision to be eventually made after such inspection, in my opinion is perfectly in order and not inconsistent with the Partition Law.",SRC027-s79,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1165:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca-dcf-1102-98-28-10-2021.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca-dcf-1102-98-28-10-2021.pdf,,COA,2021,"A court can only investigate title in a partition action within the limits of pleadings, admissions, points of contest, evidence both documentary and oral.","Although there is a duty cast on court to investigate title in a Partition action, the court can do so only within the limits of pleadings, admissions, points of contest, evidence both documentary and oral.",SRC027-s25,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1171:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca-dcf-859-1998.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca-dcf-859-1998.pdf,,COA,1998,A party cannot claim prescriptive rights to a land if the date of commencement of adverse possession is specified to thwart the other party's attempt to claim the land on prescriptive possession.,"Without offering an explanation for the basis upon which the Plaintiffs say the Defendant commenced adverse possession of the land, the specific date mentioned in the plaint is misleading and intended to thwart any attempt on the part of the Defendant to claim the land on prescriptive possession, which he would have been entitled to do had the date of his commencement of adverse possession been a mere eight and a half months prior to that claimed by the Plaintiffs.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1179:draftly-rule-courts-coa-CA-PHC-148-2018-Judgement-Final.pdf-1,courts-coa-CA-PHC-148-2018-Judgement-Final.pdf,,COA,2018,"The jurisdiction of the Primary Court is not ousted by police inaction, and the court has jurisdiction to inquire into and determine the matter once information is filed, without requiring the Magistrate to independently assess whether a breach of the peace is threatened.","The Key difference under the present law is that whoever files the information, the Magistrate is automatically vested with jurisdiction to inquire into and determine the matter; there is no requirement for the Magistrate to independently assess whether a breach of the peace is threatened.",SRC030-s40(e),none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1187:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca-rev-1-2002-18.10.2019.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca-rev-1-2002-18.10.2019.pdf,,COA,2019,A party seeking a discretionary relief such as revision shall come to Court with promptitude.,Needless to emphasize that a party seeking a discretionary relief such as revision shall come to Court with promptitude.,SRC027-s25(2),none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1188:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca-rev-1582-2006-25.10.2019.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca-rev-1582-2006-25.10.2019.pdf,,COA,2019,"A settlement in a partition case is not prohibited by the Partition Law if all parties with a right, share, or interest in the land have been made parties to the case and all of them participate in the settlement.","Settlements in partition cases are not prohibited or obnoxious to the Partition Law provided (a) all the persons who have right, share or interest to, of, or in the land to be partitioned have been made parties to the case; and (b) all of them participate in the settlement.",SRC027-s25,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1190:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca-rev.-2028-2001.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca-rev.-2028-2001.pdf,,COA,2001,A party must receive notice of a final survey and the surveyor must comply with the relevant provisions of the Partition Law.,"Of consent, the learned District Judge inspected the land in dispute on 20.12.2000 and decided to examine both Surveyors and fixed the matter for inquiry.",SRC027-s32,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1191:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca-ri-20-2017-24.10.2019.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca-ri-20-2017-24.10.2019.pdf,,COA,2019,"The Court of Appeal can set aside the Judgment and the Interlocutory Decree, and add parties to the action, even after the Judgment is delivered, to avert a miscarriage of justice.","In terms of section 48(3), the powers of the Court of Appeal to set aside the Interlocutory and Final Decrees by way of revision and/or restitutio in integrum to avert miscarriage of justice when proceedings were tainted with fundamental vice have been expressly preserved.",SRC030-s48,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1193:draftly-rule-courts-coa-CA-RII-0001-23.pdf-1,courts-coa-CA-RII-0001-23.pdf,,COA,,An admission made by a party in a previous case cannot be used to challenge a court's order in a subsequent case.,"The above admission made by the present petitioner in his testimony in 540/Testamentary that Dingiri Banda’s father was Punchi Banda is an admission between same parties as in this case; and in addition to Chandra Devi could be substituted as per the order of the learned district judge dated 01.08.2016, due to provisions in section 394(2) of the Civil Procedure Code, this is also a reason that the petitioner cannot now claim on the strength of the blank space in the 04th column of P.15, that, Punchi Banda was not the father of Dingiri Banda.",SRC030-s394(2),bad-source,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1194:draftly-rule-courts-coa-CA-RII-0065-24.pdf-1,courts-coa-CA-RII-0065-24.pdf,,COA,,Restitutio in integrum is not available when a party fails to appeal within the prescribed period due to their own negligence.,"It is a well-established principle that when a party is afforded a clear legal avenue for redress, such as the right to appeal to the Supreme Court, the onus lies on that party to exercise the option within the prescribed period.",SRC030-s753,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1237:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_1908_2005.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_1908_2005.pdf,,COA,2005,A respondent cannot invoke revisionary jurisdiction when they have an alternative remedy under section 389 of the Civil Procedure Code.,In W.K.M.D. Perera vs. People's Bank (1994) 2 Sri L.R. 384 an order absolute was made on default of the petitioner appearing. Ranaraja J held that the petitioner should seek his remedy under section 389 of the Civil Procedure Code and not by way of revision.,SRC030-s389,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1240:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_08_99.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_08_99.pdf,,COA,,"A constructive trust may be implied where the owner of property transfers it, and it cannot reasonably be inferred that the owner intended to dispose of the beneficial interest.","Where the owner of property transfers or bequeaths it, and it cannot reasonably be inferred consistently with the attendant circumstances that he intended to dispose of the beneficial interest therein, the transferee or legatee must hold such property for the benefit of the owner or his legal representative.",SRC059-s1,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1244:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_1160_00.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_1160_00.pdf,,COA,,A person who asserts the existence of a fact has the burden of proof.,lithe burden of proof in a suit or proceeding lies on that person who would fail if no evidence at all were given on either side,SRC029-s102,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1245:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_1187_98.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_1187_98.pdf,,COA,,"A party can make submissions on the devolution of title of the parties to the action even at the appeal stage, and the trial judge must investigate the title of the parties when it comes to proceedings in a partition action.","Such a duty is akin to inquisitorial system prevails over civil law jurisdictions. [Galagoda V. Mohideen 40 N L R 92. Gunatilleka V Murieal Silva 79 (1) N L R 481, Kularatne V Ariyasena 2001 B L R 06, Richard and Another V 5 .. Seibel Nona 2001 (2) S L R 01, Abeysinghe V Kumarasinghe 2008 B L R 300] Matters alleged by the 3rd defendant-respondent-petitioner in order to act under Section 773 of the Civil Procedure Code are basically in relation to the devolution of title of the parties to the corpus sUbjected to in this case. As mentioned earlier, aforesaid Section 25 of the Partition Act imposes a duty on the trial judge to consider those matters even if the parties have failed to refer to those matters at the trial.",SRC030-s25,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1247:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_1249_99.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_1249_99.pdf,,COA,,A court in a partition action has the power to exclude a separate land wrongly included by a plaintiff as being part of the corpus.,"In an action instituted under section 2 of the Partition Act to partition land the Court has inherent power, under section 839 of the Civil Procedure Code, to make an order excluding a separate or divided lot or land which has been wrongly included by the plaintiff as being part of the corpus",SRC030-s839,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1250:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_1295_1998.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_1295_1998.pdf,,COA,1998,"A tenant cultivator's rights are decided by the prevalent law, such as the Paddy Lands Act or Agricultural Lands Law, and cannot be changed without strong evidence to satisfy the requirements of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.","When a person enters a property in a particular capacity i. e tenant cultivator, his rights would be decided on the prevalent law namely the Paddy Lands Act or Agricultural Lands Law depending on the relevant period.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1255:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_242_99.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_242_99.pdf,,COA,,The burden of proof rests squarely and fairly on the defendant to establish a starting point for their acquisition of prescriptive rights.,"According to section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance such a possession must be undisturbed, uninterrupted, adverse to or independent of that of the former possessor and should have lasted for at least ten years before he could transform such possession into prescriptive title.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
