scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1294:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_955_1996.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_955_1996.pdf,,COA,1996,A contract may be avoided by the court on the ground of laesio enormis either when the purchaser pays more than double the true value of the thing or the vendor sells the thing for less than half its value.,A contract may be avoided by Court on the ground of laesio enormis either when the purchaser pays more than double the true value of the thing or the vendor sells the thing for less than half its value.,SRC029-s101,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1295:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_98_2001.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_98_2001.pdf,,COA,2001,"A partition decree is conclusive against the whole world and no ground, whether fraud or otherwise, can it be disturbed.",The partition decree is conclusive against the whole world and no ground whether fraud or otherwise can it be disturbed. The only remedy open to a party aggrieved by a partition decree is an action for damages.,SRC030-s187,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1297:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_anuradhapura_254_96.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_anuradhapura_254_96.pdf,,COA,,A plaintiff can seek an order of ejectment of the defendant who is in unlawful possession of his property without seeking a declaration of title to the same.,"It is quite interesting to note the manner in which the trial judge has answered issue No.1. Issue No. 01 is whether one Punchirala Jayasinghe was the owner of the land in question. This, the learned district judge has answered as being",SRC030-s35,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1300:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_badulla_1162_99.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_badulla_1162_99.pdf,,COA,,A person who enters into possession of a land in one capacity is presumed to continue in possession in the same capacity.,"a person who enters into possession of a land in one capacity, is presumed to continue in possession in the same capacity and a co-owner's possession in law tantamount to the possession of his other co-owners.",SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1303:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_balapitiya_830_98.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_balapitiya_830_98.pdf,,COA,,A party invoking the provisions of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance to defeat an adverse claimant's ownership of immovable property must establish a starting point for their prescriptive rights.,"Where a party invokes the provisions of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance in order to defeat the ownership of an adverse claimant to immovable property, the burden of proof rests squarely and fairly on him to establish a starting point for his or her acquisition of prescriptive rights.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1306:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_colombo_16_99.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_colombo_16_99.pdf,,COA,,"A defendant must establish that they have possessed the land in question for a period of 10 years immediately prior to the institution of the action, as contemplated in section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.","proof of undisturbed an uninterrupted possession by a defendant in any action, or by those under whom he claims, of immovable property, by a title adverse to or independent of that of the claimants or plaintiff in such action that is to say a possesswn unaccompanied by payment of rent of produce or performance of service or duty or by any other act by the possessor from which an acknowledgement of the right existing in another person would fairly and naturally be inferred for 10 years previous to the bringing of the action.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1310:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_colombo_780_98.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_colombo_780_98.pdf,,COA,,"A plaintiff can claim prescriptive title to land if they have been in uninterrupted possession of the land for ten years, without paying rent or performing any service or duty.",This position in law is supported by many authorities. Maasdorp's Institutes (7th Edition Vol2 at 96) Wigneswaran J in Leisa and another V Simon and Another [2002 (1) SLR 148] Pathirana V Jayasundera [58 NLR 169] Dona Cecilia V Cecilia Perera and others [1987 (1) SLR 235] Wijesundara and others v Constantine Dasa and another [1987 (2) SLR 66] I will now turn to consider whether the learned District Judge correctly evaluated the evidence and applied the law relevant thereto when he accepted the prescriptive rights .I 3 of the plaintiff rejecting the defence taken up by the defendant.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1311:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_colombo_907_99.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_colombo_907_99.pdf,,COA,,A party who claims prescriptive title to a particular allotment of land is obliged to clearly describe it either by boundaries or extent of the land that he claims to have prescribed.,A party who claims prescriptive title to a particular allotment of land is obliged to clearly describe it either by boundaries or extent of the land that he claims to have prescribed. Section 41 of the Civil Procedure Code requires to define such land with reference to physical metes and bounds or by map or sketch.,SRC030-s41,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1312:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_galle_1186_98.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_galle_1186_98.pdf,,COA,,A plaintiff cannot claim prescriptive rights to land when they are not certain that it belongs to the defendant.,"Hence, it is clear that the appellant could not have claimed prescriptive rights to the disputed land when he was not certain that it belonged to the respondent when claiming possession adverse to his rights.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1313:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_galle_123_99.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_galle_123_99.pdf,,COA,,A party invoking the provisions of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance to defeat ownership must establish a starting point for their acquisition of prescriptive rights.,"lt is trite law that where a party to an action for a declaration of title to a land invokes the Provisions of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance in order to defeat the ownership of an adverse claimant to immovable property, the burden of proof rests squarely and fairly on him to establish a starting point for his or her acquisition of prescriptive rights.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1316:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_galle_1292_98.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_galle_1292_98.pdf,,COA,,A party claiming prescriptive title must satisfy the court that they have established the ingredients in Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.,In order to prescribe to the said lots 9 and 10 it is important for the party concerned to satisfy Court that he has established the ingredients in Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1326:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_homagama_1113_98.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_homagama_1113_98.pdf,,COA,,"A person can claim prescriptive title to land if they have been in possession of the land for a period of over ten years, adverse to the rights of the original owner.","In the circumstances, it is abundantly clear that the respondent had been in possession of the land claimed by the plaintiff for a period of well over ten years prior to the filing of this action on 03.05.1991, adverse to the rights of the plaintiff which is the criteria applicable to claim prescriptive title in terms of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1332:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_kalutara_534_95.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_kalutara_534_95.pdf,,COA,,A certificate of sale does not divest the judgment-debtor's title to immovable property until the sale is confirmed by the court and a Fiscal's conveyance is executed.,The right and title of the judgment-debtor or of any person holding under him or deriving title through him to immovable property sold by virtue of an execution is not divested by the sale until the confirmation of the sale by the court and the execution of the Fiscal's conveyance.,SRC030-s289,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1336:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_kandy_1102_98.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_kandy_1102_98.pdf,,COA,,"There is no statutory bar to partition several lands held in common, but common ownership must prevail in respect of all lands.",There is no statutory bar to partition several lands held in common. The deciding factor seems to be that common ownership need to prevail in respect of all lands.,SRC027-s26(4),bad-source,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1339:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_kegalle_684_98.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_kegalle_684_98.pdf,,COA,,A transferee must hold property for the benefit of the transferor if it cannot be reasonably inferred that the transferor intended to dispose of the beneficial interest.,"Where the owner of property transfers or bequeaths it, and it cannot reasonably be inferred consistently with the attendant circumstances that he intended to dispose of the beneficial interest therein, the transferee or legatee must hold such property for the benefit of the owner or his legal representative",SRC059-s1,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
