scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1349:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_mt.lavinia_239_240_95.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_mt.lavinia_239_240_95.pdf,,COA,,"In a partition action, the trial judge must decide the nature and extent of interest each party is entitled to from and out of the corpus.","Section 25 of the Partition Law requires the court to examine the title of each party and try and determine all questions of law and fact arising in that action in regard to the right, share, or interest of each party to, of, or in the land to which the action relates, and shall consider and decide which of the orders mentioned in section 26 should be made.",SRC027-s25,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1351:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_mt.lavinia_619_99.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_mt.lavinia_619_99.pdf,,COA,,A party cannot deny an admission they made in a lease agreement.,"The contention of the learned President's Counsel on this matter, in my opinion is not without merit.",SRC029-s21,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1352:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_mt.lavinia_926_99.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_mt.lavinia_926_99.pdf,,COA,,"A court must investigate the title of parties, especially when it comes to claims of prescription among co-owners, as required by law.",It has been pointed out over and over again that it is the solemn duty of the learned District Judge to investigate the title of the parties specially with regard to claim of prescription among eo-owners as required in terms of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1353:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_mt.lavinia_932_932a_98.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_mt.lavinia_932_932a_98.pdf,,COA,,"A person must have undisturbed and uninterrupted possession of land for a period of ten years, adverse to the rights of others, to claim prescriptive rights.","law requires to have undisturbed and uninterrupted possession for a period of ten years, adverse to the rights of the others who claim rights to the land.",SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1356:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_rathnapura_729_00.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_rathnapura_729_00.pdf,,COA,,A possessor is always presumed to hold in his own right and as proprietor until the contrary be demonstrated.,A possessor is always presumed to hold in his own right and as proprietor until the contrary be demonstrated;,SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1358:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_hc_hambantota_33_2008.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_hc_hambantota_33_2008.pdf,,COA,2008,The law does not afford concessions to the conduct of persons who retain their self-control and give effect to a murderous intention in a more scheming and calculated manner.,"The law does not afford concessions to the conduct of such persons, who are nevertheless the direct fallout from grave and continuing cumulative provocation.",SRC029-s105,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1360:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_la_15_03.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_la_15_03.pdf,,COA,,"In effecting a partition, proper rights of way should be provided within the corpus to the district allotments as means of access to a public right of way.",It is trite Law that in effecting a partition proper rights of way should be provided within the corpus to the district allotments as means of access to a public right of way.,SRC030-s754(2),none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1361:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_la_420_2003.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_la_420_2003.pdf,,COA,2003,A court has the power to grant extensions of time for filing objections under section 91A(1) and (2) of the Civil Procedure Code.,"Where a day is fixed or time appointed for doing any act or taking any proceeding by a party to the action, the court may, from time to time, upon the motions of such party and if sufficient cause is shown, fix another day or enlarge or abridge the time appointed, upon such terms, if any, as to it may seem proper.",SRC030-s529(3),bad-source,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1362:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_la_498_2006.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_la_498_2006.pdf,,COA,2006,"A party cannot alter an Interlocutory Decree or Final Decree under the Partition Act without proving fraud, collusion, violation of the procedure or law, or a fundamental vice.","The petitioner has been an active party to the District Court case therefore he has to strictly prove that there has been a fraud, collusion of violation of the procedure or law.",SRC027-s48,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1366:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_ri_927_2008.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_ri_927_2008.pdf,,COA,2008,A party who has been sleeping over his rights does not get the privilege to invoke the discretionary jurisdiction of this court.,A party who has been sleeping over his rights does not get the privilege to invoke the discretionary jurisdiction of this court.,SRC027-s49,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1368:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_rv_262_2006.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_rv_262_2006.pdf,,COA,2006,"A decree entered in a partition action is final and conclusive against all persons whomsoever, except against a person who has not been a party to the partition action and claims a title to the land independently of the decree.",liThe three sub-sections taken collectively indicate that notwithstanding - a) any omission or defect of procedureJ or b) in the proof of title adduced before the courtJ or c) the fact that all persons concerned are not parties to the partition action; the decrees are final and conclusive against all persons whomsoever except against a person who has not been a party 9to the partition action and claims a title to the land independently of the decree.,SRC027-s48,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1391:draftly-rule-courts-coa-d.c._412_1995.pdf-1,courts-coa-d.c._412_1995.pdf,,COA,1995,"A Section 12 declaration under the Partition Law must be filed after the partition action is registered as a lis pendens, disclosing the names of all persons found to be entitled to a right, share, or interest in the land sought to be partitioned.","The purpose of this declaration is to satisfy the conscience of the Court that all persons who are seen upon an inspection of the entries in the Land Registry to be persons entitled to a right, share or interest in the land sought to be partitioned are before it.",SRC027-s12,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1393:draftly-rule-courts-coa-dcf_373_1989.pdf-1,courts-coa-dcf_373_1989.pdf,,COA,1989,A plaintiff claiming as a trustee must obtain a vesting order under section 112 of the Trusts Ordinance before instituting an action to safeguard or assert rights to the trust property.,"This was quoted with approval by Sripavan c.J. in Jegatheeswaran and another v. Rameswara Iyer and others [S.c. Appeal No. 8/2013, S.C.M. 24.01.2017]. The previous action (D. C. Chilaw Case No. 10/Trust) was to obtain a vesting order in terms of Section 112 of the Trusts Ordinance regarding the Kovil and its temporalities.",SRC059-s112,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1395:draftly-rule-courts-coa-HCC-21-20-JUDGMENT-4.pdf-1,courts-coa-HCC-21-20-JUDGMENT-4.pdf,,COA,,"A witness's credibility is not affected by trivial contradictions in their testimony, especially when they are consistent on the main points of the incident.","I hold that this trivial contradiction does not affect the credibility of this witness. On the other hand, he will remember what is important, which should have always been in his memory, because that incident would not fade from his memory.",SRC029-s165,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1402:draftly-rule-courts-coa-RII-0001-24-EDITEDpdf.pdf-1,courts-coa-RII-0001-24-EDITEDpdf.pdf,,COA,,"Before dismissing a partition action for non-prosecution, the court must compel the parties to bring the action to a termination.","In terms of the provisions of section 70 of the Partition Law, before dismissing a partition action for non-prosecution, the court has to compel the parties to bring the action to a termination.",SRC027-s70,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1403:draftly-rule-courts-coa-RII-0004-23-pdf.pdf-1,courts-coa-RII-0004-23-pdf.pdf,,COA,,A party who has an alternative remedy under the Civil Procedure Code cannot seek relief under Restitutio-in-Integrum.,"In the case of Letchumi v. Perera and another [2000] 3 Sri LR 151, it was held that “section 329 gives an alternative remedy to an aggrieved party. It is the duty of court to carry out effectually the object of statute. It must be so construed as to defeat all attempts to do so or avoid doing in a direct or circuitous manner that which has been prohibited or enjoined.”",SRC030-s329,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1404:draftly-rule-courts-coa-RII-0020-24-EDITED.pdf-1,courts-coa-RII-0020-24-EDITED.pdf,,COA,,A plaintiff in a rei vindicatio action must prove dominium to the land and its boundaries by evidence admissible in law.,"It is obvious that ownership cannot be ascribed without clear identification of the property that is subjected to such ownership, and furthermore, the ultimate objective of a person seeking to vindicate immovable property by obtaining a writ of execution in terms of Section 323 of the Civil Procedure Code will be frustrated if the fiscal to whom the writ is addressed, cannot clearly identify the property by reference to the decree for the purpose of giving effect to it.",SRC030-s323,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1405:draftly-rule-courts-coa-RII-0021-24-Judgment-pdf.pdf-1,courts-coa-RII-0021-24-Judgment-pdf.pdf,,COA,,"A person who has divested themselves of all right, title, and interest in a property is not a necessary party to a partition action.","The material placed before Court indicates that the petitioner had divested himself of all right, title, and interest in the property prior to the institution of the action.",SRC027-s5,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1409:draftly-rule-courts-coa-RII-0059-23-final.pdf-1,courts-coa-RII-0059-23-final.pdf,,COA,,A court may exclude a portion of land from a partition corpus if it is not properly identified or if the parties agree to its exclusion.,We grant relief (h) of the Petition dated 13.12.2023 and Lot 6 (Preliminary Plan) and Lot F (Final Plan) be excluded from the corpus.,SRC027-s18,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1418:draftly-rule-courts-coa-RII-35-23-2.pdf-1,courts-coa-RII-35-23-2.pdf,,COA,,A court can exercise the power of restitutio in integrum to set aside its own judgment if there has been a violation of the principles of natural justice.,"We are merely declaring that, so far as the petitioner is concerned, there has been a violation of the principles of natural justice which makes it incumbent on this Court, despite technical objections to the contrary, to do justice.",SRC027-s48,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1421:draftly-rule-courts-coa-RII-53-2024-Order-on-notice.-etc.-2.pdf-1,courts-coa-RII-53-2024-Order-on-notice.-etc.-2.pdf,,COA,2024,"A party dissatisfied with an injunction or enjoining order can apply to have it discharged, varied, or set aside.","Hence, apart from the suspension of the enjoining order to be effected in terms of section 664(3), an injunction [interim injunction] or enjoining order can be discharged, varied or set aside, by any party dissatisfied with that order, (i) on application made",SRC030-s666,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1436:draftly-rule-courts-coa-WRT-0054-20.pdf-1,courts-coa-WRT-0054-20.pdf,,COA,,The jurisdiction of the Court under Article 140 is supervisory in nature and primarily concerned with the legality of administrative action rather than the adjudication of disputed private rights.,It is well settled that the jurisdiction of this Court under Article 140 is supervisory in nature and is primarily concerned with the legality of administrative action rather than the adjudication of disputed private rights.,SRC029-s83,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1441:draftly-rule-courts-coa-WRT-0542-25.pdf-1,courts-coa-WRT-0542-25.pdf,,COA,,An administrative authority cannot disregard or pronounce upon the validity of a marriage evidenced by a certificate in the absence of a judicial determination.,"In the absence of a decree of nullity or other declaration by a competent Court, such marriage must be treated as subsisting and valid in law.",SRC030-s52,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1461:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc-_-appeal-_-39-_-2022_amd2.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc-_-appeal-_-39-_-2022_amd2.pdf,,SC,2022,"A power of attorney must be strictly construed, but its absence does not necessarily invalidate proceedings if the plaintiff has ratified the agency by their subsequent conduct of acquiescence.","If the authority of the agent is not in question, it would be obnoxious to common sense to insist on a stringent compliance with Section 25 (b) of the Civil Procedure Code so as to invalidate proceedings of a lower Court, when the Plaintiff had ratified the agency by his subsequent conduct of acquiescence in all the acts the said Nadarajah Thamilalagan took in this case.",SRC030-s25,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1465:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_100_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_100_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,The Debt Conciliation Board has the power to revise its own orders if it acts reasonably.,The Board has full power to even reason out their faults and revise its own orders.,SRC030-s6,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1467:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_101_2009.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_101_2009.pdf,,SC,2009,An action for damages should be filed within 2 years of the arrest.,"Moreover, in terms of section 9 of the Prescription Ordinance, action for damages should be filed within 2 years of the arrest.",SRC071-s9,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1468:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_101_2013-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_101_2013-1.pdf,,SC,2013,"To establish title by prescription, it is required that the possession is held as a title adverse to or independent of that of the claimant or plaintiff.","It is necessary to inquire in what manner the person who had been in possession during the time held it, if he held in a character incompatible with the idea that the title remained in the claimant to the property it would follow that the possession in such character was adverse....",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1470:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_101_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_101_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,A transferor cannot claim a constructive trust under Section 83 of the Trusts Ordinance if they did not transfer the property directly to the transferee.,"If person X transfers the property to person Y, if the attendant circumstances show that X did not intend to dispose of the beneficial interest therein, then , it can be held that Y had held the property for the benefit of X . If persons A,B and C transfer the property to person Y, how can X show any attendant circumstances that Y held the property for the benefit of X?",SRC059-s83,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1472:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_102_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_102_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,No issue was raised before the District Court as to the identity of the corpus.,It is observed that no issue was raised before the District Court as to the identity of the corpus.,SRC029-s23,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1473:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_102_2011-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_102_2011-1.pdf,,SC,2011,A co-owner's possession is in law the possession of his co-owners.,A co-owner’s possession is in law the possession of his co-owners. It is not possible for him to put an end to that possession by any secret intention in his mind. Nothing short of ouster or something equivalent to ouster could bring about the result.,SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1479:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_104_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_104_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,"If the court is satisfied that there are grounds to object to an application, the court should frame issues as in Section 533 of the Code and proceed with the matter.","In the matter of the Estate of the late Sinne Tamby Poothepillai 2 NLR 214 as per Bonser CJ at page 216 In Kanagaratnam Vs. Ananthathurai 46 NLR 302 It was held that, in an application for the issue of probate of a Will or Codicil it is the duty of Court, when the respondent shows grounds of objection to the application, to frame issues as required by Section 533 of the Civil Procedure Code.",SRC030-s533,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1482:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_105_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_105_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,A party who claims prescriptive title to a particular corpus must demonstrate possession over a specifically identified corpus.,"A person who claims a prescriptive title must demonstrate possession over a specifically identified corpus; failure to do so renders such a claim untenable, as a prescription cannot operate over an uncertain or unidentified land.",SRC029-s58,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1483:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_105_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_105_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,"A court may deal with the matter in controversy so far as regards the rights and interests of the parties actually before it, even if there is a misjoinder or non-joinder of parties.","Sec. 17: No action shall be defeated by reason of the misjoinder or non-joinder of parties, and the court may in every action deal with the matter in controversy so far as regards the rights and interests of the parties actually before it.",SRC030-s17,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1484:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_105_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_105_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,A party cannot acquire prescriptive title to a portion of land unless they can establish that their predecessors in title also possessed that portion in the manner set out by the relevant law.,"However, no such acceptable evidence was led before the District Court.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1486:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_106_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_106_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,"A party cannot shift their position from the pleadings to the trial and then on appeal, subject to strictly limited exceptions.",A party to an action is subject to specific constraints in presenting his case before Court. There must be consistency in how the case is presented from the original Court to the final Court. He cannot keep changing his position to suit the occasion. There must be an end to litigation.,SRC030-s149,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1487:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_106_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_106_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,"A court can make orders necessary for the ends of justice or to prevent abuse of process, including restoring a rightful owner in possession.",Section 839 of the Civil Procedure Code empowers the court to make orders that are necessary for the end of justice or to prevent abuse of process.,SRC030-s839,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1492:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_108_2022-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_108_2022-1.pdf,,SC,2022,A mere presence of a prayer for a declaration of title cannot make the action a rei vindicatio action.,Mere presence of a prayer for a declaration of title cannot make the action a re vidicatio action.,SRC029-s116,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1493:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_109_2022.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_109_2022.pdf,,SC,2022,A plaintiff must establish a prima facie case to justify the granting of an interim injunction.,The Plaintiff has failed to establish a prima facie to justify the granting of an interim injunction.,SRC059-s4,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1494:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_10_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_10_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,Mere possession does not suffice to prove prescription; adverse possession must be established.,"If that be so it would become adverse from 06.02.2003 or 07.02.2003 by forcible erection of fence, on the western boundary to lot 21. Plaintiff instituted action on 02.06.2003 which is shortly after the above erection of the fence. Therefore the period of 10 years cannot be contemplated or computed in this background.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1498:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_110_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_110_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,A settlement must be notified to the court by motion made in the presence of or on notice to all the parties concerned.,"applicant in person or his counsel or registered attorney, and a memorandum in writing of such motion shall be at the same time delivered to the court.",SRC030-s408,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1501:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_111_2019.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_111_2019.pdf,,SC,2019,The burden of proof shifts to the defendant to show independent rights in the form of prescription once paper title is undisputed.,Once paper title becomes undisputed the burden shifts to the defendant to show that they had independent rights in the form of prescription as claimed by them.,SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1504:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_113_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_113_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,A plaintiff must establish both title to the land sought to be partitioned and the devolution of title to the same.,"Considering the above, both the learned District Judge as well as the High Court of Civil Appeal was correct in holding that the Plaintiff had failed to establish, both, the title to the land sought to be partitioned as well as the devolution of title to the same.",SRC030-s150,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1505:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_113_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_113_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,"A party must adduce necessary material in their application to set aside an arbitral award, including grounds for setting aside under section 32 (1) (b), within sixty days of receipt of the arbitral award.",A finding cannot be made by the High Court in terms of sub paragraph (b) of section 32 (1) other than on the averments of the application and the material contained therein.,SRC030-s32,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1506:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_113_2019.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_113_2019.pdf,,SC,2019,A party can tag on to the possession of their predecessors to claim prescriptive title.,"In fact, a party can. [ see Terunnanse v Menike 1 N L R 200, Wijesundara and others V Constantine Dasa and Another (1987) 2 Sri L R 66, Kirihamy Muhandirama V Dingiri Appu 6 N L R 197].",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1513:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_116_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_116_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,The plaintiff must identify the land in suit to succeed in a rei vindicatio action.,The identification of the land is of paramount importance for the plaintiff to succeed in a rei vindicatio action.,SRC030-s323,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1514:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_117_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_117_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,"Res judicata cannot be applied if the cause of action in two cases are different, even if the parties are the same.","The cause of action in 888/RE had arisen for the Plaintiff father George, as the holder of the life interest of the property on which the house was situated, to sue the defendants who were one of his sons and the son’s wife, for eviction from the part of the house in which the Defendants were living along with the Plaintiff father, under the leave and licence of the Plaintiff father. The Plaintiff father wanted to get peaceful possession from the defendants. The cause of action in 4677/L had arisen for the Plaintiff Michael Ranjith, who was the other son of the Plaintiff in 888/RE, who had become the sole owner of the house and the land on which the house was situated to sue the defendants who were his brother and his wife, for a declaration of title to the said property and for eviction of the defendants from the house on the said property. It was a re vindicatio action.",SRC030-s207,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1519:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_118_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_118_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,"A trial judge must take custody of documents marked in evidence, even if they are subject to proof, and not return them to the parties for convenience.","There is no doubt that documents once marked in evidence become part of the record and should remain in the custody of court. (Section 114(2) of the Civil Procedure Code). As such it is the duty of the trial Judge to take to its custody, and not for convenience sake return same to the Attorneys of the respective parties.",SRC030-s114(2),bad-source,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1523:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_119_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_119_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,A party cannot claim a bigger land than they are entitled to under and in terms of their chain of title.,"Therefore, the plaintiff was certainly not claiming a bigger land than he was entitled to under and in terms of his chain of title.",SRC030-s41,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1524:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_119_2017_120_2017.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_119_2017_120_2017.pdf,,SC,2017,"A party who has performed their obligations under a contract is entitled to claim the balance sum due to them, despite any changes in the government or circumstances.","The claimants above named have performed their obligations to the full satisfaction of the SLPA and/or carried out the said services for which Sri Lanka Ports Authority has paid and settled a sum of US$ 60,000/- being 50% of the total payment that should be paid under the agreement mentioned above.",SRC030-s150,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1525:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_11_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_11_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,"In an action for declaration of title to land, the burden is on the plaintiff to prove that he has dominium.","Where , in an action for declaration of title to land, the defendant is in possession of the land in dispute, the burden is on the plaintiff to prove that he has dominium.",SRC030-s432(2),none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1527:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_120_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_120_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,Prescription begins to run from the date of the demand in an overdraft case.,"In the present case, prescription begins to run from the date of the demand.",SRC071-s7,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1528:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_120_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_120_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,Fresh evidence is not admitted in appeal unless it meets the test laid down in Ratwatte v. Bandara [1966] 70 N.L.R. 231.,"The general rule is that, fresh evidence is not admitted in appeal. However, in exceptional situations, it has been allowed as provided under section 773 of the Code of Civil Procedure read with Article 139(2) of the Constitution . The test in Ratwatte(supra) serves as a filtering mechanism to allow parties to submit fresh evidence where the test has been satisfied.",SRC030-s773,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1529:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_120_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_120_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,A power of attorney granted by a minor is void if the transaction is not for the benefit of the minor.,"It appears that she had considered that the Plaintiff, as per his age, was a minor in terms of our law at the time the said Power of Attorney was given and when the transaction is not for the benefit of the minor, it is not valid against the minor, and as such the Power of Attorney and the transactions based on that Power of Attorney are void.",SRC071-s10,bad-source,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1530:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_121_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_121_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,Declarations regarding relationship found in documents are relevant and admissible under sections 32(5) and 32(6) of the Evidence Ordinance.,"It is under these provisions, to wit, section 32(5) and (6) and section 50 of the Evidence Ordinance that it is possible to admit evidence, otherwise hearsay, of deceased persons figuring in a genealogical tree such as one often comes across in a partition case.",SRC029-s32,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1532:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_121_2019-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_121_2019-1.pdf,,SC,2019,"Courts should not permit appeals from preliminary rulings in special enactments that create limited and expedited remedies, as it would undermine the legislative intent and reintroduce procedural delays and uncertainties.","To permit appeals from preliminary rulings, such as orders on jurisdictional objections, would invite a reintroduction of precisely the procedural delays and uncertainties that the Act was enacted to eliminate.",SRC030-s15,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1533:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_121_2021.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_121_2021.pdf,,SC,2021,An affidavit in support of an application under Section 14A of the Civil Procedure Code must be prepared in accordance with the provisions of the applicable laws.,"It has further been observed in Kumarasiri and Another v Rajapakse [(2006) 1 Sri LR 359] that “... it is the flesh and blood of the affidavit which gives life to the skeleton in the petition. In the absence of a valid affidavit supporting the averments in the petition, the petition becomes a nullity.”",SRC030-s14A,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1534:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_122_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_122_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,A court cannot make a conveyance or settlement of property in favor of one spouse and deprive the other spouse of their share without a fair assessment of each party's contribution to the property.,It would be unreasonable to make such conveyance or settlement of the property in favour of the wife (Plaintiff-Respondent) and deprive the Defendant-Appellant-Respondent of his 1⁄2 share to the property.,SRC030-s615,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1535:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_122_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_122_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,Non-compliance with the rules in Sec. 31 of the Notaries Ordinance does not invalidate the Deed as provided for by Sec. 33 of the same Ordinance.,non compliance with the rules in Sec. 31 of the Notaries Ordinance does not invalidate the Deed as provided for by Sec. 33 of the same Ordinance; that section protects the deed.,SRC014-s31,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1537:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_122_2024.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_122_2024.pdf,,SC,2024,A constructive trust is created when the attendant circumstances point to a loan transaction and not a genuine sale transaction.,The trust is an obligation imposed by law on those who try to camouflage the actual nature of the transaction. When the attendant circumstances point to a loan transaction and not a genuine sale transaction the provisions of section 83 of the Trust Ordinance apply.,SRC059-s83,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1539:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_123_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_123_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,A placement of signature on an amicable partition plan by a co-owner consenting to the partition can be treated as an expression against his own rights as a co-owner and exclusive possession of each allottee of the allotment given to such allottee there onwards can be considered as adverse to the co-ownership rights that existed to the larger land and ten years of such possession will give prescriptive title to the allotted lot.,"On the other hand, as decided in the aforesaid Dona Cecilia V Cecilia Perera case even where the co-owners did not sign the partition plan other evidence may provide material to decide the starting point of adverse possession of allotted lots. As stated in Ponnambalam V Vaitialingam (1978-79) 2 Sri. L.R 166 the question whether a co-owner has prescribed to a particular divided lot as 27 against the other co-owners is one of fact, to be determined by the circumstances of each case. In the case at hand there was no acceptable evidence before the lower court judges to conclude that after the making of purported amicable partition plan, division by demarcation of the boundaries was done on the ground as well as the purported original owner of the purported corpus to be partitioned started possession to the exclusion of others. The Substituted Plaintiff in his written submission dated 7th November 2013 has stated that there are other modes of acquisition under Roman Dutch Law such as Occupatio and Accession which does not involve conveyance as contemplated by Section 2 of the Prevention of Fraud Ordinance. This appears to be in support of his argument that as co-owners have signed the plan there is no need of a notarially executed deed or evidence of prescriptive title. However, the Plaintiff while filing his amended plaint, neither had pleaded any other mode of acquisition under Roman Dutch Law nor he had led evidence in that regard. In his amended plaint dated 19.01.1998 at paragraph 3, the plaintiff had clearly relied on an amicable partition and prescriptive title acquired by the purported original owner of his pedigree, namely Andris Nanayakkara. The Plaintiff failed in proving prescriptive title of the said purported original owner as pleaded in his amended plaint. For the forgoing reasons this court cannot hold that the Learned High Court Judges erred or misdirect or non-direct them in interpreting the Ratio of aforesaid case Dona Cecilia V Cecilia Perera as well as erred in holding mere preparation of a partition plan No.2151 was not enough to validly give effect to an amicable 28 partitioning of a larger land unless such plan was followed by the execution of partition deeds since there was no acceptable evidence to prove prescriptive title. Thus, afore mentioned issues of laws are answered in the negative. Furthermore, for the reasons elaborated above it is clear that the Plaintiff failed in proving the existence and identification of the purported corpus sought to be partitioned. Hence this appeal is dismissed with costs.",SRC027-s18(1),none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1543:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_124_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_124_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,A co-owner has an interest in every part of the entire land.,"Thus, when a trespasser enjoys the fruits of the property the co-owner's rights are affected and he becomes entitled to eject the trespasser.",SRC030-s150,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1550:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_126_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_126_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,A claim of Laesio Enormis cannot be maintained if the seller knew the true value of the property at the time of sale.,"Citing relevant judgments pronounced in this regard by our Superior Courts, it was contended that under such circumstances, a claim of Laesio Enormis cannot be maintained.",SRC071-s10,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1557:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_128_2019.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_128_2019.pdf,,SC,2019,"If the language of the enactment is clear and unambiguous, it would not be legitimate for the Courts to add words by implication into the language.","If the language of the enactment is clear and unambiguous, it would not be legitimate for the Courts to add words by implication into the language. It is a settled law of interpretation that the words are to be interpreted as they appear in the provision, simple and grammatical meaning is to be given to them, and nothing can be added or subtracted.",SRC071-s9,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1560:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_129_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_129_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,"A servitude can be defined as a real right constituted for the exclusive advantage of a definite person or definite piece of land, by means of which single discretionary rights of user in the property of another belongs to the person entitled.","A servitude can be defined as ‘a real right constituted for the exclusive advantage of a definite person or definite piece of land, by means of which single discretionary rights of user in the property of another belongs to the person entitled’- Vide Von Vangerow, Pandekten, Volume III, page 3381 .",SRC030-s41,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1561:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_12_2006.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_12_2006.pdf,,SC,2006,The burden of proof lies on the party asserting that the condition of the land has changed.,"When a person is bound to prove the existence of any fact, it is said that the burden of proof lies on that person.",SRC029-s101,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1562:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_12_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_12_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,A court should not declare a property to be held for the benefit of the transferor under Section 83 of the Trusts Ordinance if the transaction was an outright transfer and not a loan transaction.,Law therefore does not declare under such circumstances (Section 83 of the Trusts Ordinance) that the Appellant would hold such property for the benefit of the Respondents.,SRC059-s83,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1565:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_131_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_131_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,Mere use of a footpath does not establish a servitude of right of way by prescription.,Merely walking on a footpath does not make it an adverse act conflicting with the rights of the owner.,SRC071-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1570:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_132_2018.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_132_2018.pdf,,SC,2018,A person can acquire land through adverse possession by possessing it openly and as if they were the owner for an uninterrupted period of ten years or more.,"For a comparable analysis, let me refer to the South African position. Section 1 of the Prescription Act, No. 68 of 1969 in South Africa reads as follows: “Subject to the provisions of this Chapter and of Chapter IV, a person shall by prescription become the owner of a thing which he has possessed openly and as if he were the owner thereof for an uninterrupted period of thirty years or for a period which, together with any periods for which such thing was so possessed by his predecessors in title, constitutes an uninterrupted period of 30 years.”",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1572:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_133_2019.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_133_2019.pdf,,SC,2019,"If a document is admitted subject to proof but not objected to at the close of the case, it becomes evidence in the case.",If no objection is taken when at the close of a case documents are read in evidence they are evidence for all purposes of the law.,SRC030-s5C,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1574:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_134_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_134_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,"A party to a partition action can transfer interests they would acquire upon the conclusion of the partition action, without violating Section 66 of the Partition Law.","Having looked at the authorities and analysing the cases, I feel that there is no bar preventing any person who is a party to a partition action, while the partition action is pending, from transferring the interests which he ‘would acquire’ upon the conclusion of the partition action.",SRC027-s66,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1579:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_135_2023.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_135_2023.pdf,,SC,2023,"A fiscal's conveyance is not required for title to pass in a fiscal sale, and possession by the purchaser for the prescriptive period establishes title by prescription.","If a fiscal’s conveyance is not executed in favour of the purchaser of a land at a fiscal sale, no title passes. If, however, the purchaser enters into possession after the sale and possessed the land for longer than the prescriptive period, title by prescription is established.",SRC071-s13,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1592:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_140_2019.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_140_2019.pdf,,SC,2019,The incidental involvement of a Kovil or the assertion of rival claims to trusteeship does not oust the jurisdiction of the Primary Court.,"The incidental involvement of a Kovil or the assertion of rival claims to trusteeship does not oust the jurisdiction of the Primary Court, which remains competent to make a preventive order, leaving the parties to seek a final determination of their rights before a competent court.",SRC030-s428,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1595:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_142_2018.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_142_2018.pdf,,SC,2018,A substituted party in an action is estopped from taking a contrary or inconsistent position to that of the original party to the action.,"when the original party agreed to certain arrangements before the Court, the substituted party is estopped and bound by the said arrangements.",SRC030-s395,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1598:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_144_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_144_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,A bona fide purchaser for valuable consideration cannot have their rights impaired by a trust.,"Nothing contained in this Chapter shall impair the rights of transferees in good faith for valuable consideration, or create an obligation in evasion of any law for the time being in force.",SRC059-s98,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1599:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_144_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_144_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,"Proof of prescriptive title against co-owners is more difficult. Unless the contrary is proved by cogent evidence, possession of one co-owner is in law the possession of the other co-owners.","Unless the contrary is proved by cogent evidence, possession of one co-owner is in law the possession of the other co-owners. (Corea v. Iseris Appuhamy (1911) 15 NLR 65, Sediris Appuhamy v. James Appuhamy (1958) 60 NLR 297 at 302-303, Rajapaksha v. Hendrick Singho (1959) 61 NLR 32, Maria Perera v. Albert Perera [1983] 2 Sri LR 399, Wickremaratne and Another v. Alpenis Perera [1986] 1 Sri LR 190 at 195, Maria Fernando v. Anthony Fernando [1997] 2 Sri LR 356)",SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1601:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_144_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_144_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,A party cannot re-agitate an issue of prescription before a higher court if they failed to appeal against the judgment of the lower court on that issue.,"His contention, therefore, is that the Defendant cannot reagitate the issue of prescription before this Court.",SRC030-s772,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1603:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_146_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_146_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,A party cannot obtain leave to produce a document under Section 175 of the CPC if they have been highly negligent in prosecuting their case.,leave under Section 175 of the CPC cannot be granted when a party is highly negligent in prosecuting his case.,SRC030-s175,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1608:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_148_2023.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_148_2023.pdf,,SC,2023,A final decree entered in a partition action cannot be challenged collaterally.,"Section 48 of the Partition Law underscores the finality and conclusiveness of partition decrees while providing limited circumstances under which they may be challenged (Fernando v. Marsal Appu (1922) 23 NLR 370, Mohamedaly Adamjee v. Hadad Sadeen (1956) 58 NLR 217, Madurapperuma v. Wijesundara [2019] 1 Sri LR 512).",SRC027-s48,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1611:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_14_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_14_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,The twelve months time frame in Section 52 of the Partition Law is applicable only if any interference or dispossession had occurred after the delivery of the possession.,Whether their Lordships of the Civil Appellate High Court have erred in law by failing to appreciate the fact that the twelve months time frame referred to in Section 52 of the Partition Law is applicable only if any interference or dispossession had occurred after the delivery of the possession?,SRC027-s52,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1616:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_151_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_151_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,The burden of proof lies on the party who wishes the court to believe in the existence of a fact.,"In terms of Section 103, since the Appellant has raised issues disputing that P 1 and P 2 is forgery he is the person who wishes the court to believe the existence of that fact. Hence the burden of proof lies on the Appellant to prove the existence of a forgery.",SRC029-s103,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1618:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_151_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_151_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,A co-owner's possession is in law the possession of all the other co-owners.,Every co-owner is presumed to be in possession in his capacity as a co-owner.,SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1619:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_151_2023.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_151_2023.pdf,,SC,2023,"A party asserting prescriptive title must discharge the burden of proof to establish uninterrupted, undisturbed, and adverse possession.","In the absence of such proof, the burden must be held not to have been discharged.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1623:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_153_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_153_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,"To claim a right of way, it is necessary to establish that the right of way exists at each and every point on the strip of land over which it is claimed.","The servitude, here a right of way, is one and indivisible, in the sense that it must be shown legally to exist at each and every point on the strip of land over which it is claimed and if the claimant fail to prove its existence at any one of such points, the servitude disappears not at that point only but at every other point;",SRC030-s41,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1624:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_153_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_153_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,"A person who is aggrieved of a judgment pronounced by a learned Judge of a District Court can prefer a notice of appeal in terms of section 755 of the Civil Procedure Code within the stipulated time, giving the particulars of case as required in the section so that the opposing party and the other relevant stakeholders is put on notice about his intention to appeal against the judgment.","A person who is aggrieved of a judgment pronounced by a learned Judge of a District Court can prefer a notice of appeal in terms of section 755 of the Civil Procedure Code within the stipulated time, giving the particulars of case as required in the section so that the opposing party and the other relevant stakeholders is put on notice about his intention to appeal against the judgment.",SRC030-s755,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1626:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_154_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_154_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,A conveyance of 1/3 of a share devolves 2/3 of the share on the grantor's descendants.,"Factually it was not the full 7 entitlement, but only 1/3 of Arnolis’s entitlement that devolved upon the said grantees since, Arnolis’s descendants as described by the Plaintiff below, purportedly became entitled to 2/3 shares of Arnolis’s entitlement;",SRC030-s187,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1630:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_155_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_155_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,The occupation of the premises must be of such character as is incompatible with the title of the owner.,The occupation of the premises must be of such character as is incompatible with the title of the owner.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1631:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_155_2022.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_155_2022.pdf,,SC,2022,A plea of prescription requires merely ten years of uninterrupted and undisturbed possession.,"Proof of the undisturbed and uninterrupted possession by a defendant in any action, or by those under whom he claims, of lands or immovable property, by a title adverse to or independent of that of the claimant or plaintiff in such action (that is to say, a possession unaccompanied by payment of rent or produce, or performance of service or duty, or by any other act by the possessor, from which an acknowledgment of a right existing in another person would fairly and naturally be inferred) for ten years previous to the bringing of such action, shall entitle the defendant to a decree in his favour with costs...",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1633:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_156_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_156_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,An appellate court should not interfere with the findings of facts of a learned judge who has had the advantage of seeing the demeanour of witnesses at the trial.,"Further, an appellate court should not interfere with the findings of facts of a learned judge who has had the advantage of seeing the demeanour of witnesses at the trial.",SRC030-s774,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1635:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_157_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_157_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,Parol evidence must be substantiated with attendant circumstances as allowed for under Section 83 of the Trusts Ordinance.,"The purpose of Article 83 has been eloquently noted by Basnayake C.J in Muttammah v. Thiyagaraja (1960) (62 NLR 559), “The section is designed to prevent transfers of property which on the face of the instrument appear to be genuine transfers, but where an intention to dispose of the beneficial interest cannot reasonably be inferred consistently with the attendant circumstances.”",SRC059-s83,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1639:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_158_2017.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_158_2017.pdf,,SC,2017,A party cannot change their claim or proof after presenting it to the court.,"The Plaintiffs had deliberately suppressed the fact that Lot 1 is a land which had already been partitioned in the previous partition action, Gampaha DC case No. 21754/P.",SRC027-s16(3),none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1640:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_159_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_159_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,A surveyor cannot divide a property without specific directions as to how much to be surveyed and the land be divided.,How can any surveyor survey and divide any property without any specific directions as to how much to be surveyed and the land be divided when there is no order as to the extent?,SRC030-s839,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1641:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_159_2019.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_159_2019.pdf,,SC,2019,"To claim prescriptive title, a party must prove possession by a title adverse to or independent of that of the claimant or plaintiff, with a specific starting point and documentary evidence.",Where a party invokes the provisions of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance in order to defeat the ownership of an adverse claimant to immovable property the burden of proof rests fairly and squarely on him to establish the starting point for his or her acquisition of prescriptive rights [Gratiean J in Chelliah v Wijenathan 54 N.L.R. 337 at 342].,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1642:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_15_2008.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_15_2008.pdf,,SC,2008,"A court may presume the existence of any fact which it thinks likely to have happened, regard being had to the common course of natural events, human conduct, and public and private business in their relation to the facts of the particular case – that judicial and official acts have been regularly performed.",This evidence contained in the affidavit has not been challenged either by raising an issue on this matter or calling the Fiscal Officer who executed the writ and eliciting the fact that possession had not been handed over as claimed by the Appellant. No independent evidence was led to rebut this presumption.,SRC029-s114(d),bad-source,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1643:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_15_2009.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_15_2009.pdf,,SC,2009,"In an appeal lodged against the judgment/decree made or entered by a court in a partition action, all the provisions of the Civil Procedure Code shall apply.","An appeal shall lie to the Supreme Court against any judgment, decree or order made or entered by any court in any partition action; and all the provisions of the Civil Procedure Code shall apply accordingly to any such appeal as though a judgment, decree or order made or entered in a partition action were a judgment, decree or order made or entered in any action as defined for the purposes of that Code.",SRC030-s67,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1645:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_15_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_15_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,A co-owner can file an action against a trespasser or imminent trespasser to protect his rights and a Court can grant a lesser relief even where a bigger relief has been prayed for.,"In our law, a co-owner can file an action against a trespasser or imminent trespasser to protect his rights and a Court can grant a lesser relief even where a bigger relief has been prayed for.",SRC030-s754(1),none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1659:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_166_2019.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_166_2019.pdf,,SC,2019,A mixed question of fact and law cannot be raised for the first time in an appeal.,"In the case of Setha Vs. Weerakoon 49 NLR 225, it was held, “A new point which was not raised in the issues or in the course of the trial cannot be raised for the 1st time in appeal, unless such point might have been raised at the trial under one of the issues raised, and the Court of Appeal has before it all the requisite material for deciding the point or the question is one of law and nothing more.”",SRC030-s545,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1662:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_168_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_168_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,The transferor must adduce evidence to show that he did not intend to dispose of the beneficial interest of the property.,"Sec. 83 of the Trusts Ordinance specifically states that where the owner of the property transfers or bequeaths it, and it cannot reasonably be inferred consistently with the attendant circumstances that he intended to dispose of the beneficial interest therein, the transferee or legatee must hold such benefit for the owner or his legal representative.",SRC059-s83,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1663:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_168_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_168_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,"A respondent who has not filed a written objection under Section 772 of the Civil Procedure Code may still be able to resist a decree of a lower Court in the interest of justice, even if the written objection has not been filed.","Hence, it is settled law that, even though a respondent-defendant who has not duly filed a written objection is unable to claim his statutory right to a decree as an entitlement, the Court sitting in appeal has the discretion, by virtue of its inherent jurisdiction, to permit such objection in the interest of justice.",SRC030-s772,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1664:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_169_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_169_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,A fresh lis pendence must be filed if the land described in the preliminary survey is significantly larger than the land described in the plaint as the land sought to be partitioned and a decision is made to partition the larger land.,It is of extreme importance to have a fresh lis pendence filed if the land described in the preliminary survey is significantly larger than the land described in the plaint as the land sought to be partitioned and a decision is made to partition the larger land.,SRC027-s16,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1666:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_169_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_169_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,"A judgment is not defective merely because the learned trial Judge has not set out answers to the points of contest in a numerical sequence, even though the reasoning of the judgment demonstrates that each of those matters has in substance been examined and resolved.","What the law insists upon is the disclosure of the judicial reasoning that led to the conclusion, not a rigid adherence to the mechanical form of answering each point of contest by number.",SRC030-s187,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1671:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_171_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_171_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,"In a possessory action, the plaintiff need not prove lawful title to the subject matter of the action.",It is absolutely clear that a possessory action can be instituted without proof of title. The Plaintiff in a possessory action need not prove at all that he has lawful title to the subject matter of the action.,SRC071-s4,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1675:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_172_2014_am1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_172_2014_am1.pdf,,SC,2014,A party seeking to challenge a notarially executed deed in a partition action must specifically plead the grounds of challenge and raise a corresponding issue.,"In the absence of such pleading and issue, when a pleaded deed is marked in evidence, the opposing party cannot merely rise and move that the deed shall be marked subject to proof.",SRC027-s68,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1679:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_174_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_174_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,A court must investigate the title of all parties in a partition action to ensure justice.,"Bare answers to issues or points of contest, whatever may be the name given to them, are insufficient unless all matters which arise for decision under each head are examined",SRC030-s187,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1680:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_174_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_174_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,"The plaintiff in a partition action must include in his plaint as parties to the action all persons who, whether in actual possession or not, to his knowledge are entitled or claim to be entitled to any right, share or interest to, of, or in the land to which the action relates.","For instance, section 5 of the Partition Law enacts that the plaintiff in a partition action shall include in his plaint as parties to the action all persons who, whether in actual possession or not, to his knowledge are entitled or claim to be entitled to any right, share or interest to, of, or in the land to which the action relates, whether vested or contingent, and whether by way of mortgage, lease, usufruct, servitude, trust, life interest, or otherwise, or to any improvements made or effected on or to the land.",SRC027-s5,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1684:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_175_2025.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_175_2025.pdf,,SC,2025,A deed executed before a notary can be proved by the notary if they knew the executant personally and can testify that the signature on the deed is that of the executant.,"Where a deed executed before a notary is sought to be proved, the notary can be regarded as an attesting witness within the meaning of section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance if the notary knew the executant personally and can testify that the signature on the deed is that of the executant.",SRC029-s68,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1685:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_176_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_176_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,"To prove possession, it is not necessary to prove that one lives in the property or occupies it.","In my view, occupation and possession are two different things. One can possess a property without occupying the same.",SRC030-s328,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1686:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_178_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_178_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,"A party cannot adduce oral evidence against the contents of a notarially executed deed to prove fraud and/or duress/intimidation, unless the fact may be proved which will invalidate any document on the basis of fraud, intimidation, illegality, want of consideration etc.","Under section 92 of the Evidence Ordinance oral evidence could not be led which is inconsistent with the contents of the deed. Under proviso (1) to section 92 of the Evidence Ordinance any fact may be proved which will invalidate any document on the basis of fraud, intimidation, illegality, want of consideration etc.",SRC029-s92,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1691:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_17_2018.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_17_2018.pdf,,SC,2018,The doctrine of res judicata should not bar the Plaintiff from proceeding with the 2nd Partition case.,"However, the doctrine of res judicata cannot be Page 18 of 18 completely barred from being employed to partition cases, and its application should be evaluated based on the specific circumstances of each case, particularly in relation to the concepts of ‘cause of action estoppel’ or ‘issue estoppel.’",SRC027-s12,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1693:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_181_182_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_181_182_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,A person's possession of land can be considered adverse to another's interest if it continues for a long time without interruption.,"The cumulative effect of the evidence of the Plaintiff supported by the evidence given by Grama Niladhari Gama Ethige Don Francis Lambert is a clear establishment before Court that the nature of the possession of the relevant land maintained and enjoyed by the 2nd, 3rd and the 4th Defendants, was a possession which was adverse to the interest of both the Plaintiff and Gnana Jayasinghe.",SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1701:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_185_2014-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_185_2014-1.pdf,,SC,2014,"Section 547 of the Civil Procedure Code does not prohibit the transfer of property which ought to have been, but has not been, administered.","Section 547 of the Code does not prohibit the transfer of property which ought to have been, but has not been, administered. It penalizes such a transfer, but the language in which the penalty is imposed as well as that of the section as a whole point, in my opinion, to the conclusion that the Legislature did not intend to do anything more than this",SRC030-s547,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1702:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_185_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_185_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,"A court must meticulously examine the evidence to determine the intention of the parties in a transfer of property, and the intention of the parties is of paramount importance.","One needs to bear in mind that where a constructive trust within the meaning of Section 83 of the Trust Ordinance is asserted, it is incumbent on the court to meticulously examine the evidence placed before the court, the reason being, on the face value the evidence placed may give the appearance of a straight forward transaction of a sale but the real intention of the parties can only be gleaned from a close scrutiny of the circumstances under which the transaction was effected.",SRC059-s83,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1705:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_188_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_188_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,"A plaintiff who has complied with Section 41 of the Civil Procedure Code by describing the land by reference to physical metes and bounds or by reference to a sufficient sketch, map or plan is entitled to a declaration of title.","According to Section 41 of the Civil Procedure Code the land must be described in the plaint so far as possible by reference to physical metes and bounds or by reference to a sufficient sketch, map or plan. The word „or‟ is important. Section 41 of the Civil Procedure Code expects the plaintiff to describe in the plaint the land by reference to physical 5 metes and bounds or by reference to a sufficient sketch, map or plan.",SRC030-s41,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1710:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_189_2017.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_189_2017.pdf,,SC,2017,A party cannot raise a new case for the first time in the appellate Court.,These matters cannot be taken up for the first time in the appellate Court.,SRC030-s46,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1713:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_18_2021.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_18_2021.pdf,,SC,2021,"Parol evidence can be admitted to establish a constructive trust, even if the trust is not declared in a notarially executed document.","The fact that a notarially executed written document is not an indispensable requirement to create a trust is also discernible by section 107 of the Trusts Ordinance, which recognises “De facto trusts”. It reads as follows: In dealing with any property alleged to be subject to a charitable trust, the court shall not be debarred from exercising any of its powers by the absence of evidence of the formal constitution of the trust, if it shall be of opinion from all the circumstances of the case that a trust in fact exists, or ought to be deemed to exist.",SRC059-s107,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1715:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_191_192_2025-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_191_192_2025-1.pdf,,SC,2025,The court shall admit a deed or document as evidence without requiring further proof if the opposing party has objected to it being received as evidence but not objected at the close of a case when such document is read in evidence.,"or (ii) if the opposing party has objected to it being received as evidence on the deed or document being tendered in evidence but not objected at the close of a case when such document is read in evidence, the court shall admit such deed or document as evidence without requiring further proof;",SRC030-s154A,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1716:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_191_2018.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_191_2018.pdf,,SC,2018,A bona fide purchaser's rights are unaffected by the existence of a constructive trust if they have paid valuable consideration.,"Nothing contained in this Chapter shall impair the rights of transferees in good faith for valuable consideration, or create an obligation in evasion of any law for the time being in force.",SRC059-s98,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1719:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_194_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_194_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,"To claim prescriptive title under Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance, the claimant’s possession to the property should be an adverse possession or independent possession.","To claim prescriptive title under Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance, the claimant’s possession to the property should be an adverse possession or independent possession.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1721:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_194_2018.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_194_2018.pdf,,SC,2018,A wider and purposive construction must be preferred to a narrow and restrictive approach when determining an application for the addition of parties under section 18(1) of the Civil Procedure Code.,"In determining an application for the addition of parties under section 18(1) of the Civil Procedure Code, a wider and purposive construction must be preferred to a narrow and restrictive approach.",SRC030-s18,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1722:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_195_2012-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_195_2012-1.pdf,,SC,2012,A constructive trust is created when the owner of a property transfers it without intention to dispose of the beneficial interest therein.,"When the owner of a property transfers it without intention to dispose of the beneficial interest therein, then a constructive trust is created and the transferee must hold such property in trust for the benefit of the transferor in line with the principle laid down in Sec. 83 of the Trusts Ordinance.",SRC059-s83,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1724:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_197_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_197_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,A party claiming a declaration of title must have title themselves.,"There is abundant authority that, a party claiming a declaration of title must have title himself. ... The authorities unite in holding that plaintiff must show title to the corpus in dispute and that if he cannot, the action will not lie.",SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1725:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_197_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_197_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,A defendant who fails to comply with the mandatory provisions of Section 75 of the Civil Procedure Code cannot be permitted to benefit from his own breach of the statute.,A defendant who fails to comply with these mandatory provisions cannot be permitted to benefit from his own breach of the statute.,SRC030-s75,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1727:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_198_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_198_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,The probative value of a death certificate is limited to the details of the birth or death or still-birth to which it refers.,The probative value of the contents of a death certificate issued under both Ordinance No. 1 of 1895 and Births and Deaths Registration Act No. 17 of 1951 is limited in terms of those two enactments to the details of the birth or death or still-birth to which it refers and applies to.,SRC029-s21,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1728:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_198_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_198_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,A party must be a defendant against whom the plaintiff has a cause of action which ought to be determined in the pending action itself.,"It seems to me to be correctly argued that those words plainly imply that the defendant to be added must be a defendant against whom the plaintiff has some cause of complaint, which ought to be determined in the action, and that it was never intended to apply where the person to be added as defendant is a person against whom the plaintiff has no claim, and does not desire to prosecute any.",SRC030-s18,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1729:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_199_2017.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_199_2017.pdf,,SC,2017,A court should have regard to the interests of all the beneficiaries when appointing new trustees.,"• This is a case inherent powers should be exercised for the ends of justice and to prevent abuse of the process of court. Being aggrieved by the said order of the High Court of Civil Appeals the Petitioner preferred an appeal to this court. When this matter was supported before this court, having heard the learned counsel, this court was inclined to grant leave to appeal on the questions of law set out in paragraph 12(a) to (e) of the petition which are as follows; (Vide journal entry dated 13.10.2017) a) Is the order of the High Court of Civil Appeal holden in Kandy and dated 2nd June 2016 contrary to Law? b) Did the High Court of Civil Appeal misdirected itself in law in coming to the conclusion that the Petitioner – Respondent is entitled to be added as a party in appellate proceedings? c) Did the High Court of Civil Appeal misdirect itself in law in calling in aid section 839 of the Civil Procedure Code in allowing the Petitioner – Respondent’s application for intervention? d) Did the High Court of Civil Appeal misdirect itself in law in delving into the merits of the Appeal when the only matter before them at present was the purported application for intervention? 9 e) Did the High Court of Civil Appeal misdirect itself in law in calling in aid Mahanayake Thero Malwatte Vihare Vs Registrar General, Kaviratne and Others Vs Commissioner General of Examination and Seneviratne Vs Abeykoon in allowing the purported application for intervention?",SRC030-s839,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1731:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_19_2008.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_19_2008.pdf,,SC,2008,"A court may order the addition of a party to a proceeding if the main evidence and inquiry will be the same, and the court can determine the rights of all parties in one proceeding.","It is sufficient if the main evidence and the main inquiry will be the same, and the Court then has the power to bring in the new parties and adjudicate in one proceeding upon the rights of all parties before it.",SRC030-s18,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1736:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_1_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_1_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,A document marked 'P3' without objection at the closure of the case is deemed to be admitted as evidence.,"Therefore, at this juncture, the document marked ‘P3’ ought to be admitted as evidence for all purposes of law considering the fact that no objection was taken at the closure of the Plaintiffs-Appellants case.",SRC029-s114,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1738:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_1_2022.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_1_2022.pdf,,SC,2022,An appellate court should be slow to reverse findings of fact made by a trial court.,"It is well-settled law that an appellate court should be slow to reverse findings of fact made by a trial court. In Allas vs Allas3 , Lord Radcliffe stated: ""To reverse this finding on appeal would be a strong step, only justified if the trial judge has demonstrably misjudged the position.""",SRC059-s84,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1739:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_1_2025-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_1_2025-1.pdf,,SC,2025,A party may successfully conduct a trial even without filing a list of witnesses and documents.,A party may successfully conduct a trial even without filing a list at all.,SRC030-s121,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1742:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_205_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_205_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,"A party to an action cannot lead evidence of rebuttal in an instance where the defendant was ordered to begin, as it would be a case materially different from that which they have placed on record.","In other words, as stated in explanation 02 appended to section 150 of the Civil Procedure Code, Evidence must reasonably accord with party's pleadings and no party can set up at trial a case which is materially different from that which he has placed on record",SRC030-s150,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1749:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_212_2017.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_212_2017.pdf,,SC,2017,The doctrine of Res Judicata does not apply to the instant case.,The Court of Appeal misdirected itself and/or erred in law by failing to appreciate that the doctrine of Res Judicata does not apply to the instant case.,SRC030-s207,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1753:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_218_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_218_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,Formal admissions recorded at the trial need no further proof unless the Court wants them to be proved.,"58. No fact need be proved in any proceeding which the parties thereto or their agents agree to admit at the hearing, or which, before the hearing, they agree to admit by any writing under their hands, or which by any rule of pleading in force at the time they are deemed to have admitted by their pleadings: Provided that the court may, in its discretion, require the facts admitted to be proved otherwise than by such admissions.",SRC029-s31,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1755:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_220_2017.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_220_2017.pdf,,SC,2017,The provisions of section 524(1)(a)-(d) are directory and not mandatory.,"However, willful suppression of material particulars will not be tolerated by court. It is in this context that Sirimane J. in the Biyanwila case stated at page 494 “I am of the view that the provisions of this section [524] are only directory, and that a failure to strictly comply with those provisions, does not render the proceedings void ab initio. They are, however, voidable, and in an appropriate case a party may ask the court for relief under section 839 of the Civil Procedure Code.”",SRC030-s524,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1759:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_225_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_225_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,"A lessee or licensee cannot question the title of the person who gave them the lease or licence, and must first quit the land before challenging the title.","No tenant of immovable property, or person claiming through such tenant, shall during the continuance of the tenancy, be permitted to deny that the landlord of such tenant had, at the beginning of the tenancy, a title to such immovable property; and no person who came upon any immovable property by the licence of the person in possession thereof shall be permitted to deny that such person had a title to such possession at the time when such licence was given.",SRC029-s116,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1762:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_22_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_22_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,Evidence recorded de bene esse during the course of the trial is not required to be read at the hearing of the action or to satisfy the court that the witnesses could not be produced at the trial.,"Accordingly, there was no necessity to read such evidence at the hearing of the action or to satisfy the Court, as contemplated by section 178(3), that the witnesses could not be produced at the trial.",SRC030-s178,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1764:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_231_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_231_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,"A deed for the sale or transfer of land, being a document which is required by law to be attested, has to be proved in the manner set out in Section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance by proof that the maker (the vendor) of that document signed it in the presence of witnesses and notary.","A deed for the sale or transfer of land, being a document which is required by law to be attested, has to be proved in the manner set out in Section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance by proof that the maker (the vendor) of that document signed it in the presence of witnesses and notary.",SRC029-s68,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1765:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_231_2017.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_231_2017.pdf,,SC,2017,"A judgment must articulate not only the decision itself, but also the rationale for arriving at that decision.","In terms of section 5 of the Civil Procedure Code, “judgment” means the statement given by the Judge of the grounds of a decree or order. Thus, a judgment must articulate not only the decision itself, but also the rationale for arriving at that decision.",SRC030-s5,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1766:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_232_2017.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_232_2017.pdf,,SC,2017,A licensee cannot claim prescriptive title against the owner or their heirs on the basis of the period they possessed the property.,"When a person starts possessing an immovable property with leave and licence of the owner, the presumption is that he continues to possess the immovable property on the permission originally granted and such a person or his agents or heirs cannot claim prescriptive title against the owner or his heirs on the basis of the period he possessed the property.",SRC029-s116,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1768:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_235_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_235_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,"In order to succeed in a claim of prescriptive title, proof of undisturbed and uninterrupted possession, under a title adverse to or independent of that of the owner of the immovable property, for a period of ten years is required.","Proof of the undisturbed and uninterrupted possession by a defendant in any action, or by those under whom he claims, of lands or immovable property, by a title adverse to or independent of that of the claimant or plaintiff in such action (that is to say, a possession unaccompanied by payment of rent or produce, or performance of service or duty, or by any other act by the possessor, from which an acknowledgment of a right existing in another person would fairly and naturally be inferred) for ten years previous to the bringing of such action, shall entitle the defendant to a decree in his favour with costs.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1771:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_239_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_239_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,A partition action can be instituted for a smaller portion of a larger land if the smaller portion is properly identified and the larger land is not jointly possessed or co-owned by the parties in the case.,"In this regard it is pertinent to observe that the 10th respondent who was present at the preliminary survey had objected for surveying a portion of the eight-acre land on the basis that he is entitled to shares from the larger land. However, he along with the appellant had showed the boundaries of the portion of the land in extent one rood and four point three zero decimal perches in extent, depicted as lot no 1 in the preliminary plan 2266.",SRC027-s25,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1772:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_23_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_23_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,"When interpreting a document, all clauses must be considered together, and not just one clause.",Clauses in a document must be interpreted in accordance with other clauses contained in the same document whether they precede or follow it.,SRC030-s147,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1775:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_23_2022ed1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_23_2022ed1.pdf,,SC,2022,"A party cannot make a case materially different from that which they have placed on record, and which their opponent is prepared to meet.","And no party can be allowed to make at the trial a case materially different from that which he has placed on record, and which his opponent is prepared to meet.",SRC030-s150,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1776:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_240_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_240_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,"In a rei vindicatio action, the plaintiff need not pray for a declaration of title and it is sufficient to plead title and prove it to obtain the relief of ejecting the trespasser.","Our Courts have decided that in a rei vindicatio action the Plaintiff need not pray for a declaration of title and it is sufficient to plead title and prove it to obtain the relief of ejecting the trespasser – vide Dharmasiri V Wickrematunga (2002) 2 Sri L R 218, T.B. Jayasinghe V Kiriwanegedara Tikiri Banda (1988) 2 C A L R 24 and Gallage Saummehammy alias Somawathie V I. A.Dharmapala SC App. 184/14 SC minutes dated 08.09.2022.",SRC030-s41,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1777:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_242_2016_amd1-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_242_2016_amd1-1.pdf,,SC,2016,A person cannot acquire prescriptive right to a property unless they produce an event or an overt act that manifests their intention to occupy the property as the owner.,the Respondent (Defendant) cannot acquire prescriptive right to the subject property unless he produces of an event or an overt act that manifest her intention to occupy the property as the owner as against the Petitioner (Appellant).,SRC071-s13,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1780:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_248_2017.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_248_2017.pdf,,SC,2017,"In partition actions, formal proof of execution of documents is generally not required unless the deed is specifically impeached.","While in ordinary civil proceedings the necessity of formal proof arises upon objection at the time of production, in partition actions such requirement is only triggered upon a clear act of impeachment, which entails contesting the deed by raising an issue or making a specific averment in the statement of claim.",SRC027-s68,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1781:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_24_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_24_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,New issues can be framed at a trial on the evidence led at the trial although facts relating to the new issues do not arise on the pleadings.,"In this connection, I would like to state here the duty of court in hearing a case is to arrive at the correct decision. This view is supported by the judicial decision in the case of Bank of Ceylon Jaffna Vs Chelliahpillai 64 NLR 25 wherein Privy Council at page27 held that the case must be tried upon the „issues on which the right decision of the case appears to the court to depend‟. Therefore, the court should allow the parties to frame issues to arrive at the correct conclusion on the evidence led before court even though the parties have failed to state facts relating to such an issue in the pleadings. Even if parties do not raise correct issues, court, on its own motion, should frame issues. Section 149 of the Civil Procedure Code reads as follows. The court may, at any time before passing a decree, amend the issues or frame additional issues on such terms as it thinks fit.",SRC030-s149,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1783:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_24_2022.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_24_2022.pdf,,SC,2022,A constructive trust does not automatically pass from one transferee to another upon subsequent conveyances.,The concept of trust does not pass automatically from one person to another with regard to the property and with regard to the original transferor since Section 83 of the Trusts Ordinance does not provide for such a reasoning to imply a constructive trust at all.,SRC059-s83,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1790:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_27_2018.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_27_2018.pdf,,SC,2018,"A person who has derived a contingent interest to the corpus of a partition action is entitled in law to execute a writ to obtain possession of his entitlement under the Partition Law, notwithstanding the fact that he is not a party to the original partition action.","A person who had derived a contingent interest to the corpus of a partition action is entitled in law to execute a writ to obtain possession of his entitlement under the Partition Law, No.21 of 1977, as amended, (hereinafter referred to as the “Partition Law”), notwithstanding the fact that he is not a party to the original partition action.",SRC027-s17,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1793:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_28_2008.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_28_2008.pdf,,SC,2008,"Where the owner of property transfers or bequeaths it, and it cannot reasonably be inferred consistently with the attendant circumstances that he intended to dispose of the beneficial interest therein, the transferee or legatee must hold such property for the benefit of the owner or his legal representative.","Where the owner of property transfers or bequeaths it, and it cannot reasonably be inferred consistently with the attendant circumstances that he intended to dispose of the beneficial interest therein, the transferee or legatee must hold such property for the benefit of the owner or his legal representative.",SRC059-s83,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1795:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_28_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_28_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,The burden of proving prescriptive right to a distinct and separate land is with the party claiming it.,once the paper title to the subject matter of this action is proved to be with the Plaintiff and the 1st Defendant the burden of proving the prescriptive right to the subject matter of this action as a distinct and separate land within the meaning of 5 SC/APPEAL/28/2014 section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance is with the 3rd Defendant which the 3rd Defendant has failed to do even on the finding of the Learned trial Judge as to the possession of the subject matter of this action?,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1796:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_28_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_28_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,"The possession of a property by a defendant can be considered 'undisturbed and uninterrupted possession' if they have been in continuous possession for a period of time, as established by the Prescription Ordinance.","In the presence of the above evidence, it is not difficult to hold that the possession of this property by Defendant is ‘undisturbed and uninterrupted possession’ within the meaning of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1804:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_2_2021.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_2_2021.pdf,,SC,2021,"To adduce fresh evidence, three conditions must be satisfied: (1) the evidence could not have been obtained with reasonable diligence for use at the trial, (2) the evidence must be such that, if given, it would probably have an important influence on the result of the case, although it may not be decisive, and (3) the evidence must be such as is presumably to be believed or, in other words, it must be apparently credible, although it need not be incontrovertible.","In order to justify the reception of fresh evidence or a new trial, three conditions must be fulfilled: first, it must be shown that the evidence could not have been obtained with reasonable diligence for use at the trial: second, the evidence must be such that, if given, it would probably have an important influence on the result of the case, although it need not be decisive: third, the evidence must be such as is presumably to be believed, or in other words, it must be apparently credible, although it need not be incontrovertible.",SRC030-s773,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1807:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_30_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_30_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,A person asserting adverse possession has no equities in his favour. He alone must prove prescriptive title with cogent evidence.,A person asserting adverse possession has no equities in his favour. He alone must prove prescriptive title with cogent evidence.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1816:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_32_2021.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_32_2021.pdf,,SC,2021,A party cannot take up new positions which are questions of fact for the first time on appeal.,The defendants cannot take up new positions which are questions of fact for the first time on appeal.,SRC029-s114,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1818:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_33_2009-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_33_2009-1.pdf,,SC,2009,The positive rule of law in Section 8 of the Prescription Ordinance applies when an action is filed after a lapse of one year after the date of the last invoice.,"In the circumstances, it is clear that the positive rule of law referred to in Section 8 of the Prescription Ordinance shall apply in this instance.",SRC071-s8,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1820:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_33_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_33_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,A person cannot acquire prescriptive title to property without demonstrating an overt act showing an intention to possess adversely to the owner.,"Without any demonstration of any overt act against the rights of the owner, court cannot recognize Nandasekera as a person who has acted as one holding prescriptive possession against the owner who had given him leave and license to step in and live there, no matter how long he had enjoyed the property.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1822:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_33_2019.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_33_2019.pdf,,SC,2019,A right of appeal must be specifically provided for in a statute.,"A right of appeal must, be specifically provided for. Such a right cannot be implied. Martin vs. Wijewardana [1989] 2 Sri LR 409.",SRC030-s23,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1823:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_33_2020.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_33_2020.pdf,,SC,2020,A defective power of attorney can be cured if the party ratifies the acts done by the lawyer on the defective proxy.,"If in fact he had his client’s authority to do so, then the defect is one which, in the absence of any positive legal bar, could be cured.",SRC014-s33,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1826:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_34_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_34_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,A person who strikes another with a formidable weapon must be presumed to have known that the act is imminently dangerous to the life of the person at whom he strikes.,Thus a man who strikes at the back of another a violent blow with a formidable weapon must be taken to know that he is doing an act imminently dangerous to the life of the person at whom he strikes and that a probable result of his act will be to cause that person’s death.,SRC029-s6,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1830:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_36_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_36_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,"A judge deciding a partition action must perform the duty cast upon them by the Partition Law, and cannot avoid it by allowing loopholes.",no loophole should be allowed to a judge by which he can avoid performing the duty cast expressly upon him by Ordinance.,SRC027,ok,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1831:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_36_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_36_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,"If no objection is made to a document being marked when it was first tendered in evidence, it becomes evidence for all the purposes of the case and it cannot be challenged only in appeal.","Hence, if no objection is made to a document being marked when it was first tendered in evidence, it becomes evidence for all the purposes of the case and it cannot be challenged only in appeal.",SRC029-s154,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1832:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_36_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_36_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,A court has no power to enter an order of abatement under Section 402 of the Civil Procedure Code where the failure to prosecute the action for twelve months after the last order was due to the death of the plaintiff within that period.,"In the case of Selamma Achie Vs Palavasam 41 NLR 186, the Supreme Court held that “ A court has no power to enter an order of abatement under Section 402 of the Civil Procedure Code where the failure to prosecute the action for twelve months after the last order was due to the death of the plaintiff within that period”.",SRC030-s402,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1842:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_38_2025.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_38_2025.pdf,,SC,2025,"A prima facie case requires the plaintiff to show a serious matter in relation to their legal rights, to be tried at the hearing, and that they have a good chance of winning.","In Sri Lanka we start off with a prima facie case. That is, the applicant for an interim injunction must show that there is a serious matter in relation to his legal rights, to be tried at the hearing and that he has a good chance of winning. It is not necessary that the plaintiff should be certain to win. It is sufficient if the probabilities are he will win.",SRC030-s1,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1844:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_39_2024.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_39_2024.pdf,,SC,2024,A successful action for declaration of title interrupts prescription.,A successful action for declaration of title is an interruption of possession.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1845:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_3_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_3_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,"In a partition action, if the corpus cannot be identified, ipso facto, the action shall fail.","In a partition action, if the corpus cannot be identified, ipso facto, the action shall fail. There is no necessity to investigate title until the corpus is properly identified. The decision that the corpus has not been properly identified decides the fate of the action 7 without further ado.",SRC027-s18(1),bad-source,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1846:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_3_2020.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_3_2020.pdf,,SC,2020,"A court cannot partition or make an order relating to a right, title, or interest in a land that fell outside the corpus.","In partition action, once a certain land has been excluded from the corpus sought to be partitioned, the court has no authority under the Partition Act to determine the right, title and interest of any person who claims to be entitled to the land that has been excluded, or to the plantations, buildings or other improvements on it.",SRC027-s26,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1849:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_40_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_40_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,A tenant who disclaims to hold of his landlord and puts him at defiance is not entitled to have the action against him dismissed for want of a valid notice to quit.,A tenant who disclaims to hold of his landlord and puts him at defiance is not entitled to have the action against him dismissed for want of a valid notice to quit [Muttu Natchia et al v. Patuma Natchia et al (1 NLR 21); Sundra Ammal v. Jusey Appu (36 NLR 400); Pedrick v. Mendis (62 NLR 471); K. Hassen v. A.O.Nagaria (75 NLR 335); Mansoor v. Umma (1984) 1 Sri LR 151; Subramaniam v. Pathmanathan (1984) 1 Sri LR 252; Ranasinghe v. Premadharma (1985) 1 Sri LR 63 at 71].,SRC030-s772,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1851:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_40_2018.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_40_2018.pdf,,SC,2018,A trial court has a bounden duty to examine and investigate the title in a partition action.,"...the court shall examine the title of each party and shall hear and receive evidence in support thereof and shall try and determine all question of law and facts arising in that action in regard to the right, share or interest of each party to, of, or in the land to which the action relates....",SRC027-s1,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1852:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_41_2008.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_41_2008.pdf,,SC,2008,A trial judge must consider and answer all issues raised by the parties in their pleadings.,"In the case of Warnakula Vs Ramani Jayawardena 1990 1 SLR 206 , it was held that “Bare answers to issues without reasons are not in compliance with the requirements of Section 187 of the Civil Procedure Code. The evidence germane to each issue must be reviewed or examined. The Judge must evaluate and consider the totality of the evidence. Giving a short summary of the evidence of the parties and witnesses and stating that he prefers to accept the evidence of one party without giving reasons are insufficient.”",SRC030-s187,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1853:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_41_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_41_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,"A constructive trust will be formed if it can be proven that the donor did not intend to transfer the beneficial interest, even if the written document appears to speak otherwise.","Section 83 of the Trusts Ordinance provides that if one can prove that in the attendant circumstances that if the donor did not intend to transfer the beneficial interest, even though the written document appears to speak otherwise, a constructive trust will be formed.",SRC059-s83,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1857:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_42_2015_46_2015-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_42_2015_46_2015-1.pdf,,SC,2015,The High Court should not summarily reject an application for enforcement of an arbitral award without giving an opportunity to tender duly certified copies.,The claimant should have been given an opportunity to tender duly certified copies interpreting “accompany” in section 31 (2) purposively and widely (as in Sri Lanka General Workers’ Union v Samaranayake and Nagappa Chettiar v. Commissioner of Income Tax.),SRC030-s31(2),bad-source,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1862:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_43_2017.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_43_2017.pdf,,SC,2017,A licensee cannot challenge the title of the owner of the property in suit.,"A licencee as the Defendant who entered the property in dispute with the leave and licence of Plaintiff cannot in law challenge the title of the owner of the property in suit, in this case the Plaintiff party.",SRC029-s116,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1867:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_44_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_44_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,A party is obligated to take actions and proceed with all possible and reasonable dispatch to prosecute an action without allowing it to accrue the case list.,"The work of the Courts must be conducted on ordinary business principles, and no Judge is obliged, or is entitled to allow the accumulation upon is cause list of a mass of inanimate or semi animate actions.",SRC030-s92,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1869:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_44_2019.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_44_2019.pdf,,SC,2019,An executor's conveyance is not necessary to pass title to the devisee to whom the property is devised.,The title of the property of the deceased testator vests with the heirs and a conveyance by the executor is not essential to pass title of the property owned by the deceased to his devisee under his Last Will.,SRC030-s5,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1870:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_44_2020-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_44_2020-1.pdf,,SC,2020,"A trial Judge must investigate the title of each party in a partition action, even if the plaintiffs have not pleaded for the partitioning of the portion of the land belonging to the defendant.","It has been determined that since it is the duty of the Court to investigate the title of each party, the learned District Judge could have examined the title of the defendant as well, although the plaintiffs have not sought a partition decree against the defendant.",SRC027-s25,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1872:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_45_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_45_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,"A sale of immovable property cannot be set aside on the ground of irregularity unless the applicant proves to the satisfaction of the court that he has sustained substantial injury by reason of such irregularity, and unless the grounds of the irregularity shall have been notified to the court within thirty days of the receipt of the Fiscal's report.","but no sale shall be set aside on the ground of irregularity unless the applicant proves to the satisfaction of the court that he has sustained substantial injury by reason of such irregularity, and unless the grounds of the irregularity shall have been notified to the court within thirty days of the receipt of the Fiscal's report",SRC030-s282,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1873:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_45_2017.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_45_2017.pdf,,SC,2017,"A claim of prescription cannot lie in respect of an undivided share, but only in respect of a definite and identifiable portion of land.","In any event, it is well settled that a claim of prescription cannot lie in respect of an undivided share, but only in respect of a definite and identifiable portion of land.",SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1875:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_46_2017.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_46_2017.pdf,,SC,2017,A bona fide purchaser is not bound to make an application under Section 578 of the Civil Procedure Code for a declaration that a person was of sound mind prior to the execution of a deed.,"Therefore, the questions of law [no. iv and viii] pertaining to whether the Court of Appeal erred by not taking into cognizance that the 2nd Defendant being a bona fide purchaser was not bound to make application in terms of Section 578 of the Civil Procedure Code for a declaration that the said Weerasinghe was of sound mind prior to the execution of Deed No. 21525 by the 1st Defendant and whether the Courts below err in law by the conclusion that the presumption of lunacy created by the Court Order was in operation as the Defendants had not taken steps under Section 578, must be answered in the affirmative, in favour of the Appellant.",SRC030-s578,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1876:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_46_2022.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_46_2022.pdf,,SC,2022,Attendant circumstances are to be considered as circumstances which precede or follow the transfer but are not too far removed in point of time to be regarded as attendant.,Attendant circumstances are to my mind circumstances which precede or follow the transfer but are not too far removed in point of time to be regarded as attendant which expression in this context may be understood as,SRC059-s83,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1880:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_47_2018.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_47_2018.pdf,,SC,2018,The discretion vested in the judge to continue with the trial or to commence proceedings afresh does not affect the nature of the order made under section 80 of the Civil Procedure Code relating to the fixing of the first trial date.,The discretion vested in the judge either to continue with the trial or to commence proceedings afresh does not affect the nature of the order made under section 80 of the Civil Procedure Code relating to the fixing of the first trial date. The order made fixing the date of trial in terms of section 80 becomes the day first fixed for trial within the meaning of section 93(2) of the Civil Procedure Code.,SRC030-s80,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1887:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_49_2003-part_II.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_49_2003-part_II.pdf,,SC,2003,A trial Judge should assess the oral evidence and bring his mind to bear on the facts relevant to the dispute and give reasons for his decision of the dispute as required by Section 187 of the Code.,A trial Judge should assess the oral evidence and bring his mind to bear on the facts relevant to the dispute and give reasons for his decision of the dispute as required by Section 187 of the Code.,SRC030-s187,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1889:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_49_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_49_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,"Courts should not reverse or vary judgments, decrees, or orders on account of errors, defects, or irregularities unless they have prejudiced the substantial rights of the parties or occasioned a failure of justice.","Provided that no judgement, decree or order of any court shall be reversed or varied on account of any error, defect or irregularity, which has not prejudiced the substantial rights of the parties or occasioned a failure of justice.",SRC030-s138,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1893:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_4_2007.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_4_2007.pdf,,SC,2007,An acknowledgment of liability in writing can take a cause of action out of the limitation period imposed by the Prescription Ordinance.,"If so, in any event, is the cause of action of the Plaintiff-Appellants set out in the Plaint, taken out from the limitation imposed in terms of Section 8 of the Prescription Ordinance?",SRC071-s8,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1894:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_4_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_4_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,The court cannot permit the addition of a party after the prescriptive period.,The District Court could not permit the Plaintiff to add a party after the prescriptive period.,SRC071-s9,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1897:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_4_2016-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_4_2016-1.pdf,,SC,2016,"A party relying on a notarially executed deed must formally prove it by calling the necessary evidence, including attesting witnesses, as required by law.","Section 68; If a document is required by law to be attested, it should not be used as evidence until one attesting witness at least has been called for the purpose of proving its execution, if there be an attesting witness alive, and subject to the process of the court and capable of giving evidence",SRC014-s31,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1898:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_50_2008.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_50_2008.pdf,,SC,2008,"A power of attorney serves a purpose similar to that of a proxy, in both an authority is being given by a principal to carry out certain functions.","A power of attorney serves a purpose similar to that of a proxy, in both an authority is being given by a principal to carry out certain functions.",SRC030-s46(2),none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1901:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_50_2018.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_50_2018.pdf,,SC,2018,A constructive trust may be implied by the court if it can be reasonably inferred from the attendant circumstances that the beneficial interest in the land was intended to be with the defendant.,"It will be apparent from Lewin’s observations that an implied trust is one which the Court deduces from the conduct of the parties, and the circumstances of the transaction. It is important to notice that here the function of the Court is to discover what the presumed intention of the parties was, and to give effect to it",SRC059-s90,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1903:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_51_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_51_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,A contract requires an offer and acceptance.,"every contract requires an offer and acceptance. Therefore an offer or promise which is not accepted, is not actionable [vide Justice Weerasooriya in Muthukuda v. Sumanatwathie, (1962) 65 NLR 205, 208, 209].",SRC029-s116,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1905:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_51_2020-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_51_2020-1.pdf,,SC,2020,Courts may countenance infirmities in proving original ownership of land if it is a realistic approach rather than a legalistic one.,"Courts by and large countenance infirmities in this regard, if infirmities they be, in an approach which is realistic rather than legalistic, as to do otherwise would be to put the relief given by partition decrees outside the reach of very many persons seeking to end the co-ownership.",SRC029-s32(5),none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1911:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_53_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_53_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,"A co-owner cannot acquire prescriptive title to undivided land, and a partition among co-owners terminates co-ownership only after undisturbed, uninterrupted, and exclusive possession of the divided lots for a period of over ten years.","Where a land is divided with the consent of all the co-owners but no cross conveyances are executed in respect of the lots, co-ownership terminates only after undisturbed, uninterrupted and exclusive possession of the divided lots for a period of over ten years",SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1914:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_53_2019.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_53_2019.pdf,,SC,2019,A party invoking the provisions of section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance must establish a starting point for their acquisition of prescriptive rights with consistent and credible evidence.,"In order to defeat the ownership of an adverse claimant to immovable property, the burden of proof rests squarely and fairly on him to establish a starting point for his or her acquisition of prescriptive rights (Chelliah vs. Wijenathan 54 NLR 337, Solomon Dias vs. William Singho and others [2015] 1 SLR 277).",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1920:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_54_2020.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_54_2020.pdf,,SC,2020,A defence of prescription must be specially pleaded by way of defence and cannot be raised for the first time in appeal.,"Basnayaka CJ, in Brampy Appuhamy v Gunasekera (supra) has held that (at p. 255); “[I]t is settled law that when, as in the case of Sections 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10 and 11 of the Prescription Ordinance, the effect of the statute is merely to limit the time in which an action may be brought and not to extinguish the right, the Court will not take the statute into account unless it is specially pleaded by way of defence”.",SRC071-s5,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1922:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_54a_2008.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_54a_2008.pdf,,SC,2008,"A plaintiff must adduce adequate and reliable evidence to establish prescriptive title, including assertive evidence of adverse possession as against mere evidence of occupation.","However, the evidence of the defendant and her son does not suggest that they did any such thing. In this connection, the defendant does not claim that she made any statement to the plaintiff‟s father that she does not accept his title. The defendant does not claim that, after the plaintiff‟s father obtained title, she made any alterations to the property or paid the rates and taxes in respect of the property in her name or obtained electricity and water connections in her name. The defendant has not led the evidence of the Grama Sevaka or her neighbours to suggest that she was considered to be holding the property in her own right after the property was transferred to the plaintiff‟s father in 1978.",SRC071-s13,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1924:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_55_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_55_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,"A judge must consider all prayers in a plaint, even in an ex parte trial.","Even in an ex parte trial, the judge must act according to law and ensure that the relief claimed is due in fact and in law, and must dismiss the plaintiff's claim if he is not entitled to it. An ex parte judgment cannot be entered without a hearing and an adjudication.",SRC029-s68,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1926:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_55_2020.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_55_2020.pdf,,SC,2020,A party must prove the due execution of a document to establish its validity.,"The learned judge has noted that both the 2nd Defendant and the Plaintiff have not discharged the burden cast on them by section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance read with section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance, which required them to prove that the two documents",SRC029-s68,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1929:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_56_2023.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_56_2023.pdf,,SC,2023,A plea of prescription requires ten years of uninterrupted and undisturbed possession.,"Proof of the undisturbed and uninterrupted possession by a defendant in any action, or by those under whom he claims, of lands or immovable property, by a title adverse to or independent of that of the claimant or plaintiff in such action (that is to say, a possession unaccompanied by payment of rent or produce, or performance of service or duty, or by any other act by the possessor, from which an acknowledgment of a right existing in another person would fairly and naturally be inferred) for ten years previous to the bringing of such action, shall entitle the defendant to a decree in his favour with costs...",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1935:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_58_2019.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_58_2019.pdf,,SC,2019,A person who possesses a land with secret intention cannot claim that his possession is an adverse possession.,A person who possesses a land with secret intention cannot claim that his possession is an adverse possession. Possession of a land by a person with secret intention cannot be considered as an adverse possession in terms of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1939:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_59_2018.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_59_2018.pdf,,SC,2018,"A voluntary alienation, lease, or hypothecation of an undivided share or interest in land during a partition action is void.","Any voluntary alienation, lease or hypothecation made or effected in contravention of the provisions of subsection (1) of this section shall be void.",SRC027-s66,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1941:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_59_2021.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_59_2021.pdf,,SC,2021,A court must consider the relevance of evidence before admitting it.,"Evidence may be given in any suit or proceeding of the existence or non-existence of every fact in issue, and of such other facts as are hereinafter declared to be relevant and of no others.",SRC029-s5,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1943:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_5_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_5_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,"A party claiming prescription must prove the commencement of adverse possession, which includes an act of ouster, prior to the running of prescription.",8 unless a party is able to prove that there had been an act of ouster prior to the running of prescription.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1945:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_60_2004.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_60_2004.pdf,,SC,2004,The failure to challenge evidence by cross-examination may not be sufficient to hold that a particular fact had been proved within the meaning of Section 3 of the Evidence Ordinance.,"It might, however, be a factor to be taken into account in accepting such evidence.",SRC029-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1948:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_61_2013-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_61_2013-1.pdf,,SC,2013,A party claiming prescriptive title to a land must identify the land with definite boundaries.,One cannot claim prescription to an unidentified or unknown land.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1950:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_63_2021.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_63_2021.pdf,,SC,2021,A document cannot be admitted into evidence until its genuineness has either been admitted by the opposing party or established through proof.,"The judicial approach to authenticity was clearly articulated in the case of Robins v Grogan [43 N.L.R. 269 at 270], where the Court held that a document cannot be admitted into evidence until its genuineness has either been admitted by the opposing party or established through proof.",SRC029-s67,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1954:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_66_64_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_66_64_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,"A decree in a District Court case is an accepted document by parties to that action, and it is not necessary to challenge its validity in a subsequent appeal.",The decree has clearly done justice by the parties by declaring the entitlement of parties and clearly mentioning the lots each party is entitled to. It is mentioned in the decree that the Plan P5 is part and parcel of this decree and filed of record in L3476.,SRC030-s1,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1963:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_69_2019-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_69_2019-1.pdf,,SC,2019,A party claiming prescriptive possession must provide clear and cogent evidence of an overt act marking the commencement of adverse possession.,"Moreover, our courts have consistently held that, where a co-owner claims prescriptive title, there must be clear and cogent evidence of an overt act marking the commencement of adverse possession.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1964:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_69_2020.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_69_2020.pdf,,SC,2020,A licensee cannot claim prescriptive title to a property without an overt act demonstrating adverse possession.,"A person who is let into occupation of property as a tenant or as a licensee must be deemed to continue to occupy on the footing on which he was admitted, until by some overt act he manifests his intention of occupying in another capacity.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1968:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_6_2023_ed1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_6_2023_ed1.pdf,,SC,2023,"A transfer of property shall be deemed to create a constructive trust if, having regard to the attendant circumstances, it cannot reasonably be inferred that the transferor intended to part with the beneficial interest therein.","A transfer of property shall be deemed to create a constructive trust if, having regard to the attendant circumstances, it cannot reasonably be inferred that the transferor intended to part with the beneficial interest therein.",SRC059-s83,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1974:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_71_72_2023.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_71_72_2023.pdf,,SC,2023,A substituted party in a partition action cannot take up a new position contrary to or inconsistent with that of the original party.,"A substituted party cannot take up a new position contrary to or inconsistent with that of the original party, as he merely steps into the shoes of the original party.",SRC027-s79,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1975:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_72_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_72_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,"A respondent to an appeal can resist the appeal and support the decree on any grounds decided against him in the court below, without filing a written objection under section 772, but can also file a written objection to dispute specific findings in or aspects of the decree.","Thus, where an appellant from a decree entered by an original Civil Court has filed an appeal seeking to set aside or vary that decree: the first limb of section 772 recognises the right of a respondent to that appeal to resist the appeal and support the decree on any grounds including those decided against him by the trial court, without filing a written objection under section 772; and the second limb of section 772 enables a respondent to the appeal who is dissatisfied with some specific finding in or aspect of the decree but has not filed an appeal to canvass it, to dispute that finding or aspect of the decree and seek to have it set aside or varied or decided in his favour by the appellate court, provided he has duly filed a written objection under section 772.",SRC030-s772,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1976:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_73_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_73_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,An appellate court should be slow to interfere with a trial judge's decision on facts and circumstances of a case.,"Hence, they become the best judges as to the facts of the case and His Lordship with approval referred to the observation made by Justice G.P.S.de Silva (as he then was) in the case of Alwis v. Piyasena Fernando 1993 1 SLR 111",SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1977:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_73_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_73_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,A plaintiff claiming a prescriptive use of a right of way over a defined route must imperatively comply with Section 41 of the Civil Procedure Code.,"David Vs Gnanawathie [2000]2 SLR 352 where it was held that a plaintiff claiming a prescriptive use of a right of way over a defined route should imperatively comply with Section 41 of the Civil Procedure Code,",SRC030-s41,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1979:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_73_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_73_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,A co-owner may sue another co-owner of an adjoining land for the definition of boundaries without the participation of other co-owners.,"It is a well-established principle of law that a co-owner could sue a trespasser without the participation of other co-owners. (Hevawitrane v. Dangan Rubber Co., Ltd. 17 NLR 49; Arnolisa et al v. Dissan et al 4 NLR 163; Meera Lebbe Cassi Lebbe Marikkar v. Kalavilage Baba (1885) 7 S.C.C.) If a co-owner could sue a trespasser, in the same way a co-owner should be able to be sued without participation of other co-owners.",SRC029-s115,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1981:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_74_2017.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_74_2017.pdf,,SC,2017,A subsequent purchase of title by a party claiming prescription to the same land does not negate the prescriptive title acquired by such party to such land.,"any subsequent purchase of title by a party claiming prescription to the same land, does not negate the prescriptive title acquired by such party to such land.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1983:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_75_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_75_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,A party does not need to show any other right to use a road if it is part of the subject matter in a previous partition action.,"Hence, the Plaintiff has every right to use this road as part of the subject matter in that partition action.",SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1988:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_77_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_77_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,A party's prescriptive title to a defined portion of land can be established by showing possession of that portion for a period of over 60 years.,"the Defendant-Appellant contends inter alia, that she and her predecessors in title possessed the unallotted Lot No. 3 in extent of 2R and 8.20P, depicted in Preliminary Plan No. 1175/P, dated 30/07/1998, made by A.C.P. Gunasena, Licensed Surveyor, for a period of over 60 years and thereby acquired prescriptive title to the said Lot No.3 in the said Plan No. 1175/P.",SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1992:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_78_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_78_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,A mere statement of a witness of occupying a land for long years would not suffice to satisfy the requirements of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.,A mere statement of a witness of occupying a land for long years would not suffice to satisfy the requirements of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1995:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_79_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_79_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,A plaintiff cannot proceed with a case on trust against a defendant who has bought the property for good consideration without the original owner's evidence.,How can only the 2 nd Plaintiff Farook go on with the case on trust against the Thero and the tenants who had bought the property for good consideration of Rs. 100000/- in the year 1990 from the 1 st Plaintiff Mowjood?,SRC059-s83,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1999:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_7_2020.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_7_2020.pdf,,SC,2020,"A person can acquire prescriptive title to land by possessing it for a long time, even if they do not claim it as part of a larger landholding.",There is no doubt that the 4th Defendant has prescribed to Lots D and E by a title adverse to or independent of that of the Plaintiff and the 1st to 3rd Defendants as required by section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2004:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_80_2018.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_80_2018.pdf,,SC,2018,A plaintiff must disclose in the plaint why the cause of action is not barred by prescription as per section 44 of the Civil Procedure Code.,"Section 44 of the Civil Procedure Code provides that “if the cause of action arose beyond the period ordinarily allowed by any law for instituting the action, the plaint must show the ground upon which exemption from such law is claimed.”",SRC030-s44,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2005:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_80_2022.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_80_2022.pdf,,SC,2022,Adverse possession commences from the moment the defendant intends to possess the land of the plaintiff as if he were the owner of it.,"There must be a corporeal occupation of land attended with a manifest intention to hold and continue it and when the intent plainly is to hold the land against the claim of all other persons, the possession is hostile or adverse to the rights of the true owner",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2009:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_82_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_82_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,Partition of land subject to service (rajakariya) to a temple or individual is not possible under the Partition Law.,Partition is possible subject to service.,SRC027-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2012:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_82_2021.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_82_2021.pdf,,SC,2021,The burden of proof lies on the person who would fail if no evidence at all were given on either side.,Section 102 – “The burden of proof in a suit or proceeding lies on that person who would fail if no evidence at all were given on either side.”,SRC029-s102,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2015:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_83_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_83_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,Dispossession may be by force or by not allowing the possessor to use at his discretion what he possesses.,"Dispossession may be by force or by not allowing the possessor to use at his discretion what he possesses whether it is done by sowing, or ploughing or by building or repairing something or by doing anything at all by which they do not leave the free possession of the person who was dispossessed.",SRC071-s4,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2016:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_84_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_84_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,"Courts must exercise great caution when applying Section 83 of the Trusts Ordinance, and must guard against allowing a false or belated claim of 'Trust' made by a transferor who has transferred his property and then had second thoughts or seeks to profit from changed circumstances.","The Court has to keep in mind that, a notarially attested deed of transfer should not be lightly declared to be a nullity. The Court must also guard against allowing a false or belated claim of ‘Trust’ made by a transferor who has transferred his property and then had second thoughts or seeks to profit from changed circumstances.",SRC059-s83,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2024:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_87_2017.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_87_2017.pdf,,SC,2017,"In a rei vindicatio action, the plaintiff must establish that he is the owner of the property.","Since the Plaintiff-Appellant sought a declaration of title to the property in question, the burden of proof is on him to prove that he is the owner of the property. This view is supported by judicial decision in the case of D.A.Wanigaratne Vs Juwanis Appuhamy 65 NLR 167 wherein this court held that in action rei vindication the plaintiff must prove and establish his title.",SRC030-s146(2),none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2025:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_88_2018.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_88_2018.pdf,,SC,2018,A party cannot put forward a ground of appeal for the first time in appeal unless it might have been put forward in the Court below and in other hand the matter in question should be on which deals with the pure question of law.,"Accordingly the Appellate Court could consider a point raised for the first time in appeal, if the following requirements are fulfilled. a. the question raised for the first time in appeal, is a pure question of law and is not a mixed question of law and fact; b. the question raised for the first time in appeal is an issue put forward in the Court below under one of the issues raised; and c. the Court which hears the appeal has before it all the material that is required to decide the question.",SRC030-s150,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2027:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_89_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_89_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,A party cannot file a re-vindicatio action against a co-owner without first obtaining a partition of the co-owned property.,Therefore there is no legal bar to allow the Plaintiff to proceed with the case as a re-vindicatio action against the Defendants.,SRC030-s147,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2031:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_8_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_8_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,"When the Plaintiffs claiming as Trustees institute an action to safeguard or assert rights to the Trust property and the question at issue is whether the title to the Trust property is vested in the Plaintiffs or in the Defendants, the Plaintiffs are not entitled to maintain the action without first obtaining a vesting order under Section 112 of the Trusts Ordinance.","When the Plaintiffs claiming as Trustees institute an action to safeguard or assert rights to the Trust property and the question at issue is whether the title to the Trust property is vested in the Plaintiffs or in the Defendants, the Plaintiffs are not entitled to maintain the action without first obtaining a vesting order under Section 112 of the Trusts Ordinance.",SRC059-s112,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2034:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_8_2018.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_8_2018.pdf,,SC,2018,A plaintiff must show the grounds on which they seek exemption from the operation of the law of limitation.,"the rule in Section 44 is mandatory and therefore whenever a Plaintiff seeks exemption from the operation of the law of limitation, he must show the grounds on which he seeks such exemption – see Don Palis Appuhamy v. Perera2.",SRC030-s44,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2036:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_8a_2009.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_8a_2009.pdf,,SC,2009,"A borrower who executes a deed of conveyance to secure a loan may reserve the beneficial interest in the property, and the transferee holds the property in trust until the loan is repaid with interest.",if the relevant “attendant circumstances” were sufficient to demonstrate that the Plaintiff hardly intended to dispose of his beneficial interest then it would be logical to elucidate that the beneficial interest of the property was not parted with by the Plaintiff.,SRC059-s83,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2039:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_90_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_90_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,"A person who has a grievance in a partition action where a writ issued, when the fiscal seeks to evict him, he could resist if he has a claim to remain in possession.","A person who has a grievance in a partition action where a writ issued, when the fiscal seeks to evict him, he could resist if he has a claim to remain in possession.",SRC027-s338,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2042:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_92_2012-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_92_2012-1.pdf,,SC,2012,A co-owner is not entitled as of right to be allotted the portion containing his improvements.,A co-owner is not entitled as of right to be allotted the portion containing his improvements.,SRC027-s33,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2043:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_92_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_92_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,A tenant cannot deny the landlord's title to the property during the continuance of the tenancy.,"No tenant of immovable property, or person claiming through such tenant, shall during the continuance of the tenancy, be permitted to deny that the landlord of such tenant had, at the beginning of the tenancy, a title to such immovable property;",SRC029-s116,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2046:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_93_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_93_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,A party is not entitled to plead legal or paper title and prescriptive title together in the same action.,a party is not entitled to plead legal or paper title and prescriptive title together in the same action,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2047:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_93_2017.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_93_2017.pdf,,SC,2017,"A party cannot invoke the extraordinary jurisdiction of revision as of right, and must explain in the petition why they did not exercise their right of appeal.","When a right of appeal is available against a Judgment or an Order, a party seeking to come before Court by way of revision shall explain in the petition why he did not exercise his right of appeal.",SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2048:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_94_2013-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_94_2013-1.pdf,,SC,2013,A possessory action under Section 4 of the Prescription Ordinance does not apply when the plaintiff's case is based on the terms and conditions of a lease agreement.,"Therefore, merely because the reliefs sought in the plaint are to restore him in possession, it cannot change the character and the scope of the plaint particularly when arguing an appeal.",SRC071-s4,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2050:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_94_2025.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_94_2025.pdf,,SC,2025,"To establish prescriptive acquisition of immovable property, a defendant must prove undisturbed and uninterrupted possession for 10 years.","Proof of the undisturbed and uninterrupted possession by a defendant in any action, or by those under whom he claims, of lands or immovable property, by a title adverse to or independent of that of the claimant or plaintiff in such action (that is to say, a possession unaccompanied by payment of rent or produce, or performance of service or duty, or by any other act by the possessor, from which an acknowledgment of a right existing in another person would fairly and naturally be inferred) for ten years previous to the bringing of such action, shall entitle the defendant to a decree in his favour with costs.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2052:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_95_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_95_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,A party is bound to take steps and proceed with all possible and reasonable expedition to prosecute an action without allowing it to accumulate on the case list.,"But whenever that step has been taken they are bound to proceed with all possible and reasonable expedition, and it is the duty of their legal advisors and of the Courts themselves to seek that this is done.",SRC030-s402,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2054:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_95_2018.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_95_2018.pdf,,SC,2018,Uncontradicted evidence adduced by a party is a fact in favour of that party's case.,"In other words, uncontradicted evidence adduced by the 2nd Defendant in the trial is a fact in favour of the 2nd Defendant’s case.",SRC029-s03,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2057:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_96_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_96_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,"A period of prescription can be 'tacked' onto another period, and the progress of prescription is not arrested by a disability such as minority or absence beyond seas.","As far as this case is concerned, the period from 1954 to 1962 [7 years] can be ‘tacked’ onto the period from 1979 to 1984 [6 years] in computing the period the Defendants were in possession for the purpose of term of prescription and regarding the application of the proviso to Section 3 of the Ordinance the progress of prescription is not arrested subject, however, to discounting the period Rosa enjoyed life interest to the corpus.",SRC071-s13,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2061:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_97_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_97_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,"When a deed is produced to prove a transfer of land, the court should examine the title carefully to determine whether the deed refers to the land in question.","The trial judge, having carefully examined the title correctly held that these three deeds refer to the land which is the subject matter of the partition case.",SRC030-s187,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2063:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_97_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_97_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,A possessory action presupposes the existence of possession ut dominus and a dispossession otherwise than by process of law.,A possessory action presupposes the existence of possession ut dominus and a dispossession otherwise than by process of law.,SRC071-s4,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2064:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_98_2007.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_98_2007.pdf,,SC,2007,"A court can exercise its revisionary jurisdiction to correct errors, even if the party affected has failed to appeal, if the error is a violation of a statutory provision.",Disregarding a statutory provision alone would amount to have established exceptional circumstances that are necessary to invoke revisionary jurisdiction.,SRC027-s25,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2069:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_99_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_99_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,A District Judge presiding over a partition action should not wait for some lapse on the part of the plaintiff to dismiss the action and relieve himself of further responsibility.,A District Judge presiding over a partition action should not wait for some lapse on the part of the plaintiff to dismiss the action and relieve himself of further responsibility.,SRC027-s52,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2070:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_99a_2009.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_99a_2009.pdf,,SC,2009,A party cannot make a case at trial materially different from the one they placed on record.,"Explanation 2 of S.150 of the Civil Procedure Code states the case enunciated must reasonably accord with the party’s pleading. And no party can be allowed to make at the trial a case materially different from that which he has placed on record, and which his opponent is prepared to meet.",SRC030-s150,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2071:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_9_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_9_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,A bona fide purchaser cannot be held not to own the property he bought on the ground that his predecessor was holding the property under a constructive trust.,"A bona fide purchaser cannot be held not to own the property he bought on the ground that his predecessor was holding the property under a constructive trust, specially when the person on behalf of whom he was holding the property in trust, directs him to transfer the same to another.",SRC059-s83,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2073:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_9_2017.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_9_2017.pdf,,SC,2017,"Once paper title becomes undisputed, the burden shifts to the defendants to show that they have independent rights in the form of prescription as claimed by them.",Once paper title became undisputed the burden shifted to the defendants to show that they had independent rights in the form of prescription as claimed by them.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2076:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_10_2017.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_10_2017.pdf,,SC,2017,A plaintiff cannot split a single cause of action into multiple actions against the same defendant without obtaining leave of the court.,"It is designed to prevent multiplicity of actions, vexatious litigation, and abuse of the process of court.",SRC030-s34,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2079:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_13_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_13_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,Substitution in appellate proceedings does not confer upon the person substituted the status of a legal heir of the deceased party.,"It is therefore not necessary to substitute all heirs of the deceased as parties to the appeal. As I have already stated, substitution is solely for the limited purpose of prosecuting the appeal.",SRC030-s760A,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2081:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_14_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_14_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,Silence in response to letters of demand amounts to admission of liability.,"In other words, there was a failure to respond to the letters of demand. These letters of demand were also marked in evidence and they had not been challenged. In the circumstances, it would appear that the Defendants admitted the liability to pay the monies mentioned in the said letters of demand.",SRC014-s31,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2083:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_19_2013-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_19_2013-1.pdf,,SC,2013,A party may lead evidence aliunde to controvert the consideration stated in a deed.,"By reason of proviso (1) to Section 92 of the Evidence Ordinance, a party is not per se precluded from leading evidence aliunde to controvert the consideration stated in a deed.",SRC029-s92,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2084:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_1_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_1_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,A party cannot recover a debt if the amount due has prescribed under the Prescription Ordinance.,"Focusing his attention on the sums of money purportedly due to the Plaintiff from the Defendant on account of the ‘running account’ (“P5” to “P23”), the learned Judge has noted that the amount due per each invoice had become prescribed (as per section 8 of the Prescription Ordinance) as a result of action not having been filed within 1 year of the last payment due.",SRC071-s8,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2088:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_22_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_22_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,Each cause of action must be considered to assess any inconsistency with Sections 33 and 34 of the Civil Procedure Code in light of the District Court action filed by the Plaintiff.,"Accordingly, it is my view that each cause of action must be considered to assess any inconsistency with Sections 33 and 34 of the Civil Procedure Code in light of the District Court action filed by the Plaintiff.",SRC030-s207,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2090:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_23_2008.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_23_2008.pdf,,SC,2008,"The prescription period should be calculated from the date of the alleged cause of action and the institution of the action in the District Court, not from the date on which the case was transferred to the Commercial High Court.","Hence, in computing the prescription period, it should be calculated from the date of the alleged cause of action and the institution of the action in the District Court and not from the date on which the case was transferred to the Commercial High Court.",SRC071-s6,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2092:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_24_2008.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_24_2008.pdf,,SC,2008,"A contract of suretyship is in essence a contract by which one person, the surety, agrees to answer for some existing or future liability of another, the principal (or principal debtor), to a third party, the creditor, and by which the surety’s liability is in addition to, and not in substitution for, the liability of the principal.","A contract of suretyship is in essence a contract by which one person, the surety, agrees to answer for some existing or future liability of another, the principal (or principal debtor), to a third party, the creditor, and by which the surety’s liability is in addition to, and not in substitution for, the liability of the principal.",SRC029-s101,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2094:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_26_2010-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_26_2010-1.pdf,,SC,2010,A sequestration order can be issued at the commencement of the action or at any time before judgment.,A sequestration order can be issued at the commencement of the action or at any time before judgment.,SRC030-s653,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2097:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_29_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_29_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,An unwritten promise or agreement between parties is enforceable if the action is commenced within three years from the time the cause of action arose.,"I hold according to the evidence placed by documents before court that there was an unwritten promise/bargain between the parties with regard to payments for the advertisements and programs telecast after 2004 December. The cause of action to recover the dues had arisen in June, 2005 and action had been filed in September, 2006 which is within 3 years from the date of the cause of action.",SRC071-s7,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2098:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_2_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_2_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,The burden of proof is on the judgment debtor to establish substantial losses if the writ is issued.,In the light of the foregoing case law this Court finds that the burden of proof is on the Petitioner (judgement – debtor) to establish substantial losses if the writ is issued.,SRC030-s763,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2100:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_32_32A_2008.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_32_32A_2008.pdf,,SC,2008,A party cannot raise new grounds on appeal or at a later stage of proceedings if such issues were neither reflected in the pleadings nor framed as issues during trial.,"As observed in Thalwatte vs. Somasundaram1 , a party cannot raise new grounds on appeal or at a later stage of proceedings if such issues were neither reflected in the pleadings nor framed as issues during trial.",SRC030-s150,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2101:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_35_2009.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_35_2009.pdf,,SC,2009,A cause of action based on a written promise or contract is not prescribed in law if the action is brought within six years from the date of the breach of such promise or contract.,"Before it could be said that the action falls under section 6 of the Ordinance, it must be shown that the action is based upon a written promise or contract.",SRC071-s6,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2103:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_39_2006.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_39_2006.pdf,,SC,2006,A court may frame additional issues at the stage of judgment if necessary for the just determination of the case.,the Court was entitled to frame the following two additional issues at the stage of judgment.,SRC030-s149,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2104:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_3_2000.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_3_2000.pdf,,SC,2000,A Public Officer cannot bind the State unless expressly empowered to do so.,a Public Officer cannot bind the State unless expressly empowered to do so.,SRC030-s756,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2105:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_3_2009.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_3_2009.pdf,,SC,2009,An action based on a written contract is not prescribed if brought within six years from the date of the breach of such contract.,"Section 6 of the Prescription Ordinance provides that; “No action shall be maintainable upon ... any written promise, contract, bargain, or agreement, ... unless such action shall be brought within six years from the date of the breach of such ... contract, bargain or agreement, ... from the date when such note or bill shall have become due, or of the last payment of interest thereon.”",SRC071-s6,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2109:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_45_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_45_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,"A demand for repayment of a loan must be clear, peremptory, and unconditional, and the word 'demand' need not be used.","It must be of a peremptory character and unconditional, but the nature of the language is immaterial provided it has this effect.",SRC030-s40(d),none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2113:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_4_2006.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_4_2006.pdf,,SC,2006,A defendant who fails to move the court to try an issue as a preliminary issue at the commencement of the trial is deemed to have waived objections in regard to lack of jurisdiction of the court to hear and determine the defendant's action.,The defendant has failed to move Court to try the said issue as a preliminary issue. The defendant has failed to formulate a preliminary issue relating to the jurisdiction of the Court at the commencement of the trial. His failure to move Court to try the said issue as a preliminary issue on such a vital matter will amount to a waiver of objections in regard to lack of jurisdiction of Court to hear and determine the defendant’s action.,SRC030-s147,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2115:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_56_2013-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_56_2013-1.pdf,,SC,2013,A mortgage bond can be admitted as evidence without requiring further proof if it was produced at the trial without objection.,"When considering the above provision of law in light of this case, as the said mortgage bond was produced at the trial without objection, it is my view that the Court shall admit the same in evidence without requiring further proof.",SRC030-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2120:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_69_2013-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_69_2013-1.pdf,,SC,2013,A document required by law to be attested must have at least one attesting witness called to prove its execution.,"According to that section, such document shall not be used as evidence until one attesting witness at least has been called for the purpose of proving its execution, if there is an attesting witness alive, and subject to the process of the court and capable of giving evidence.",SRC029-s68,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2123:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_72_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_72_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,Compound interest is not prohibited in Sri Lanka.,"On the basis of the above authorities and the reasoning therein, I am of the opinion that compound interest is not prohibited in Sri Lanka.",SRC030-s192,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2124:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_7_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_7_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,An action to recover a debt based on an account stated is not prescribed within three years.,An action to recover the balance was prescribed in three years.,SRC071-s7,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2127:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_9_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_9_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,"A judge has discretion to grant or deny legal interest in a case, and the decision to grant legal interest is not mandatory.","...although it is understood that legal interest may be provided and in most cases is provided from the date of the institution of the action, as per section 192(1) judges are not mandatorily obligated to grant such relief.",SRC030-s192,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2151:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_fr_311_2016-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_fr_311_2016-1.pdf,,SC,2016,A public officer cannot be transferred due to service exigencies at the whims and fancies of a superior.,"Moreover, a public officer cannot be transferred due to service exigencies at the whims and fancies of a superior.",SRC029-s114(f),none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2156:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_fr_350_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_fr_350_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,A police officer has the right to arrest an individual if they have reasonable grounds to believe that a criminal offence has been committed.,"If a Police officer has reasonable grounds to believe that a criminal offence had been committed by a person, he has a right under the Criminal Procedure Code to arrest the offender.",SRC030-s1,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2161:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_fr_39_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_fr_39_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,"The court will not intervene in every wrong decision or breach of the law, but only in cases where a fundamental right is infringed.","Every wrong decision or breach of the law does not attract the constitutional remedies relating to fundamental rights. Where a transgression of the law takes place, due solely to some corruption, negligence or error of judgment, a person cannot be allowed, to come under Article 126 and allege that there has been a violation of the constitutional guarantees.",SRC030-s1,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2175:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_hc_cala_127_128_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_hc_cala_127_128_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,A court has the power to vacate its own order if it is made per incuriam.,This support the proposition that the ‘Supreme Court has power to vacate its orders in appropriate circumstances if it is an order made by it per incuriam’.,SRC027-s81(9),none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2178:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_hc_cala_303_2019-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_hc_cala_303_2019-1.pdf,,SC,2019,"A party seeking leave to appeal against an order or judgment of the Provincial High Court of the Provinces must comply with the mandatory requirements of the Supreme Court Rules 1990, including Rules 2 and 6.",The nature of the requirements identified under Rules 2 and 6 were discussed in the case of D.S. Aron Senarath Vs. The Manager Moray Estate Maskeliya and another (Supra) by Priyasath Dep PC J (as he then was) as follows;,SRC030-s5A,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2179:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_hc_cala_306_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_hc_cala_306_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,"A substituted plaintiff can be appointed to continue an appeal after a change of status of a party, without deciding the rights of the parties.",In determining a proper person I would venture to hold that what the section envisages is a proper person necessary to prosecute the appeal.,SRC030-s760A,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2182:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_hcla_42_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_hcla_42_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,An order is a judgment or a final order under section 754(1) read with 754(5) of the Civil Procedure Code if it finally disposes of the rights of the parties.,Sharvananda J held that for an order to have the effect of a final judgment under section 754(5) of the Civil Procedure Code it must be an order finally disposing of the rights of the parties.,SRC030-s754,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2188:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_rule_3_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_rule_3_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,"A practicing attorney-at-law and notary public who is convicted of serious offenses, including forgery and fraud, and receives a light sentence, may be suspended from practicing their profession for a period of time.","It is unimaginable that the original records and files maintained by the High Court of Colombo and the Attorney General’s Department, which are the primary institutions involved in law enforcement, could be made to disappear so easily.",SRC014-s19(2),bad-source,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2189:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_rule_3_2023.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_rule_3_2023.pdf,,SC,2023,An Attorney-at-Law must avoid any situation where his professional obligation as an Attorney-at-Law could be compromised by his personal involvement as a witness.,An Attorney-at-Law shall not accept any professional matter in respect of which he knows or has reason to believe that he would be required as a witness.,SRC030-s12,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2192:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_rule_5_2023.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_rule_5_2023.pdf,,SC,2023,An attorney-at-law must exercise their skill with due diligence to the best of their ability and care in the interest of their client.,"by reason of the aforesaid acts and conduct, you have not exercised your skill with due diligence to the best of your ability and care in the interest of your client and have thus committed the breach of Rule 15 of the Supreme Court (Conduct of and Etiquette for Attorneys-at-Law) Rules 1988,",SRC014-s26(b),bad-source,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:26:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1959-21-1,commonlii-LKCA-1959-21,79 NLR 1,LKCA,1959,A defendant can be held liable for losses caused to a plaintiff.,"(1) That the first defendant was liable for the loss caused to the plaintiff. (2) (Vythialingam, J. dissenting) That though the first defendant's servants issued the delivery orders without production of the Bills of Lading to persons who, to their knowledge, were other than those entitled under the Bills of Lading to receive them, the 2nd defendant is not liable for the non-delivery of the goods as he was protected by the exemption clause in the Bills of Lading. The plaintiff has failed to show any fundamental breach of his contract of affreightment committed-by the 2nd defendant to disentitle him of the benefit of the exemption clause. (3) That a party to a contract may be precluded from relying upon an exemption clause where such parties have been guilty of a fundamental breach of contract or of a breach of a funda�mental term. A fundamental breach disentitling the party from relying on an exemption clause occurs if an act amount�ing to a complete departure from the contract is done which can be imputed to the contracting party himself as opposed to an act for which the contracting party is vicariously res�ponsible as having been committed by one of his servants",SRC030-s772,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:27:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1959-21-2,commonlii-LKCA-1959-21,79 NLR 1,LKCA,1959,A party cannot claim damages for non-delivery of goods if the delivery orders were issued to unauthorized persons.,"(1) That the first defendant was liable for the loss caused to the plaintiff. (2) (Vythialingam, J. dissenting) That though the first defendant's servants issued the delivery orders without production of the Bills of Lading to persons who, to their knowledge, were other than those entitled under the Bills of Lading to receive them, the 2nd defendant is not liable for the non-delivery of the goods as he was protected by the exemption clause in the Bills of Lading. The plaintiff has failed to show any fundamental breach of his contract of affreightment committed-by the 2nd defendant to disentitle him of the benefit of the exemption clause. (3) That a party to a contract may be precluded from relying upon an exemption clause where such parties have been guilty of a fundamental breach of contract or of a breach of a funda�mental term. A fundamental breach disentitling the party from relying on an exemption clause occurs if an act amount�ing to a complete departure from the contract is done which can be imputed to the contracting party himself as opposed to an act for which the contracting party is vicariously res�ponsible as having been committed by one of his servants",SRC030-s772,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:28:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1959-21-3,commonlii-LKCA-1959-21,79 NLR 1,LKCA,1959,A party to a contract may be precluded from relying on an exemption clause if they have committed a fundamental breach of contract.,"(1) That the first defendant was liable for the loss caused to the plaintiff. (2) (Vythialingam, J. dissenting) That though the first defendant's servants issued the delivery orders without production of the Bills of Lading to persons who, to their knowledge, were other than those entitled under the Bills of Lading to receive them, the 2nd defendant is not liable for the non-delivery of the goods as he was protected by the exemption clause in the Bills of Lading. The plaintiff has failed to show any fundamental breach of his contract of affreightment committed-by the 2nd defendant to disentitle him of the benefit of the exemption clause. (3) That a party to a contract may be precluded from relying upon an exemption clause where such parties have been guilty of a fundamental breach of contract or of a breach of a funda�mental term. A fundamental breach disentitling the party from relying on an exemption clause occurs if an act amount�ing to a complete departure from the contract is done which can be imputed to the contracting party himself as opposed to an act for which the contracting party is vicariously res�ponsible as having been committed by one of his servants",SRC030-s772,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:76:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1977-4-1,commonlii-LKCA-1977-4,80 NLR 256,LKCA,1977,A misdescription of land boundaries in a deed does not invalidate the deed.,"That as the evidence and the findings of the Trial Judge show that the assessment numbers given in the respective deeds are the correct assessment numbers of the allotment of land each party intended to buy and in fact purchased and was in possession of, the description of the land by the boundaries was a misdescription which although it would not invalidate the deeds, could be rejected or ignored. The maxim falsa demonstrate non nocet which is embodied in section 95 of the Evidence Ordinance applied. Per Pathirana, J. ""The Assessment numbers provide a definite and sufficient description of the land in the respective deeds of the plaintiff and the defendant of what was intendend to pass and the descriptions in those deeds by boundaries are erroneous"". A PPEAL from the judgment of the District Court of Kandy. H. W. Jayewardene with B, J. Fernando and Miss S. Senaratne for Defendant-Appellant. T. B. Dissanayake for Plaintiff-Respondent",SRC029-s95,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:111:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1984-24-1,commonlii-LKCA-1984-24,(1985) 2 Sri LR 35,LKCA,1984,A party to a suit cannot set up the title of a third party who is not their predecessor in title and who has not been joined in the action.,A party to a suit cannot under s. 3 of the Prescription Ordinance set up the title of a third party who is not his predecessor in title and who has not been joined in the action. The judgment in a case must be declaratory of the right of a party to the suit not of a stranger,SRC071-s3,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:126:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1985-22-1,commonlii-LKCA-1985-22,(1985) 2 Sri LR 1,LKCA,1985,A tenant is entitled to obtain a tenancy in their own name if the original tenant has abandoned the premises.,"(1) Mrs. Sinniahpillai had abandoned the premises and in the changed circumstances Nagaratnam was entitled to obtain the tenancy for himself. (2) A co-owner has the right to lease out his share and this gives the lessee the right to possess and his possession is therefore a bona fide possession. (3) The question of estoppel by acquiescence was, not raised in the District Court proceedings. and cannot be raised now. (4) Under s. 328 of the Civil Procedure Code all that had to be established was that the possession of the disputant was bona fide on his own account or on account of some person other than the judgment-debtor and that he was not a party to the action in which the decree was passed. (N.B. Section 328 referred to in this decision was as it stood before the passage of Law No. 20 of 1977.) 2",SRC030-s328,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:127:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1985-22-2,commonlii-LKCA-1985-22,(1985) 2 Sri LR 1,LKCA,1985,A co-owner has the right to lease out his share.,"(1) Mrs. Sinniahpillai had abandoned the premises and in the changed circumstances Nagaratnam was entitled to obtain the tenancy for himself. (2) A co-owner has the right to lease out his share and this gives the lessee the right to possess and his possession is therefore a bona fide possession. (3) The question of estoppel by acquiescence was, not raised in the District Court proceedings. and cannot be raised now. (4) Under s. 328 of the Civil Procedure Code all that had to be established was that the possession of the disputant was bona fide on his own account or on account of some person other than the judgment-debtor and that he was not a party to the action in which the decree was passed. (N.B. Section 328 referred to in this decision was as it stood before the passage of Law No. 20 of 1977.) 2",SRC030-s328,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
