scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1183:draftly-rule-courts-coa-RII-39-23-Final-Print.pdf-1,courts-coa-RII-39-23-Final-Print.pdf,,COA,,A person affected by void proceedings can apply to have them set aside ex debito justitiae in the exercise of the inherent jurisdiction of the court.,"The proceedings being void, the person affected by them can apply to have them set aside ex debito justitiae in the exercise of the inherent jurisdiction of the court which is saved by S. 839 of the Civil Procedure Code.",SRC030-s839,section-not-found,S. 839 of the Civil Procedure Code,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1190:draftly-rule-courts-coa-WRT-0542-25.pdf-1,courts-coa-WRT-0542-25.pdf,,COA,,An administrative authority cannot determine the validity of a marriage without a judicial determination.,"In the absence of a decree of nullity or other declaration by a competent Court, such marriage must be treated as subsisting and valid in law.",SRC030-s52,section-not-found,Section 52 of the Marriage Registration Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1193:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc-_-appeal-_-39-_-2022_amd2.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc-_-appeal-_-39-_-2022_amd2.pdf,,SC,2022,"A power of attorney must be strictly construed as giving only such authority as it expressly or by necessary implication creates, but this Court cannot decline to recognize the authority that the plaintiff himself has impliedly recognized if the agent has not been objected to.","a power of attorney must be strictly construed as giving only such authority as it expressly or by necessary implication creates, this Court cannot decline to recognize the authority that the 1st Plaintiff himself has impliedly recognized what Nadarajah Thamilalagan had to do whatever was necessary or ordinarily incidental to the effective execution of an express authority conferred on him by a written document that got onto the record long after the appeal had been preferred to the Civil Appellate High Court of Jaffna.",SRC030-s17,section-not-found,Section 17 of the Civil Procedure Code,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1196:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_101_2009.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_101_2009.pdf,,SC,2009,A cause of action for damages arising from the delict of setting the law in motion is prescribed within 2 years of the arrest.,"Did the Court of Appeal fall into substantial error by completely ignoring the provisions of Section 9 of the Prescription Ordinance? Moreover, in terms of section 9 of the Prescription Ordinance, action for damages should be filed within 2 years of the arrest.",SRC071-s9,ok-unchecked,Section 9 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1198:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_102_2011-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_102_2011-1.pdf,,SC,2011,A co-owner's possession is in law the possession of his co-owners.,A co-owner’s possession is in law the possession of his co-owners. It is not possible for him to put an end to that possession by any secret intention in his mind. Nothing short of ouster or something equivalent to ouster could bring about the result.,SRC071-s3,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1200:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_104_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_104_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,"If the court is satisfied that there are grounds to object to an application, court should frame issues as in Section 533 of the Code and proceed with the matter.",In the manner issues are framed District Court need to answer same and ensure Justice is done.,SRC030-s533,section-not-found,Section 533 of the Civil Procedure Code,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1202:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_105_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_105_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,"A party who claims prescriptive title must demonstrate possession over a specifically identified corpus; failure to do so renders such a claim untenable, as a prescription cannot operate over an uncertain or unidentified land.","A person who claims a prescriptive title must demonstrate possession over a specifically identified corpus; failure to do so renders such a claim untenable, as a prescription cannot operate over an uncertain or unidentified land.",SRC029-s58,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1203:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_106_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_106_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,A rightful owner of a property who was dispossessed by illegal means has the right to obtain an order from court for eviction and restoration.,A rightful owner of a property who was dispossessed by illegal means has the right to obtain an order from court for eviction and restoration.,SRC030-s839,section-not-found,section 839 of the Civil Procedure Code,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1207:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_108_2022-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_108_2022-1.pdf,,SC,2022,"A rei vindicatio action proper requires proof of title, but an action against an overholding lessee or licensee is based on privity of contract and issues as to title are irrelevant.",What has been discussed above indicate the deference between the re vidicatio action and an acton against an overholding lessee or licensee.,SRC029-s116,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1209:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_110_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_110_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,"A settlement must be notified to the court by motion made in the presence of, or on notice to, all the parties concerned, and the court shall pass a decree in accordance therewith, so far as it relates to the action.","such agreement, compromise, or satisfaction shall be notified to the court by motion made in presence of, or on notice to, all the parties concerned, and the court shall pass a decree in accordance therewith, so far as it relates to the action, and such decree shall be final, so far as relates to so much of the subject-matter of the action as is dealt with by the agreement, compromise, or satisfaction.",SRC030-s408,section-not-found,Section 408 of the Civil Procedure Code,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1210:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_110_2018.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_110_2018.pdf,,SC,2018,"If the transferor continued to remain in possession after the execution of the deed, or if the transferor paid the whole cost of the conveyance, or if the consideration expressed on the deed is utterly inadequate to what would be the fair, purchase money for the property conveyed, or if on the day of the conveyance, the transferee, by a non-notarial document, agreed to re-convey the property to the transferor on fulfillment of certain conditions stated in the said non-notarial document, or if the transferor did not intend to pass the beneficial interest in the property, the transferee holds the property on a constructive trust on behalf of the transferor.","In order to consider whether a transaction mentioned in a deed attested by a Notary Public is a genuine sale transaction or whether the transferee holds the property in question on a constructive trust on behalf of the transferor, the following matters would be relevant. 1. The transferor continued to remain in possession after the execution of the deed by which the transferee claims ownership of the property. 2. If the transferor paid the whole cost of the conveyance. 3. If the consideration expressed on the deed is utterly inadequate when compared to the actual value of the property at the time of the conveyance. 4. On the day of the conveyance, the transferee, by a non-notarial document, agreed to re-convey the property to the transferor on fulfillment of certain conditions stated in the said non-notarial document. 5. If the transferor did not intend to pass the beneficial interest in the property to the transferee.",SRC059-s2,ok-unchecked,section 83 of the Trusts Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1212:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_113_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_113_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,"The High Court cannot set aside an arbitral award on grounds not specified in the Arbitration Act, and must make a finding based on the application and material contained therein.",A finding cannot be made by the High Court in terms of sub paragraph (b) of section 32 (1) other than on the averments of the application and the material contained therein.,SRC030-s32,section-not-found,section 32 (1) of the Arbitration Act No. 11 of 1995,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1216:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_116_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_116_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,The plaintiff must identify the land in suit with precision before the execution of the decree.,"If the plaintiff fails to identify the land he claims dominium over, his action must fail.",SRC030-s323,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1220:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_119_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_119_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,A plaintiff cannot claim a bigger land than he is entitled to under and in terms of his chain of title.,"Therefore, the plaintiff was certainly not claiming a bigger land than he was entitled to under and in terms of his chain of title.",SRC030-s41,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1221:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_11_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_11_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,In a case where the party claiming a declaration of title must prove title to the corpus in dispute having identified the corpus in the first instance.,"It is trite law substantiated by a plethora of authorities, that in a case where the party claiming a declaration of title must prove title to the corpus in dispute having identified the corpus in the first instance.",SRC030-s432(2),none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1222:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_120_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_120_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,"A contract by a minor is only voidable and not void, thus, the Power of Attorney remained valid until it is set aside and that subsequent setting aside of the Power of Attorney, will not affect the property rights acquired by the Petitioner as innocent third parties through the Power of Attorney.","Even if an unassisted contract is void against the minor it can be ratified and make valid by the minor after becoming a major as happened in the case at hand. Once it is ratified and made valid and used for his benefit, he is estopped from stating that it is void ab initio.",SRC071-s10,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1229:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_124_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_124_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,"A co-owner has an interest in every part of the entire land, and can eject a trespasser.","Thus, when a trespasser enjoys the fruits of the property the co-owner's rights are affected and he becomes entitled to eject the trespasser.",SRC030-s150,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1230:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_124_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_124_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,"A court commissioner must comply with Section 32 of the Partition Law when preparing a scheme of partition, including stating where any lot is less than the minimum extent required by written law and providing a certificate that the plan of partition is in conformity with written law.","We observe that the Court Commissioner has failed to comply with Section 32 (1) (c), Section 32 (1) (d) and Section 32 (1) (f) of Partition Law when preparing the proposed Scheme of Partition as per P9 A and his report.",SRC027-s32,ok,Section 32 of the Partition Law,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1234:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_128_2012-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_128_2012-1.pdf,,SC,2012,"When partitioning is not possible, the action must not be dismissed, but rather the portion acquired by prescriptive possession must be excluded from the corpus.","Once the District Court decides that a party has acquired prescriptive title to an identifiable portion of the corpus, that portion needs to be excluded from the corpus.",SRC027-s26,ok,Section 26(2)(d) and (f) of the Partition Law,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1235:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_128_2019.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_128_2019.pdf,,SC,2019,A prosecution under the Food Act is time barred if it is instituted more than three months after the date of detection of the offence.,"It is contended that the intention of the Legislature by imposing a time bar as contained in Section 20 (1) (b) of the Food Act for instituting an action was to ensure inter alia credibility of the Complainant, as it is with the expectation that a Complainant will initiate an action by bringing the offence to the notice of the relevant Officers at the earliest point of discovery that the offence had taken place.",SRC071-s9,none,Section 20 (1) (b) of the Food Act,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1236:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_12_2006.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_12_2006.pdf,,SC,2006,The burden of proving that there had been a change in the condition of the land solely rests on the appellants.,Therefore the burden of proving that there had been a change in the condition of the land solely rests on the appellants.,SRC029-s101,section-not-found,Section 101 of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1237:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_12_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_12_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,The court will not declare a trust under Section 83 of the Trusts Ordinance if the attendant circumstances show that the transferor intended to dispose of the beneficial interest in the property.,Law therefore does not declare under such circumstances (Section 83 of the Trusts Ordinance) that the Appellant would hold such property for the benefit of the Respondents.,SRC059-s83,none,Section 83 of the Trusts Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1238:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_131_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_131_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,Mere use of a footpath for the period of prescription is insufficient to establish a servitude of right of way by prescription as per section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.,"If prescription is allowed to be established merely by walking through someone’s land, it would pave the way for countless claims to be levelled against owners of land. It would give rise to drastic consequences in village settings where people casually walk through the lands of each other as a practice. If all persons casually exercising physical use of footpaths across neighboring lands were to bring their claims stating that each such footpath constitutes a right of way by prescription simply by physical use, the doctrine of prescription would have far reaching and drastic consequences which were never intended by its proponents.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1240:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_132_2018.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_132_2018.pdf,,SC,2018,A possession unaccompanied by payment of rent or produce or performance of service or duty or by any other act by the possessor from which an acknowledgment of a right existing in another person would fairly and naturally be inferred is considered adverse possession.,"The words in section 3—“A possession unaccompanied by payment of rent or produce or performance of service or duty or by any other act by the possessor from which an acknowledgment of a right existing in another person would fairly and naturally be inferred”—have been held by this Court to contain not an illustration, but a definition of “adverse possession” (see Daniel v. Markar Ram. 1843-55, 2, Vand. Rep. 44, Carrim v. Dholl 2 CLR 12).",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1242:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_134_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_134_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,A party alleging that a deed is a forgery must prove the execution of the deed to establish its genuineness.,"If these deeds are excluded, the Plaintiff cannot establish his title and his action will necessarily fail.",SRC029-s114,section-not-found,section 114 of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1243:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_134_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_134_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,"A party to a partition action can transfer their entitlement to the share of the land which would be allocated to them at the end of the case, despite the provisions in the Partition Ordinance and Partition Law.","I am of the view that it is settled law for many decades that in spite of the provisions included in the Partition Ordinance firstly by Section 17 and thereafter in the Partition Law by Section 66, any party to a law suit of partitioning a co-owned land is able to gift, sell, or hypothecate his entitlement to the share of the land which would be allocated to him at the end of the case.",SRC027-s66,section-not-found,Section 66 of the Partition Law,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1254:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_142_2018.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_142_2018.pdf,,SC,2018,A substituted party in an action is estopped from taking a contrary or inconsistent position to that of the original party to the action.,"When the original party agreed to certain arrangements before the Court, the substituted party is estopped and bound by the said arrangements.",SRC030-s395,section-not-found,Section 395 of the Civil Procedure Code Act,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1256:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_144_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_144_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,"A defendant cannot prove prescriptive title against co-owners unless the contrary is proved by cogent evidence, and possession of one co-owner is in law the possession of the other co-owners.","Unless the contrary is proved by cogent evidence, possession of one co-owner is in law the possession of the other co-owners.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1260:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_148_2023.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_148_2023.pdf,,SC,2023,A final decree entered in a partition action cannot be challenged collaterally.,"Section 48 of the Partition Law underscores the finality and conclusiveness of partition decrees while providing limited circumstances under which they may be challenged (Fernando v. Marsal Appu (1922) 23 NLR 370, Mohamedaly Adamjee v. Hadad Sadeen (1956) 58 NLR 217, Madurapperuma v. Wijesundara [2019] 1 Sri LR 512).",SRC027-s48,ok,section 48 of the Partition Law,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1263:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_151_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_151_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,The burden of proof lies on the person who wishes the court to believe in the existence of a fact.,Hence the burden of proof lies on the Appellant to prove the existence of a forgery.,SRC029-s103,section-not-found,Section 103 of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1264:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_151_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_151_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,"A co-owner cannot acquire prescriptive title to a co-owned property by proving exclusive possession for ten years after changing the nature of the possession to one of adverse title to the others, if the possession is not hostile to the real owner and does not amount to a denial of the owner's title.",In order to acquire prescriptive title a co-owner should prove exclusive possession for ten years after changing the nature of the possession to one of adverse title to the others.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1271:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_155_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_155_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,The occupation of the premises must be of such character as is incompatible with the title of the owner.,The occupation of the premises must be of such character as is incompatible with the title of the owner.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1275:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_158_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_158_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,A Notary Public who attested a Deed cannot be regarded as an attesting witness for the purposes of Section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance if he does not know the executant of the Deed.,"Therefore, the fact that, the Notary Public did not state in his Attestation that he knew the executants, leads compellingly to the conclusion that, he did not know the executants.",SRC029-s68,section-not-found,Section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1276:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_158_2017.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_158_2017.pdf,,SC,2017,A party cannot acquire prescriptive title to an undivided portion of land.,when admittedly the 1st Plaintiff said that Reja Nona was in possession for well over 50 years and in the Plan No. 5788 marked 181 the eastern boundary is the defendants' land marked lot 2 in Plan marked 'X' which was proved by the Defendants by taking a commission and submitting the Plan No. 1054 where it is clearly stated that Lot 3 in that Plan is Lot 2 in Plan marked X' and also all the improvements and plantation were possessed by the Defendants?,SRC027-s16(3),none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1277:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_159_2019.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_159_2019.pdf,,SC,2019,"To claim prescriptive title, a party must establish a starting point for their acquisition of prescriptive rights and provide documentary evidence of their adverse possession.",Where a party invokes the provisions of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance in order to defeat the ownership of an adverse claimant to immovable property the burden of proof rests fairly and squarely on him to establish the starting point for his or her acquisition of prescriptive rights [Gratiean J in Chelliah v Wijenathan 54 N.L.R. 337 at 342].,SRC071-s3,none,Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1278:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_15_2008.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_15_2008.pdf,,SC,2008,"A statutory presumption in favour of the execution of a judicial or official act can be made if it is likely to have happened, considering the common course of natural events, human conduct, and public and private business.","This Court rules on a statutory presumption in favour of the execution, in terms of Section 114 (d) of the Evidence Ordinance. This Section reads as follows; 4 “The Court may presume the existence of any fact which it thinks likely to have happened, regard being had to the common course of natural events, human conduct, and public and private business in their relation to the facts of the particular case – that judicial and official acts have been regularly performed.”",SRC029-s114(d),none,Section 114 (d) of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1279:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_15_2009.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_15_2009.pdf,,SC,2009,A discretion under S-770 of the Civil Procedure Code is a very wide one and should be exercised cautiously.,On the other hand it is needless to stress that the discretion given under S - 770 is a very wide one and same has to be exercised cautiously which being a power expressly and plainly conferred on the Judge who hears the appeal.,SRC030-s770,section-not-found,Section 770 of the Civil Procedure Code,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1281:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_15_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_15_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,A co-owner can file an action against a trespasser or imminent trespasser to protect his rights and a Court can grant a lesser relief even where a bigger relief has been prayed for.,"In our law, a co-owner can file an action against a trespasser or imminent trespasser to protect his rights and a Court can grant a lesser relief even where a bigger relief has been prayed for.",SRC030-s754(1),none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1283:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_161_2019.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_161_2019.pdf,,SC,2019,A cause of action must accrue to a party before they can be made a plaintiff.,Hence no cause of action has accrued to the 3rd defendant against the 1st and 2nd defendants.,SRC030-s11,ok,section 11 of the Civil Procedure Code,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1289:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_166_2019.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_166_2019.pdf,,SC,2019,"A new point which was not raised in the issues or in the course of the trial cannot be raised for the 1st time in appeal, unless such point might have been raised at the trial under one of the issues raised, and the Court of Appeal has before it all the requisite material for deciding the point or the question is one of law and nothing more.","In the case of Setha Vs. Weerakoon 49 NLR 225, it was held, “A new point which was not raised in the issues or in the course of the trial cannot be raised for the 1st time in appeal, unless such point might have been raised at the trial under one of the issues raised, and the Court of Appeal has before it all the requisite material for deciding the point or the question is one of law and nothing more.”",SRC030-s545,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1291:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_168_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_168_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,The transferor is duty bound to adduce evidence to show that he did not intend to dispose of the beneficial interest of the property.,"Sec. 83 of the Trusts Ordinance specifically states that where the owner of the property transfers or bequeaths it, and it cannot reasonably be inferred consistently with the attendant circumstances that he intended to dispose of the beneficial interest therein, the transferee or legatee must hold such benefit for the owner or his legal representative.",SRC059-s83,ok-unchecked,Sec. 83 of the Trusts Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1294:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_171_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_171_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,"In a possessory action, the plaintiff need not prove lawful title to the subject matter of the action.",I therefore hold that the Court of Appeal has erred when it held that the Plaintiff in order to institute this action should prove that the Plaintiff herself had lawful title.,SRC071-s4,ok-unchecked,Section 4 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1297:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_172_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_172_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,"A written promise to pay a sum of money must be in writing, but no special form of writing is required, and the promise may be contained in one document or evidenced by more than one document or by an exchange of documents.","For the purpose of constituting a written promise, contract, bargain or agreement no special form of writing is required.",SRC071-s6,ok-unchecked,Section 6 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1302:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_174_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_174_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,"The plaintiff must include in the plaint all persons who, whether in actual possession or not, to his knowledge are entitled or claim to be entitled to any right, share or interest to, of, or in the land to which the action relates, whether vested or contingent, and whether by way of mortgage, lease, usufruct, servitude, trust, life interest, or otherwise, or to any improvements made or effected on or to the land.","For instance, section 5 of the Partition Law enacts that the plaintiff in a partition action shall include in his plaint as parties to the action all persons who, whether in actual possession or not, to his knowledge are entitled or claim to be entitled to any right, share or interest to, of, or in the land to which the action relates, whether vested or contingent, and whether by way of mortgage, lease, usufruct, servitude, trust, life interest, or otherwise, or to any improvements made or effected on or to the land.",SRC027-s5,ok,section 5 of the Partition Law,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1303:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_175_2025.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_175_2025.pdf,,SC,2025,A deed executed before a notary can be proved by the notary if they knew the executant personally and can testify that the signature on the deed is that of the executant.,"Where a deed executed before a notary is sought to be proved, the notary can be regarded as an attesting witness within the meaning of section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance if the notary knew the executant personally and can testify that the signature on the deed is that of the executant.",SRC029-s68,section-not-found,section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1304:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_176_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_176_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,"A person can possess a property without occupying it, and to prove possession, it is not necessary to prove that he or she lives in the property or occupies it.",One can possess a property without occupying the same. To prove possession it is not necessary to prove that he or she lives in the property or occupies the property.,SRC030-s328,ok,Section 328 of the Civil Procedure Code,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1317:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_18_2021.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_18_2021.pdf,,SC,2021,"A constructive trust arises where a person has transferred property subject to a notarial agreement to reconvey within a specified period, and they cannot enforce the agreement because the period has elapsed.","If this is so, a trust under section 83 will also arise where a person has transferred property subject to a notarial agreement to reconvey within a specified period, and he cannot enforce the agreement because the period has elapsed.",SRC059-s83,ok-unchecked,section 83 of the Trusts Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1318:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_191_192_2025-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_191_192_2025-1.pdf,,SC,2025,"A document marked subject to proof is admissible without formal proof unless its execution or genuineness is impeached in the pleadings and raised as an issue, or the court otherwise requires proof.","The law relating to the proof of documents has been laid down in the Civil Procedure Code (Amendment) Act, No. 17 of 2022. Section 2 of this Act introduces section 154A to be immediately inserted after section 154 of the principal enactment, which provides that when a deed or any document required by law to be attested is tendered in evidence, it shall be admissible without requiring formal proof, unless its execution or genuineness is impeached in the pleadings and raised as an issue, or the court otherwise requires proof.",SRC029-s154A,section-not-found,"Section 2 of the Civil Procedure Code (Amendment) Act, No. 17 of 2022",False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1319:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_191_2018.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_191_2018.pdf,,SC,2018,A bona fide purchaser's rights are unaffected by a constructive trust if the conditions of good faith and valuable consideration are established.,"However, upon reviewing the evidence, I am not satisfied that the conditions of good faith and valuable consideration have been established to the satisfaction of the Court.",SRC059-s98,ok-unchecked,section 98 of the Trusts Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1321:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_194_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_194_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,A person who possesses a land with a secret intention that he would be able to claim prescription at the end of ten years is not entitled to claim prescription under Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.,"If a person, who claims prescription in terms of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance, possesses the property with a secret intention his possession cannot be considered as an adverse possession and as such he is not entitled to succeed in a claim of prescription.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1322:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_194_2018.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_194_2018.pdf,,SC,2018,A wider and purposive construction must be preferred to a narrow and restrictive approach when determining an application for the addition of parties under section 18(1) of the Civil Procedure Code.,"In determining an application for the addition of parties under section 18(1) of the Civil Procedure Code, a wider and purposive construction must be preferred to a narrow and restrictive approach.",SRC030-s18,section-not-found,section 18 of the Civil Procedure Code,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1323:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_195_2012-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_195_2012-1.pdf,,SC,2012,"A transferee holds a property in trust for the transferor if the transferor did not intend to dispose of the beneficial interest, as inferred from attendant circumstances.","When the attendant circumstances show that the transferor did not intend to dispose the beneficial interest of the property to the transferee, then the law declares that the transferee would hold such property for the benefit of the transferor.",SRC059-s83,ok-unchecked,section 83 of the Trusts Ordinance,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1324:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_197_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_197_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,"A wife cannot claim prescriptive title to a property owned by her husband, as her possession of the property is not considered adverse possession.",Further when wife possesses a property of her husband she cannot claim prescriptive title under Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance against her husband because such a possession cannot be considered to be an adverse possession against her husband.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1327:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_19_2008.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_19_2008.pdf,,SC,2008,"The court may on or before the hearing, upon the application of either party, and on such terms as the court thinks just, order that any plaintiff be made a defendant, or that any defendant be made a plaintiff, and that the name of any person who ought to have been joined, whether as plaintiff or defendant, or whose presence before the court may be necessary in order to enable the court effectually and completely to adjudicate upon and settle all the questions involved in that action, be added.","The court may on or before the hearing, upon the application of either party, and on such terms as the court thinks just, order that............any plaintiff be made a defendant, or that any defendant be made a plaintiff, and that the name of any person who ought to have been joined, whether as plaintiff or defendant, or whose presence before the court may be necessary in order to enable the court effectually and completely to adjudicate upon and settle all the questions involved in that action, be added.",SRC030-s18,section-not-found,section 18 of the Civil Procedure Code,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1329:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_1_2025-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_1_2025-1.pdf,,SC,2025,"A party who did not file the list of witnesses and documents as required by section 79B can invoke section 142D to call additional witnesses and produce additional documents at the pre-trial conference, but only if the other party consents to it.",A party who did not file the list as required by section 79B cannot invoke this provision unless the other party consents to it at the pre-trial conference.,SRC030-s79B,ok,section 79B of the Civil Procedure Code,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1334:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_212_2017.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_212_2017.pdf,,SC,2017,A judgment of a court of competent jurisdiction operates as an estoppel under the principle of estoppel per rem judicatam.,"where a final judicial decision has been pronounced by either an English, (or with certain exemptions) a foreign judicial tribunal of competent jurisdiction over the parties to, and the subject matter of, the litigation, any party or privy to such litigation, as against any other party or privy thereto, and, in the case of a decision in rem, any person whatsoever, as against any other person, is estopped in any subsequent litigation from disputing or questioning such decision on the merits, whether it be used as the foundation of an action, or relied upon as a bar to a claim, indictment or complaint, or to any affirmative defence, case, or allegation, if, but not unless, the party interested raises the point of estoppel at the proper time and in the proper manner.",SRC030-s207,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1343:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_232_2017.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_232_2017.pdf,,SC,2017,A person who possesses an immovable property with leave and licence of the owner cannot claim prescriptive title against the owner or his heirs on the basis of the period he possessed the property.,"When a person starts possessing an immovable property with leave and licence of the owner, the presumption is that he continues to possess the immovable property on the permission originally granted and such a person or his agents or heirs cannot claim prescriptive title against the owner or his heirs on the basis of the period he possessed the property.",SRC029-s116,section-not-found,Section 116 of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1348:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_242_2016_amd1-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_242_2016_amd1-1.pdf,,SC,2016,A person who enters into occupation of property as a licensee cannot acquire prescriptive right to the property unless they produce an event or overt act that manifests their intention to occupy the property as the owner.,"A person, who is let into occupation of property as a tenant, or as a licensee, must be deemed to continue to occupy on the footing on which he was admitted, until by some overt act by which he manifests his intention of occupying it in another capacity.",SRC071-s13,ok-unchecked,Section 13 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1350:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_24_2022.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_24_2022.pdf,,SC,2022,"A person is said to have notice of a fact either when he actually knows that fact, or when, but for willful abstention from inquiry or gross negligence, he would have known it, or when information of the fact is given to or obtained by any person whom the court may determine to have been his agent for the purpose of receiving or obtaining such information","A person is said to have notice of a fact either when he actually knows that fact, or when, but for willful abstention from inquiry or gross negligence, he would have known it, or when information of the fact is given to or obtained by any person whom the court may determine to have been his agent for the purpose of receiving or obtaining such information",SRC059-s3,ok-unchecked,Section 3(j) of the Trust Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1353:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_27_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_27_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,"A decision of a District Judge confirming a final scheme of partition is not an order as envisaged by Section 36A of the Partition Law, if it finally disposes of the matter in dispute.","In other words, if the District Judge concluded the other way, granting that the alternative scheme of partition was correct instead of granting that the final scheme of partition was correct, then again the matter comes to a finality.",SRC027-s35,ok,Section 36A of the Partition Law as amended by Act No. 17 of 1997,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1356:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_28_2008.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_28_2008.pdf,,SC,2008,A transferee must hold property for the benefit of the owner or their legal representative if it cannot be reasonably inferred that the owner intended to dispose of the beneficial interest.,"Where the owner of property transfers or bequeaths it, and it cannot reasonably be inferred consistently with the attendant circumstances that he intended to dispose of the beneficial interest therein, the transferee or legatee must hold such property for the benefit of the owner or his legal representative.",SRC059-s83,ok-unchecked,section 83 of the Trusts Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1358:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_28_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_28_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,The possession of a property by a defendant is considered 'undisturbed and uninterrupted possession' if they have been in continuous possession for more than ten years.,"In the presence of the above evidence, it is not difficult to hold that the possession of this property by Defendant is ‘undisturbed and uninterrupted possession’ within the meaning of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1362:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_30_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_30_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,Time cannot run against remaindermen in terms of section 3 of the Ordinance.,"As explained above, time cannot run against remaindermen in terms of section 3 of the Ordinance.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1364:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_32_2021.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_32_2021.pdf,,SC,2021,A party cannot take up new positions which are questions of fact for the first time on appeal.,The defendants cannot take up new positions which are questions of fact for the first time on appeal.,SRC029-s114,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1367:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_33_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_33_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,A person cannot acquire prescriptive title without demonstrating an overt act showing an intention to possess adversely to the owner.,"Without any demonstration of any overt act against the rights of the owner, court cannot recognize Nandasekera as a person who has acted as one holding prescriptive possession against the owner who had given him leave and license to step in and live there, no matter how long he had enjoyed the property.",SRC029-s3,section-not-found,Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1373:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_36_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_36_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,An Interlocutory Decree entered in a partition action binds the whole world subject to the matters referred to in Section 48 of the Partition Law.,Such finality would operate subject to the matters referred to in Section 48 of the Partition Law particularly to the matters in sub section 4 thereof.,SRC027-s48,ok,Section 48 of the Partition Law,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1374:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_36_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_36_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,A deed required by law to be attested must be proved in terms of section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance if it is impeached or challenged through an issue raised.,"However, if the deed is impeached or challenged through an issue raised, it has to be proved as per the provisions of Evidence Ordinance.",SRC029-s68,section-not-found,section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1375:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_36_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_36_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,A court has no power to enter an order of abatement under Section 402 of the Civil Procedure Code where the failure to prosecute the action for twelve months after the last order was due to the death of the plaintiff within that period.,"In the case of Selamma Achie Vs Palavasam 41 NLR 186, the Supreme Court held that “ A court has no power to enter an order of abatement under Section 402 of the Civil Procedure Code where the failure to prosecute the action for twelve months after the last order was due to the death of the plaintiff within that period”.",SRC030-s402,section-not-found,Section 402 of the Civil Procedure Code,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1383:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_39_2024.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_39_2024.pdf,,SC,2024,A successful action for declaration of title interrupts prescription.,"A successful action for declaration of title interrupts prescription and therefore, when the appellant was successful in case 17819/L, by Judgment dated 12.06.2009, the plea of prescriptive title by the respondent fail, as during the pendency of the successful action, the respondents have failed to be in uninterrupted and undisturbed possession for 10 years.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1384:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_3_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_3_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,"A partition action may proceed even if there is a discrepancy in the extent of the land described in the schedule to the plaint and the preliminary plan, if the surveyor's report is satisfactory and the deeds through which rights are claimed are consistent with the plaint.",It shall not be used as a convenient method to summarily dispose of long-drawn-out and complicated partition actions without embarking on the arduous task of investigating the title of each party.,SRC027-s18(1),bad-source,Section 18(1)(a)(iii) of the Partition Law No. 21 of 1977,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1385:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_3_2020.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_3_2020.pdf,,SC,2020,"A court cannot partition or make an order relating to a right, title, or interest in a land that fell outside the corpus.","There is no provision in the Partition Act that the Court is obliged to make any of the orders set out in Section 26 (2), in respect of the land that is described in the plaint. Nor is there any provision in the Act providing for the declaration of title to a land solely owned by a person, which has been wrongly included in the corpus sought to be partitioned.",SRC027-s26,ok,Section 26 (2) of the Partition Act,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1386:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_40_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_40_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,"In a rei vindicatio action, the burden of proof is on the defendant to show that he is in lawful possession, once the plaintiff's legal title to the corpus is proved.","Where the legal title to the corpus is proved, the burden of proof is on the Respondent to show that he is in lawful possession [Theivandran v. Ramanathan Chettiar (1986) 2 Sri LR 219 at 222; Wijetunge v. Thangarajah (1999) 1 Sri LR 53 at 55; Candappa Nee Bastian v. Ponnambalampillai (1993) 1 Sri LR 184 at 187; Beebi Johara v. Warusavithana (1998) 3 Sri LR 227 at 228].",SRC030-s772,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1387:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_40_2018.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_40_2018.pdf,,SC,2018,A trial court has a bounden duty to examine and investigate the title in a partition action.,"the court shall examine the title of each party and shall hear and receive evidence in support thereof and shall try and determine all question of law and facts arising in that action in regard to the right, share or interest of each party to, of, or in the land to which the action relates....",SRC027-s1,ok,"Section 25(1) of the Partition Act, bearing No. 21 of 1977 as amended",True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1388:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_41_2008.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_41_2008.pdf,,SC,2008,"A trial judge must consider and answer the issues raised by the parties in a partition case, and must not fail to do so under the guise of not considering documents that were not tendered with the written submissions.","It is the very basic point in writing a judgment. It is so important that it is the accepted procedure that when issues are raised, the pleadings go to the background and the case is heard based on the points of contest meaning the issues raised by parties after putting down the admissions.",SRC030-s187,section-not-found,Section 187 of the Civil Procedure Code,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1390:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_42_2015_46_2015-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_42_2015_46_2015-1.pdf,,SC,2015,"The High Court should not summarily reject an application for enforcement of an arbitral award without giving an opportunity to tender duly certified copies, even if the copy of the award filed with the application is not a duly certified copy.",The claimant should have been given an opportunity to tender duly certified copies interpreting “accompany” in section 31 (2) purposively and widely (as in Sri Lanka General Workers’ Union v Samaranayake and Nagappa Chettiar v. Commissioner of Income Tax.),SRC030-s31(2),bad-source,section 31 (2) of the Arbitration Act,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1391:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_42_2018.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_42_2018.pdf,,SC,2018,A partition action cannot be maintained where no co-ownership exists.,A partition action lies only where common ownership subsists.,SRC027-s2,ok,Partition Law,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1393:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_43_2017.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_43_2017.pdf,,SC,2017,A licensee cannot challenge the title of the owner of the property in suit.,"A licencee as the Defendant who entered the property in dispute with the leave and licence of Plaintiff cannot in law challenge the title of the owner of the property in suit, in this case the Plaintiff party.",SRC029-s116,section-not-found,Section 116 of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1397:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_45_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_45_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,"A sale of immovable property cannot be set aside on the ground of irregularity unless the applicant proves to the satisfaction of the court that he has sustained substantial injury by reason of such irregularity, and unless the grounds of the irregularity shall have been notified to the court within thirty days of the receipt of the Fiscal's report.",no sale shall be set aside on the ground of irregularity.....unless the grounds of the irregularity shall have been notified to the Court within thirty days of the receipt of the Fiscal’s Report,SRC030-s282,section-not-found,Section 282 (2) of the Civil Procedure Code,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1402:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_49_2003-part_II.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_49_2003-part_II.pdf,,SC,2003,A trial Judge should assess the oral evidence and bring his mind to bear on the facts relevant to the dispute and give reasons for his decision of the dispute as required by Section 187 of the Code.,A trial Judge should assess the oral evidence and bring his mind to bear on the facts relevant to the dispute and give reasons for his decision of the dispute as required by Section 187 of the Code.,SRC030-s187,section-not-found,Section 187 of the Civil Procedure Code,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1406:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_4_2007.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_4_2007.pdf,,SC,2007,An acknowledgment of a debt made after the limitation period has run is valid and effective to start time running again.,"In view of my finding that the Prescription Ordinance merely bars the remedy with lapse of time and does not extinguish the debt, there is no rational justification to insist that an acknowledgment must be made before the expiry of the limitation period to be effective. I hold that an acknowledgment of the debt to be effective for the purposes of section 12 of the Prescription Ordinance need not be made before the expiry of the period of limitation.",SRC071-s12,ok-unchecked,section 12 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1407:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_4_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_4_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,The District Court cannot permit the Plaintiff to add a party after the prescriptive period.,The District Court could not permit the Plaintiff to add a party after the prescriptive period.,SRC071-s9,ok-unchecked,section 9 of the Prescription Ordinance No.22 of 1871,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1416:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_53_2019.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_53_2019.pdf,,SC,2019,A party invoking the provisions of section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance must establish a starting point for their acquisition of prescriptive rights with consistent and credible evidence.,"In order to defeat the ownership of an adverse claimant to immovable property, the burden of proof rests squarely and fairly on him to establish a starting point for his or her acquisition of prescriptive rights (Chelliah vs. Wijenathan 54 NLR 337, Solomon Dias vs. William Singho and others [2015] 1 SLR 277).",SRC071-s3,none,section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1419:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_54a_2008.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_54a_2008.pdf,,SC,2008,"A person cannot acquire prescriptive title to a property if they have not demonstrated an intention to possess the property adversely to and independent of the owner's title, and have not manifested that intention to the owner.","To again apply the words of Bertram CJ in in TILLEKERATNE vs. BASTIAN, the defendant has not demonstrated to the plaintiff‟s father that the defendant had “an intention on his [her] part to possess adversely” and had not demonstrated “a manifestation of that intention” to the plaintiff‟s father.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1420:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_55_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_55_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,A court must consider all prayers in a plaint when deciding on an ex parte judgment.,"The learned District Judge has not stated, in his judgment, that the Plaintiff also seeks a declaration to the effect that deed No 1014 dated 29.12.1994 attested by IM Wimalasena is a valid deed.",SRC029-s68,section-not-found,section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1428:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_58_2019.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_58_2019.pdf,,SC,2019,A person who possesses a land with secret intention cannot claim benefit of the law of the land.,A person who possesses a land with secret intention cannot claim that his possession is an adverse possession.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1433:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_59_2021.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_59_2021.pdf,,SC,2021,"A court must issue notice on respondents when a prima facie case of an illegality is established, warranting full investigation with the participation of all parties.","In order to have notice issued on the Respondents, the burden cast on the Appellant was to establish a prima facie sustainable case and for the Court to be satisfied that there is a prima facie case to be looked into.",SRC029-s5,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1434:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_5_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_5_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,A party claiming prescription must prove that there was undisturbed adverse possession for 10 years.,"Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance provides, “ Proof of the undisturbed and uninterrupted possession by a defendant in any action, or by those under whom he claims, of lands or immovable property, by a title adverse to or independent of that of the claimant or plaintiff in such action (that is to say, a possession unaccompanied by payment of rent or produce, or performance of service or duty, or by any other act by the possessor, from which an acknowledgment of a right existing in another person would fairly and naturally be inferred) for ten years previous to the bringing of such action, shall entitle the defendant to a decree in his favour with costs...”",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1435:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_5_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_5_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,A licensee of an owner of a property cannot acquire prescriptive title to the property against the owner of the property so long as he holds the status of a licensee.,I hold that licensee of an owner of a property cannot acquire prescriptive title to the property against the owner of the property so long as he holds the status of a licensee.,SRC029-s116,section-not-found,Section 116 of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1436:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_60_2004.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_60_2004.pdf,,SC,2004,The failure to challenge evidence by cross-examination may be a factor to be taken into account in accepting such evidence.,"However, in MMBL Teas (Pvt) Ltd. v. British Ceylon Produce Export (Pvt) Ltd. [S.C. Case No. SC/CHC/35/2008, S.C.M. 17.12.2021] Aluwihare, PC, J., (at page 17) was of the view that failure to challenge evidence by cross examination by itself may not be sufficient to hold that a particular fact had been proved within the meaning of Section 3 of the Evidence Ordinance. It might, however, be a factor to be taken into account in accepting such evidence.",SRC029-s2,ok,Section 3 of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1442:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_68_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_68_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,The burden of proving that a property is Thediathetam is on the party who claims it to be so.,I am of the view that the burden of proving that a property is Thediathetam is on the party who claims it to be so;,SRC029-s19,section-not-found,Section 106 of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1444:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_69_2019-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_69_2019-1.pdf,,SC,2019,A party claiming prescriptive title must demonstrate a clear separation of the land in question from the remainder of the property.,"There was no cogent evidence led to demonstrate a clear separation of Lot B, on the northern boundary, from the remainder of the land.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1449:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_70_2020.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_70_2020.pdf,,SC,2020,A constructive trust may be held to have been created if the Court can infer from the attendant circumstances that the owner did not intend to dispose of the beneficial interest in the property at the time the legal interest was transferred to the transferee.,"Where the Court can infer, as contemplated by section 83, from the “attendant circumstances”, namely the circumstances which precede or follow the transfer, that the owner did not intend to dispose of the beneficial interest in the property at the time the legal interest was transferred to the transferee, a constructive trust may be held to have been created.",SRC059-s83,ok-unchecked,section 83 of the Trusts Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1451:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_71_72_2023.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_71_72_2023.pdf,,SC,2023,"A party to a partition action who has been brought into substitute a deceased original defendant, who has failed and neglected to file a statement of claim or even a proxy despite notice/summons being served on such defendant, cannot be permitted to file fresh pleadings nearly 24 years after the institution of the action when the said action has reached the stage envisaged in section 184 of the Civil Procedure Code.",I answer that question in the negative.,SRC030-s184,section-not-found,section 184 of the Civil Procedure Code,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1452:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_73_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_73_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,A well-defined and identifiable course or track should have been adversely used by the owner of the dominant tenement for over ten years to acquire a right of way by prescription.,It is a prerequisite to the acquisition of a right of way by prescription that a well-defined and identifiable course or track should have been adversely used by the owner of the dominant tenement for over ten years,SRC071-s3,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1454:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_73_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_73_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,A co-owner may sue another co-owner of an adjoining land for the definition of boundaries without the participation of other co-owners.,"It is a well-established principle of law that a co-owner could sue a trespasser without the participation of other co-owners. (Hevawitrane v. Dangan Rubber Co., Ltd. 17 NLR 49; Arnolisa et al v. Dissan et al 4 NLR 163; Meera Lebbe Cassi Lebbe Marikkar v. Kalavilage Baba (1885) 7 S.C.C.) If a co-owner could sue a trespasser, in the same way a co-owner should be able to be sued without participation of other co-owners.",SRC029-s115,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1455:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_74_2017.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_74_2017.pdf,,SC,2017,A subsequent purchase of title by a party claiming prescription to the same land does not negate the prescriptive title acquired by such party to such land.,"any subsequent purchase of title by a party claiming prescription to the same land, does not negate the prescriptive title acquired by such party to such land.",SRC071-s3,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1456:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_75_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_75_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,A party cannot acquire prescriptive title to a portion of a road if the final decree in a partition action has already wiped out all such rights.,"Nor can they claim any prescriptive rights to that portion because the Final Decree P4 wiped out all such rights, if they had any.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1463:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_7_2020.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_7_2020.pdf,,SC,2020,"A party can acquire prescriptive title to land by possessing it as a separate land for several decades, independent of the title of the original owners.",There is no doubt that the 4th Defendant has prescribed to Lots D and E by a title adverse to or independent of that of the Plaintiff and the 1st to 3rd Defendants as required by section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1467:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_80_2022.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_80_2022.pdf,,SC,2022,"For the defendant to prove 'adverse' possession, there should have been an intention by him to claim title to the land against its owner.","There must be a corporeal occupation of land attended with a manifest intention to hold and continue it and when the intent plainly is to hold the land against the claim of all other persons, the possession is hostile or adverse to the rights of the true owner",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance No. 22 of 1871 (as amended),True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1470:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_82_2009.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_82_2009.pdf,,SC,2009,"A trust may be created for any lawful purpose, and the unlawful purposes that would forbid a trust being created are specifically referred to in section 4(1) of the Trusts Ordinance.","Section 4(1) of the Trusts Ordinance as stated earlier clearly refers to the fact that a trust may be created for any lawful purpose. The unlawful purposes, which would forbid a Trust being created, are specifically referred to in section 4(1).",SRC059-s4,ok-unchecked,section 4(1) of the Trusts Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1475:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_83_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_83_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,A possessory action can be filed if the plaintiff is disturbed but not ousted from the land in their occupation.,Disturbance of possession was protected by the mandament van maintenue. The applicant for such a relief had to give a concise account of his possession and of the disturbance caused by the other party.,SRC071-s4,ok-unchecked,Section 4 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1478:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_87_2017.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_87_2017.pdf,,SC,2017,"In a rei vindicatio action, the plaintiff must establish that he is the owner of the property.",In the case of D.A.Wanigaratne Vs Juwanis Appuhamy 65 NLR 167 wherein this court held that in action rei vindication the plaintiff must prove and establish his title.,SRC030-s146(2),none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1479:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_88_2018.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_88_2018.pdf,,SC,2018,A party cannot put forward a ground of appeal for the first time in appeal unless it might have been put forward in the Court below and the matter in question should be a pure question of law.,"Accordingly the Appellate Court could consider a point raised for the first time in appeal, if the following requirements are fulfilled. a. the question raised for the first time in appeal, is a pure question of law and is not a mixed question of law and fact; b. the question raised for the first time in appeal is an issue put forward in the Court below under one of the issues raised; and c. the Court which hears the appeal has before it all the material that is required to decide the question.",SRC030-s150,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1480:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_89_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_89_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,A co-owner can eject a trespasser from the co-owned property without joining the other co-owners as parties to the suit.,even a co-owner has every right to eject the trespassers without making other co-owners parties to the suit.,SRC030-s147,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1482:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_8_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_8_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,"When the Plaintiffs claiming as Trustees institute an action to safeguard or assert rights to the Trust property and the question at issue is whether the title to the Trust property is vested in the Plaintiffs or in the Defendants, the Plaintiffs are not entitled to maintain the action without first obtaining a vesting order under Section 112 of the Trusts Ordinance.","When the Plaintiffs claiming as Trustees institute an action to safeguard or assert rights to the Trust property and the question at issue is whether the title to the Trust property is vested in the Plaintiffs or in the Defendants, the Plaintiffs are not entitled to maintain the action without first obtaining a vesting order under Section 112 of the Trusts Ordinance.",SRC059-s102,ok-unchecked,Section 112 of the Trusts Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1485:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_8_2018.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_8_2018.pdf,,SC,2018,A plaintiff must show the grounds on which they seek exemption from the operation of the law of limitation.,"the rule in Section 44 is mandatory and therefore whenever a Plaintiff seeks exemption from the operation of the law of limitation, he must show the grounds on which he seeks such exemption – see Don Palis Appuhamy v. Perera2.",SRC030-s44,section-not-found,Section 44 of the CPC,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1488:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_92_2012-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_92_2012-1.pdf,,SC,2012,A co-owner should be allotted the portion which contains his improvements so far as is practicable.,"Whenever possible, co-owners should be allotted the portions containing their improvements. A co-owner is not entitled as of right to be allotted the portion containing his improvements.",SRC027-s33,section-not-found,section 33 of the Partition Law No.21 of 1977,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1491:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_94_2025.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_94_2025.pdf,,SC,2025,"To establish prescriptive acquisition of immovable property, a defendant must prove undisturbed and uninterrupted possession for more than 10 years.","Proof of the undisturbed and uninterrupted possession by a defendant in any action, or by those under whom he claims, of lands or immovable property, by a title adverse to or independent of that of the claimant or plaintiff in such action (that is to say, a possession unaccompanied by payment of rent or produce, or performance of service or duty, or by any other act by the possessor, from which an acknowledgment of a right existing in another person would fairly and naturally be inferred) for ten years previous to the bringing of such action, shall entitle the defendant to a decree in his favour with costs.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1493:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_95_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_95_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,A party is bound to take steps and proceed with all possible and reasonable expedition to prosecute an action without allowing it to accumulate on the case list.,"People may do what they like with their disputes as long as they do not invoke the assistance of the Courts of Law. But whenever that step has been taken they are bound to proceed with all possible and reasonable expedition, and it is the duty of their legal advisors and of the Courts themselves to seek that this is done.",SRC030-s402,section-not-found,Section 402 of the Civil Procedure Code,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1497:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_96_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_96_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,The progress of prescription is not arrested by the minority of the plaintiffs or the absence of their father beyond the seas.,"the mere fact that his succession passed to the plaintiffs on his death, and that they were minors at the time, cannot arrest the progress of prescription.",SRC071-s13,ok-unchecked,Section 13 of the Prescription Ordinance No. 2 of 1889,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1500:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_98_2007.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_98_2007.pdf,,SC,2007,"A court exercising revisionary jurisdiction can correct errors, including those that violate statutory provisions, even if the affected party has failed to appeal.",Disregarding a statutory provision alone would amount to have established exceptional circumstances that are necessary to invoke revisionary jurisdiction.,SRC027-s25,section-not-found,section 25(1) of the partition law,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1503:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_9_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_9_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,"A bona fide purchaser cannot be held not to own the property he bought on the ground that his predecessor was holding the property under a constructive trust, specially when the person on behalf of whom he was holding the property in trust, directs him to transfer the same to another.","A bona fide purchaser cannot be held not to own the property he bought on the ground that his predecessor was holding the property under a constructive trust, specially when the person on behalf of whom he was holding the property in trust, directs him to transfer the same to another.",SRC059-s83,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1505:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_13_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_13_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,Substitution in appellate proceedings does not confer upon the person substituted the status of a legal heir of the deceased party.,"It is therefore not necessary to substitute all heirs of the deceased as parties to the appeal. As I have already stated, substitution is solely for the limited purpose of prosecuting the appeal.",SRC030,ok,Civil Procedure Code No.2 of 1889,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1506:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_14_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_14_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,A notary's failure to comply with Section 31 of the Notaries Ordinance does not vitiate mortgage documents.,"Thus, we find this argument based on non-compliance with the rules set out in the Notaries Ordinance would not hold water.",SRC014-s31,ok,Section 31 of the Notaries Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1509:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_1_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_1_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,Claims arising out of goods sold and delivered become prescribed in 1 year.,"As observed by the learned Judge of the High Court, in terms of section 8 of the Prescription Ordinance, claims arising out of such transactions become prescribed in 1 year.",SRC071-s8,ok-unchecked,section 8 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1511:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_22_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_22_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,A contract for the sale of goods requires a sale and delivery of the goods within a year for the prescriptive period to apply.,"No action shall be maintainable for or in respect of any goods sold and delivered, or for any shop bill or book debt, or for work and labour done, or for the wages of artisans, laborers, or servants, unless the same shall be brought within one year after the debt shall have become due.",SRC071-s8,ok-unchecked,Section 8 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1513:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_23_2008.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_23_2008.pdf,,SC,2008,The prescription period for an action without a written agreement is three years from the date of the cause of action.,"In the case of an unwritten agreement, an action should be filed within three years from the breach of the said agreement (from the date of the cause of action), in terms of section 7 of the said Ordinance.",SRC071-s7,ok-unchecked,section 7 of the Prescription Ordinance No. 22 of 1871 as amended,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1514:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_24_2008.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_24_2008.pdf,,SC,2008,A surety who has undertaken liability as co-principal debtor has impliedly and tacitly renounced their right to plead the defence of beneficium ordinis seu excussionis.,"I am therefore of the view that by undertaking liability as co-principal debtor, the Defendants have impliedly as well as tacitly renounced their right to plead the defence of beneficium ordinis seu excussionis.",SRC029-s101,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1516:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_2_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_2_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,The burden of proof is on the judgment debtor to establish substantial losses if the writ is issued.,In the light of the foregoing case law this Court finds that the burden of proof is on the Petitioner (judgement – debtor) to establish substantial losses if the writ is issued.,SRC030-s763,ok,section 763 of the Civil Procedure Code,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1517:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_3_2000.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_3_2000.pdf,,SC,2000,The undertaking of the Secretary to the Treasury contained in clauses 9 and 10 of the Agreement binds the State.,"The undertaking of the Secretary to the Treasury contained in clauses 9 and 10 of the Agreement marked P7, binds the State.",SRC023-s3,ok,"Section 2(3) of the Conversion of Public Corporations Act, No. 23 of 1987",True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1518:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_3_2009.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_3_2009.pdf,,SC,2009,An action based on a written contract is not prescribed if brought within six years from the date of the breach of such contract.,"Section 6 of the Prescription Ordinance provides that; “No action shall be maintainable upon ... any written promise, contract, bargain, or agreement, ... unless such action shall be brought within six years from the date of the breach of such ... contract, bargain or agreement, ... from the date when such note or bill shall have become due, or of the last payment of interest thereon.”",SRC071-s6,ok-unchecked,Section 6 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1520:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_45_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_45_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,A demand for repayment of a loan repayable on demand will arise only at the time when a demand is made.,"It is settled law that the cause of action concerning a loan repayable on demand will arise only at the time when a demand is made [Seylan Bank Ltd v. Inter Trade Garments (Pvt.) Ltd. (2005) 1 Sri.L.R 80; Sivasubramaniam v. Alagamutui (1950) 53 N.L.R 150; See also, Bank of Ceylon v. Flex Port (Pvt.) Ltd., SC Appeal 120/2012, S.C.M. 03.07.2020 at pages 6-7].",SRC030-s40(d),none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1522:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_4_2006.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_4_2006.pdf,,SC,2006,A defendant who fails to move the court to try an issue as a preliminary issue at the commencement of the trial is deemed to have waived objections in regard to lack of jurisdiction of the court to hear and determine the defendant's action.,His failure to move Court to try the said issue as a preliminary issue on such a vital matter will amount to a waiver of objections in regard to lack of jurisdiction of Court to hear and determine the defendant’s action.,SRC030-s147,section-not-found,Section 147 of the Civil Procedure Code,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1526:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_7_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_7_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,"An action to recover a debt based on an account stated is not prescribed in three years, but in one year if it is based on goods sold and delivered.",An action to recover the balance was prescribed in three years.,SRC071-s7,ok-unchecked,Section 7 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1534:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_fr_311_2016-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_fr_311_2016-1.pdf,,SC,2016,A public officer's transfer must be based on a valid reason and the officer must be made aware of the reason.,"The PSC Rules recognise three different categories of exigencies of service, so merely stating that it was on exigencies of service is not sufficient.",SRC029-s114(f),none,Article 12 (1) of the Constitution,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1535:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_fr_350_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_fr_350_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,A police officer has the right to arrest a person on a complaint of a breach of agreement if they have reasonable grounds to believe that the violating party had entertained dishonest intention.,"If a Police officer has reasonable grounds to believe that a criminal offence had been committed by a person, he has a right under the Criminal Procedure Code to arrest the offender.",SRC030-s1,ok,Section 32 (1) of the Code of Criminal Procedure Act,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1540:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_rule_3_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_rule_3_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,"A practicing attorney-at-law and notary public who has been convicted of serious offenses, including forgery and fraud, and has received a light sentence, may be suspended from practicing his profession for a period of eight years.","It is unimaginable that the original records and files maintained by the High Court of Colombo and the Attorney General’s Department, which are the primary institutions involved in law enforcement, could be made to disappear so easily.",SRC014-s19(2),bad-source,"Section 42(2) of the Judicature Act No. 2 of 1978, Section 30(12) and Section 39 of the Notaries Ordinance No. 1 of 1907, as subsequently amended, Rule 60 and 61 of the Supreme Court (Conduct of and Etiquette for Attorney-at-Law) Rules 1988",False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
