scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:1091:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca-1130-99-10.01.19.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca-1130-99-10.01.19.pdf,,COA,,"An interlocutory order in a Partition Act case is not a judgment, but an order with a final expression of the decision, and thus requires leave to appeal.",It is to be noted that the question arises whether the Interlocutory Order of the learned Judge of the District Court of Ratnapura falls under Section 754(1) or 754(2) of the Civil Procedure Code.,SRC030-s754,section-not-found,"Section 754(1) of the Civil Procedure Code, Section 754(2) of the Civil Procedure Code, Section 754(5) of the Civil Procedure Code",False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:1094:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca-417-1998-f-18.1.2019.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca-417-1998-f-18.1.2019.pdf,,COA,2019,"A party cannot be inconsistent in their approach in legal proceedings, they cannot blow hot and cold, affirm and disaffirm the same transaction simultaneously to suit the occasion.","He cannot blow hot and cold, affirm and disaffirm the same transaction simultaneously to suit the occasion.",SRC030-s772,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:1095:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca-423-2000-f-22.1.2019.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca-423-2000-f-22.1.2019.pdf,,COA,2019,A person who claims prescriptive title against the rightful owner who has the paper title has a very heavy burden to prove all the requirements prescribed in section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.,A person who claims prescriptive title against the rightful owner who has the paper title has a very heavy burden to prove all the requirements prescribed in section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.,SRC071-s3,none,section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:1096:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca-472-97-16.01.19.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca-472-97-16.01.19.pdf,,COA,,"A party claiming prescriptive title must prove undisturbed and uninterrupted possession, independent or adverse to the claimant, for ten years prior to the action.","In order to initiate a prescriptive title, it is necessary to show a change in the nature of the possession and the party claiming prescriptive right should show an ouster.",SRC029-s3,section-not-found,Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:1100:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca-891-2000-f-21.1.2019.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca-891-2000-f-21.1.2019.pdf,,COA,2019,"A partition action can order that any portion of the land representing the share of any particular party only shall be demarcated and separated from the remainder of the land, and can also order that any share remain unallotted.","In terms of section 26(2)(d) of the Partition Law, No. 21 of 1977, as amended, the District Judge, after trial inter alia can “order that any portion of the land representing the share of any particular party only shall be demarcated and separated from the remainder of the land”, and in terms of section 26(2)(g) can “order that any share remain unallotted”.",SRC027-s26(2),bad-source,"section 26(2)(d) of the Partition Law, No. 21 of 1977, as amended",True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:1101:draftly-rule-courts-coa-CA-DC-1308-98.pdf-1,courts-coa-CA-DC-1308-98.pdf,,COA,,A party claiming the benefit of Section 83 of the Trust Ordinance must establish that they did not intend to dispose of the beneficial interest in the property transferred.,"Where the owner of property transfers or bequeaths it, and it cannot reasonably be inferred consistently with the attendant circumstances that he intended to dispose of the beneficial interest therein, the transferee or legatee must hold such property for the benefit of the owner or his legal representative",SRC059-s83,ok-unchecked,Section 83 of the Trust Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:1110:draftly-rule-courts-coa-CA-RII-0001-23.pdf-1,courts-coa-CA-RII-0001-23.pdf,,COA,,It is too late in the day to challenge the district court’s order on substitution both in law and facts.,Hence the application of the substituted 01st defendant petitioner is dismissed with costs.,SRC030-s394(2),bad-source,section 394(2) of the Civil Procedure Code,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:1129:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_1187_98.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_1187_98.pdf,,COA,,"A party can make submissions on the devolution of title of the parties to the action even at the appeal stage, and the court may order a re-trial if necessary.","Merely because her claim was to have an exclusion of a particular portion of the corpus, she is not prevented from taking up the matters pertaining to the devolution of title of the parties to the action even at this appeal stage.",SRC030-s25,ok,Section 25 of the Partition Act,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:1134:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_242_99.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_242_99.pdf,,COA,,"A party invoking the provisions of section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance to defeat the ownership of an adverse claimant to immovable property must establish a starting point for their acquisition of prescriptive rights, which must be undisturbed, uninterrupted, adverse to or independent of that of the former possessor and must have lasted for at least ten years.","According to section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance such a possession must be undisturbed, uninterrupted, adverse to or independent of that of the former possessor and should have lasted for at least ten years before he could transform such possession into prescriptive title.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:1136:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_476_1999.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_476_1999.pdf,,COA,1999,A person who has been dispossessed of immovable property otherwise than by process of law can institute proceedings against the person dispossessing him at any time within one year of such dispossession.,Section 4 of the Prescription Ordinance declares: ~,SRC071-s4,ok-unchecked,Section 4 of the Prescription Ordinance No.22 of 1871,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
