scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:1144:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_853_99.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_853_99.pdf,,COA,,A party who remains on the record without participating in the trial cannot be considered an aggrieved party.,"A party qua Appellants who missed the opportunity of availing himself of putting forward his case in the original court cannot be treated on par with a victim of an offence who is so desirous of taking advantage of Section 260 of the Code of Criminal Procedure Act, No. 15 of 1979.",SRC027-s25,section-not-found,"Section 25 of the Partition Law, No. 21 of 1977",False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:1145:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_955_1996.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_955_1996.pdf,,COA,1996,"A person who knows the value of the property is not entitled to rescission of the sale merely by reason of the fact that the price at which he has sold it, is less than half its true value.",a person who knows the value of his property is not entitled to recession of the sale merely by reason of the fact that the price at which he had sold the property is less than half its true value .....,SRC029-s101,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:1146:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_badulla_1162_99.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_badulla_1162_99.pdf,,COA,,"A person who enters into possession of a land in one capacity is presumed to continue in possession in the same capacity, and a co-owner's possession in law is tantamount to the possession of his other co-owners.",It is not possible for him to put an end to that possession by any secret intention in his mind unless there is proof of nothing short of ouster or something equivalent to ouster.,SRC071-s3,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:1148:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_balapitiya_830_98.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_balapitiya_830_98.pdf,,COA,,The burden of proof rests squarely and fairly on a party invoking the provisions of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance to defeat the ownership of an adverse claimant to immovable property to establish a starting point for their acquisition of prescriptive rights.,"Where a party invokes the provisions of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance in order to defeat the ownership of an adverse claimant to immovable property, the burden of proof rests squarely and fairly on him to establish a starting point for his or her acquisition of prescriptive rights.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:1150:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_colombo_438_1999.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_colombo_438_1999.pdf,,COA,1999,"In an action by a lessor against an overholding lessee for restoration and ejectment, privity of contract is the foundation of the right to relief, and issues as to title are irrelevant to the proceedings.",Privity of contract (whether it be by original agreement or by attornment) is the foundation of the right to relief and issues as to title are irrelevant to the proceedings.,SRC029-s58,section-not-found,section 58 of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:1151:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_colombo_780_98.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_colombo_780_98.pdf,,COA,,"A plaintiff can claim prescriptive title to land if they have been in uninterrupted possession of the land for ten years, without paying rent or performing any service or duty, and without acknowledging the rights of another person.",This position in law is supported by many authorities. Maasdorp's Institutes (7th Edition Vol2 at 96) Wigneswaran J in Leisa and another V Simon and Another [2002 (1) SLR 148] Pathirana V Jayasundera [58 NLR 169] Dona Cecilia V Cecilia Perera and others [1987 (1) SLR 235] Wijesundara and others v Constantine Dasa and another [1987 (2) SLR 66] I will now turn to consider whether the learned District Judge correctly evaluated the evidence and applied the law relevant thereto when he accepted the prescriptive rights .I 3 of the plaintiff rejecting the defence taken up by the defendant.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:1152:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_galle_123_99.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_galle_123_99.pdf,,COA,,A party invoking the provisions of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance to defeat ownership must establish a starting point for their acquisition of prescriptive rights.,"lt is trite law that where a party to an action for a declaration of title to a land invokes the Provisions of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance in order to defeat the ownership of an adverse claimant to immovable property, the burden of proof rests squarely and fairly on him to establish a starting point for his or her acquisition of prescriptive rights.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:1155:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_homagama_1113_98.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_homagama_1113_98.pdf,,COA,,"A prescriptive title can be claimed if the respondent has been in possession of the land for a period of well over ten years prior to the filing of this action, adverse to the rights of the plaintiff.","In the circumstances, it is abundantly clear that the respondent had been in possession of the land claimed by the plaintiff for a period of well over ten years prior to the filing of this action on 03.05.1991, adverse to the rights of the plaintiff which is the criteria applicable to claim prescriptive title in terms of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:1156:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_kalutara_534_95.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_kalutara_534_95.pdf,,COA,,A certificate of sale does not divest the judgment-debtor's title to immovable property until the sale is confirmed by the court and a Fiscal's conveyance is executed.,The right and title of the judgment-debtor or of any person holding under him or deriving title through him to immovable property sold by virtue of an execution is not divested by the sale until the confirmation of the sale by the court and the execution of the Fiscal's conveyance.,SRC030-s289,section-not-found,section 289 of the Civil Procedure Code,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:1157:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_kandy_1102_98.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_kandy_1102_98.pdf,,COA,,"There is no statutory bar to partition several lands held in common, but common ownership need to prevail in respect of all lands.",There is no statutory bar to partition several lands held in Common. The deciding factor seems to be that common ownership need to prevail in respect of all lands.,SRC027-s26(4),bad-source,section 26(4) of Partition Law No. 21 of 1977,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:1158:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_kegalle_684_98.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_kegalle_684_98.pdf,,COA,,The transferee must hold the property for the benefit of the owner or his legal representative if it cannot be reasonably inferred that the owner intended to dispose of the beneficial interest.,"Where the owner of property transfers or bequeaths it, and it cannot reasonably be inferred consistently with the attendant circumstances that he intended to dispose of the beneficial interest therein, the transferee or legatee must hold such property for the benefit of the owner or his legal representative",SRC059-s83,ok-unchecked,Section 83 of the Trust Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:1159:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_kuliyapitiya_293_00.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_kuliyapitiya_293_00.pdf,,COA,,A claim for recovery of property belonging to a temple is not barred by prescription under the Prescription Ordinance.,The Respondent's plea of prescription should necessarily fail.,SRC071-s34,ok-unchecked,section 34 of the Prescription Ordinance,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:1160:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_matale_827_2000.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_matale_827_2000.pdf,,COA,2000,"A notary is entitled to practice in a judicial zone, even if they reside in a different district, as long as the districts are within the same judicial zone.","Every warrant issued to a notary under the provisions of section 3 or 4, authorizing him to practice as a notary in any judicial division in which he resides, shall be deemed to authorize him to practise as a notary in the judicial zone in which he resides.",SRC014-s4A,ok,section 4A of the Notaries Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:1164:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_rathnapura_729_00.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_rathnapura_729_00.pdf,,COA,,A possessor is always presumed to hold in his own right and as proprietor until the contrary be demonstrated.,A possessor is always presumed to hold in his own right and as proprietor until the contrary be demonstrated;,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:1165:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_la_15_03.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_la_15_03.pdf,,COA,,"In effecting a partition, proper rights of way should be provided within the corpus to the district allotments as means of access to a public right of way.",It is trite Law that in effecting a partition proper rights of way should be provided within the corpus to the district allotments as means of access to a public right of way.,SRC030-s754(2),none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:1166:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_la_420_2003.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_la_420_2003.pdf,,COA,2003,A court may grant an extension of time to file objections under section 91A(1) and (2) of the Civil Procedure Code.,"Where a day is fixed or time appointed for doing any act or taking any proceeding by a party to the action, the court may, from time to time, upon the motions of such party and if sufficient cause is shown, fix another day or enlarge or abridge the time appointed, upon such terms, if any, as to it may seem proper.",SRC030-s91A,section-not-found,section 91A of the Civil Procedure Code,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:1175:draftly-rule-courts-coa-RII-0001-24-EDITEDpdf.pdf-1,courts-coa-RII-0001-24-EDITEDpdf.pdf,,COA,,"Before dismissing a partition action for non-prosecution, the court must compel the parties to bring the action to a termination.","In terms of the provisions of section 70 of the Partition Law, before dismissing a partition action for non-prosecution, the court has to compel the parties to bring the action to a termination.",SRC027-s70,ok,section 70 of the Partition Law No. 21 of 1997,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:1177:draftly-rule-courts-coa-RII-0021-24-Judgment-pdf.pdf-1,courts-coa-RII-0021-24-Judgment-pdf.pdf,,COA,,"A person who has divested themselves of all right, title, and interest in a property prior to the institution of a partition action is not a necessary party to the action.","The petitioner is neither a co-owner of the subject property nor a person presently possessing any. The petitioner has no right, share or interest in the corpus of the Partition Action. The material placed before Court indicates that the petitioner had divested himself of all right, title, and interest in the property prior to the institution of the action.",SRC027-s5,ok,Section 5 of the Partition Law No. 21 of 1977 (as amended),True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:1183:draftly-rule-courts-coa-RII-39-23-Final-Print.pdf-1,courts-coa-RII-39-23-Final-Print.pdf,,COA,,A person affected by void proceedings can apply to have them set aside ex debito justitiae in the exercise of the inherent jurisdiction of the court.,"The proceedings being void, the person affected by them can apply to have them set aside ex debito justitiae in the exercise of the inherent jurisdiction of the court which is saved by S. 839 of the Civil Procedure Code.",SRC030-s839,section-not-found,S. 839 of the Civil Procedure Code,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:1190:draftly-rule-courts-coa-WRT-0542-25.pdf-1,courts-coa-WRT-0542-25.pdf,,COA,,An administrative authority cannot determine the validity of a marriage without a judicial determination.,"In the absence of a decree of nullity or other declaration by a competent Court, such marriage must be treated as subsisting and valid in law.",SRC030-s52,section-not-found,Section 52 of the Marriage Registration Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:1193:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc-_-appeal-_-39-_-2022_amd2.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc-_-appeal-_-39-_-2022_amd2.pdf,,SC,2022,"A power of attorney must be strictly construed as giving only such authority as it expressly or by necessary implication creates, but this Court cannot decline to recognize the authority that the plaintiff himself has impliedly recognized if the agent has not been objected to.","a power of attorney must be strictly construed as giving only such authority as it expressly or by necessary implication creates, this Court cannot decline to recognize the authority that the 1st Plaintiff himself has impliedly recognized what Nadarajah Thamilalagan had to do whatever was necessary or ordinarily incidental to the effective execution of an express authority conferred on him by a written document that got onto the record long after the appeal had been preferred to the Civil Appellate High Court of Jaffna.",SRC030-s17,section-not-found,Section 17 of the Civil Procedure Code,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:1196:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_101_2009.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_101_2009.pdf,,SC,2009,A cause of action for damages arising from the delict of setting the law in motion is prescribed within 2 years of the arrest.,"Did the Court of Appeal fall into substantial error by completely ignoring the provisions of Section 9 of the Prescription Ordinance? Moreover, in terms of section 9 of the Prescription Ordinance, action for damages should be filed within 2 years of the arrest.",SRC071-s9,ok-unchecked,Section 9 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:1198:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_102_2011-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_102_2011-1.pdf,,SC,2011,A co-owner's possession is in law the possession of his co-owners.,A co-owner’s possession is in law the possession of his co-owners. It is not possible for him to put an end to that possession by any secret intention in his mind. Nothing short of ouster or something equivalent to ouster could bring about the result.,SRC071-s3,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:1200:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_104_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_104_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,"If the court is satisfied that there are grounds to object to an application, court should frame issues as in Section 533 of the Code and proceed with the matter.",In the manner issues are framed District Court need to answer same and ensure Justice is done.,SRC030-s533,section-not-found,Section 533 of the Civil Procedure Code,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:1202:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_105_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_105_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,"A party who claims prescriptive title must demonstrate possession over a specifically identified corpus; failure to do so renders such a claim untenable, as a prescription cannot operate over an uncertain or unidentified land.","A person who claims a prescriptive title must demonstrate possession over a specifically identified corpus; failure to do so renders such a claim untenable, as a prescription cannot operate over an uncertain or unidentified land.",SRC029-s58,none,,Fa
